BBC News,Western Europe,en,"Violent clashes, looting and buildings ablaze in France as over 1,000 arrested after protests","Violent clashes, looting and buildings ablaze in France as over 1,000 arrested after protests","Violent clashes, looting and buildings ablaze in France as over 1,000 arrested after protests Disorder broke out in cities across the country on a day in which more than half a million joined cost of living protests. Violence broke out across France on Saturday during the latest day of demonstrations against hardship in schools and the cost of living. At least 1,200 people were arrested during what the French government described as an ""escalation in violence"" alongside an estimated 550,000 taking to the streets. In Paris, there were significant clashes into the evening with riot police, who used tear gas and water cannon on crowds. Buildings were set alight in the capital and other areas, including in Limoges where a large fire in the town hall caused the roof to partly collapse. Looting broke out in several cities, with widespread vandalism targeting city halls and businesses also reported. Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu said 150 police officers were injured in the unrest. In Paris, the main march appeared to be largely peaceful during the afternoon. A group of young people told the BBC they were ""pacifists"" who are ""just trying to make things change, but nothing is moving"". However, as the day wore on some groups lit fires in the street and torched vehicles, including in iconic locations in the city centre like the Place de la République. Shops were ransacked, at least one restaurant was gutted by fire, and the BBC saw fighting breaking out among some of the groups. Riot police were seen charging at one crowd in central Paris and hitting several people with batons. In Lyon, Mayor Grégory Doucet said the burning of the city's tourist office was ""a tragedy"" for the city, telling BFMTV ","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 20:52:17 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6d937wedj7jo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:50
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Trump waives several Russia sanctions as row with Zelensky intensifies,Trump waives several Russia sanctions as row with Zelensky intensifies,"Trump waives several Russia sanctions as row with Zelensky intensifies The US president temporarily ends many restrictions on Moscow recently passed by Congress over ""national security"" concerns. US President Donald Trump blocked numerous sanctions on Russia two days before they were due to take effect, as a row with his Ukrainian counterpart Volodymyr Zelensky deepens. The sanctions were passed by the US Congress and signed into law by the president in September. But Trump issued an executive order Friday night saying that suspending some was in the US national interest, prompting a backlash from US lawmakers. Zelensky said it was unfortunate the US has decided to ""completely disregard"" the sanctions. US Secretary of State Marco Rubio said on Saturday that the suspension would consist of a 60-day pause intended to analyse the impact on the global economy. The new US sanctions law took aim at Russia's economy by giving Trump broad powers to levy tariffs of up to 100% on imports from the top five purchasers of Russian oil and gas – most significantly China and India. It also included sanctions against senior Russian figures, including Putin, as well as Russian financial institutions and Moscow's so-called ""shadow fleet"" of vessels used to circumvent existing embargoes. Rubio said Trump wanted to use the 60 days to analyse ""what imposing both sanctions and tariffs will mean to the global economy and even to the US economy"". Relations between Washington and Kyiv frayed afterthe US struck a controversial deal to import Russian diesellast week. Ukraine has accused the Trump administration of withdrawing key military support. Since then, Ukraine's president declared it was ","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 01:10:25 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cxr5yvzv7rp0o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:50
BBC News,Western Europe,en,European diplomats attacked by Israeli settlers in West Bank,European diplomats attacked by Israeli settlers in West Bank,"European diplomats attacked by Israeli settlers in West Bank The Israeli military condemns the violence but the prime minister's office says it is part of a ""smear"" campaign. A delegation of European diplomats has been attacked by masked Israeli settlers in the occupied West Bank. Belgium condemned the assault on its consul general and the other envoys and called for Israel to bring those responsible to justice. The Israel Defense Forces (IDF) also condemned what they called ""the severe act of violence"" and said they were investigating. But the office of the Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu cast doubt on what happened and suggested it was part of a ""coordinated campaign to smear Israel"". The attack came only a day after a number of Palestinians and Heidi Levine, a Washington Post photographer, were injured in a separate assault by settlers in another village in the West Bank. US Secretary of State Marco Rubio said the attack on Levine was ""disturbing"" and ""not a good look"". It was also condemned by the US Ambassador to Israel, Mike Huckabee, who called it a ""despicable act of terror carried out by masked cowards"". A staunch ally of Israel, Huckabee said the assailants must face ""prosecution to the fullest extent of the law"". In Saturday's incident the European diplomats - from Belgium, France, Poland and the Catholic Church - had come to the largely Christian village of Taybeh near Ramallah. They were there to assess the situation on the ground after almost near daily attacks by Israeli settlers on Palestinians trying to bring in their olive harvest. Video footage from the scene showed masked settlers confronting the convoy. Reports suggest stone","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 23:52:38 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c962ydpmjm3zo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:50
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Anti-government protesters battle with police in Albania,Anti-government protesters battle with police in Albania,"Anti-government protesters battle with police in Albania There were violent clashes between demonstrators and riot police throughout Saturday in the capital Tirana. Demonstrators and riot police fought pitched battles in the centre of Tirana on Saturday for the fifth day in a row. Masked protesters, many of them high school and university students, hurled rocks and petrol bombs at riot police trying to defend the education ministry, one of the focal points of the protests. The police responded with water cannon and volleys of tear gas, sending the crowds scattering. There are also reports that rubber bullets were used for the first time. Protests which began in the summerhave grown into a wider anti-government movement, with calls for the prime minister to resign following corruption allegations linked to his administration. Many of the protesters in central Tirana wore gas masks or face masks, while volunteers handed out syringes of saline eyewash to reduce the pain from the tear gas which billows down the street. The constant boom of petrol bombs could be heard exploding on Saturday. During the afternoon, the focus of the protests shifted to the office of Prime Minister Edi Rama on the city's main boulevard. Here the crowds swelled to far larger numbers than on previous days. Riot police faced a steady hail of cobble stones and fireworks, and responded with barrage after barrage of tear gas, some of the canisters landing far back in the crowds, causing panic. Incensed by the gas, the crowds waited for it to clear, then renewed their attacks. Black smoke from a burning vehicle and the sirens of police cars and ambulances split the after","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 20:53:00 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/crwydey50xeno?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:50
BBC News,Western Europe,en,At least 17 dead in crush at Hindu festival event in India,At least 17 dead in crush at Hindu festival event in India,"At least 17 dead in crush at Hindu festival event in India Many more have been injured at an event in the Indian city of Kolkata to mark a Hindu festival. At least 17 people have been killed and many more injured in a crush at an event in the Indian city of Kolkata to mark the Hindu festival of Durga Puja, officials say. The incident occurred during sudden rainfall, which witnesses say prompted a surge of people to try to seek shelter. West Bengal's Chief Minister Suvendhu Adhikari said a seven-year-old girl was among the dead, and that 25 people were being treated for injuries. Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi described it as a ""tragic"" incident and said the regional government was providing help to affected families. Six of the dead have been identified, Adhikari said. A senior police official told the AFP news agency that an announcement caused ""commotion"" after it started raining and the crowd started ""pushing, pulling and rushing forward and fell on the ground, leading to the stampede."". Deadly crowd incidents, including during religious festivals, are not uncommon in India due to poor management and lack of adequate safety precautions. Two years ago,121 people were killed in Uttar Pradeshduring a Hindu religious gathering.","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 19:19:16 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c820vwq7qg2ko?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:50
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Photos show luxury boat with 50m pool and water slides near Kim Jong Un's summer retreat,Photos show luxury boat with 50m pool and water slides near Kim Jong Un's summer retreat,"Photos show luxury boat with 50m pool and water slides near Kim Jong Un's summer retreat Satellite images give clues about how Kim spends his holidays - something far out of reach for most North Koreans. An 80m (263ft) luxury vessel has been repeatedly spotted in satellite images off North Korea's Wonsan coast from July to mid-October this year. What stands out is the 50m swimming pool along the deck, with a cluster of waterslides at one end. After sitting idle for months at a maintenance facility, it relocated in early July to waters off a strip of coastline known as the Kalma Peninsula. This is close to Wonsan, a seaside resort with a sprawling villa used by North Korean leader Kim Jong Un and his family. It was not the only large vessel on the move. A handful of others were also repeatedly seen near Kim's villa, in satellite images analysed by the BBC. The vessels are widely believed to be used by Kim and his family. Former North Korean officials, defectors and experts say it would be difficult for anyone unconnected to the regime's leadership to own or freely use vessels of this size. ""In winter, they would keep them at the shipyard. When summer came, they would bring them out and move them to Kim Jong Un's villa or towards Kalma, and use them for swimming and recreation,"" says Ri Jong Ho. Ri was once an official at the Workers' Party's Office 39, which is believed to be involved in raising and managing foreign currency for the leadership. The wealth of those who sit at the top of the secretive North Korean dictatorship is just as hard to pin down as the country's economic figures. The United Nations and others rely on estimates to classify North Korea as one of the world","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 22:09:50 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmy4xnyyg1lyo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:50
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Teacher fought US school attacker before armed dad shot suspect 'multiple times',Teacher fought US school attacker before armed dad shot suspect 'multiple times',"Teacher fought US school attacker before armed dad shot suspect 'multiple times' The 16-year-old suspect previously attended the primary school and had targeted a special educational needs classroom, police say. Some readers may find this article distressing. A female teacher in the US state of Oregon tackled a teenage shooter who entered her classroom and tried to wrestle the gun from his grasp, police have said. The teacher, who has not been named, threw her mobile phone before trying to disarm the 16-year-old on the floor of a classroom at Silver Rail Elementary in the city of Bend. The attacker killed three children and a teacher after shooting his way into the building on Thursday. He was shot dead by a father with two daughters at the school, who was driving past as the attack started to unfold and, armed with a pistol, ran in to confront the suspect. Police said the attacker used to be a student at the school. His motive remains unclear. He had been armed with about 44 more rounds of shotgun ammunition when he was stopped, police said. He fired as many as 15 rounds in the class. ""To the people who showed courage in the face of such violence and intervened to prevent this tragedy from being any worse, we owe you a debt of gratitude,"" Bend Police Chief Mike Krantz said. Police used a news conference on Friday to identify two of the four victims of the school shooting. They were five-year-old Rhoam Summer and 25-year-old teacher Meghan Lambert. Lambert's family said in a statement shared by the police that her goal was to be a teacher in the district in which she grew up and that she was ""compassionate and had a huge heart"". ""She always fought for the underdog and those with spec","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 09:58:17 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cv98zw1k8kp3o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:51
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Three men jailed for 158 years each for murdering Australian and US surfers in Mexico,Three men jailed for 158 years each for murdering Australian and US surfers in Mexico,"Three men jailed for 158 years each for murdering Australian and US surfers in Mexico Brothers Jake and Callum Robinson and their friend Carter Rhoad were killed on a surfing trip in an apparent botched robbery. A Mexican judge has sentenced three men convicted of murdering Australian brothers Jake and Callum Robinson and their US friend Carter Rhoad to 158 years and nine months in jail each. The Robinsons and Rhoad were killed in an apparent botched robbery of their truck on a secluded beach in Baja California in 2024 while they were on a surfing trip, the court heard during the trial. The murders sent shockwaves around the world, particularly in Australia, as the area is usually safe from the violence seen in other parts of Mexico. The guilty verdict was handed down last week. Debra Robinson, the brothers' mother, told the courtroom that her sons' murders had taken ""the future we had imagined with them"". The convicted killers' full names were not given for legal reasons, meaning they were only identified as Jesús Gerardo, Irineo Francisco and Ángel Jesús. In November last year, the girlfriend of the alleged ringleader, Jesús Gerardo (alias ""El Kekas""), wassentenced in a separate trialto 20 years in prison. She had admitted to involvement in the robbery. The court heard that the bodies of the victims were found dumped down a well with gunshot wounds to their heads. At the time, the murders shocked locals and tourists in the beach town of Ensenada who were disturbed at the apparent ease and sheer pointlessness of the killings. Ensenada has a reputation as a welcoming spot for surfers, but the chilling murders revealed how commonplace such extreme violence remains in Mexico. The only ","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 03:11:10 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c3kgqjkzw29wo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:51
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Nursery owner in China jailed for life after hundreds of children poisoned,Nursery owner in China jailed for life after hundreds of children poisoned,"Nursery owner in China jailed for life after hundreds of children poisoned She and five staff members were found to have used the inedible paint in meals to make them look more appealing. A nursery owner and five members of her staff have been jailed after they were found to have poisoned more than 200 children with lead-tainted food. Li Huifang, who operated the Heshi Peixin Kindergarten in Tianshui, in the Gansu province, was sentenced to life in prison for producing and selling toxic and harmful food, according to Chinese state media. In July 2025, 247 pupils and staff at the nursery were found to haveabnormally high blood lead levelsafter children began falling ill. A report into the incident found that chefs at Heshi Peixin had used inedible paint to ""enhance the look"" of its meals to attract more students - despite the paint being being clearly marked as such. In addition to the sentence, the Lanzhou Intermediate People's Court also ordered the confiscation of 500,000 yuan (£56,200; $74,700) of Li's personal assets, state broadcaster CCTV reported. Nursery executive principal Zhi Jinglin was sentenced to 15 years in prison and fined 300,00 yuan. Four other members of staff who worked in the nursery's kitchen received sentences of between six to eight years, according to state media. The court found the four had been instructed by Li and Zhi to add toxic and harmful ingredients to food served to children and staff. Food samples were later found to contain lead 2,000 times in excess of China's national safety limit. The investigation into the poisoning began after 235 children from Heshi Peixin nursery had to be treated for lead poisoning after eating stea","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 12:11:58 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqe9lx54g7x9o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:51
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Brazil sees record fall in Amazon deforestation as key election looms,Brazil sees record fall in Amazon deforestation as key election looms,"Brazil sees record fall in Amazon deforestation as key election looms The Amazon rainforest is a crucial buffer against climate change - but deforestation had expanded under former president Jair Bolsonaro. Brazil's government says deforestation rates in the country's Amazon and savanna regions have fallen to record lows ahead of a presidential election run-off in which the rainforest's future is a key issue. Left-wing President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva, when he assumed office three years ago, pledged to end deforestation by 2030 in the Amazon. It had surged under his right-wing predecessor, Jair Bolsonaro. Lula will face Bolsonaro's son Flavio in the run-off on 25 October, with polls suggesting a neck-and-neck race. Flavio is expected to continue his father's policies if he wins, though he has pledged to fight organised crime in the Amazon. Satellite data shows that in the year to July 2026, the deforestation rate fell by 41.8% in the Amazon - the biggest drop since records began in 1988. The fall in the Cerrado, Brazil's savanna region, was 23.8%. The Amazon rainforest - the world's largest - is a crucial buffer in the global fight against climate change and 60% of it is located in Brazil. Why the Amazon rainforest is so important What in the World podcast Jair Bolsonaro promoted mining in indigenous lands in the Amazon and forest clearances soared at the same time as resources to protect the forest were cut. During Bolsonaro's presidency from 2019 to 2022, the Amazon lost nearly 60% more in forest lands compared to the previous four years, news agency AFP reported, citing official data. Lula reversed his policies and oversaw deforestation ratesdrop to a six-year lowduring hi","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 02:33:09 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqe8rzg6zl00o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:51
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Rare pink diamond goes on display in France 100 years after amateur heist,Rare pink diamond goes on display in France 100 years after amateur heist,"Rare pink diamond goes on display in France 100 years after amateur heist The French royal jewel will be on public display for the first time since it was found hidden in an apple after a 1926 theft. A rare pink diamond is going on display in France for the first time since it was stolen almost 100 years ago to the day - the last time it was shown to the public. The nine-carat, pear-shaped gemstone known as the ""Grand Condé"" was pilfered in an overnight raid on the Château de Chantilly in October 1926 and concealed in a hollowed-out apple. The diamond - one of few royal jewels to survive the French Revolution - was recovered a few weeks later when a hungry maid at the hotel in which the thieves were staying took a bite of the apple. It will once again be on display in the Condé Museum at the Château de Chantilly until 3 January. The museum's director Mathieu Deldicque said that while the apple story may sounds far-fetched, it was widely covered by newspapers at the time. Deldicque has faced questions about whether the exhibition may be tempting fate, especially given the string of heists on European cultural institutions in the past 12 months, including the daring daylight robbery of jewellery from the Louvre museum in Paris. While he declined to reveal to CNN how much the diamond was insured for, he noted that extensive academic study into the properties of the diamond had taken place to make it uniquely identifiable. The results of this analysis, as well as previously unseen archive material about the gem's history and theft, form part of the exhibition, according to the museum. Naturally occurring pink diamonds are exceptionally rare and often sell for tens of millions","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:46:41 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c61m4zkx47vlo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:51
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Pope discharged from hospital days after lung cancer surgery,Pope discharged from hospital days after lung cancer surgery,"Pope discharged from hospital days after lung cancer surgery The pontiff will give the traditional Angelus prayer and blessing in St Peter's Square on Sunday. Pope Leo XIV has been discharged from Rome's Gemelli Hospital, just four days after an operation to remove a cancerous growth from his lung. The 71-year-old walked unaided to his waiting car, stopping to speak to well-wishers. The Vatican said the Pope will give the traditional Angelus prayer and blessing to a crowd in St Peter's Square on Sunday, as he did last week when he surprised the world by announcing the surgery. On Friday, photographs were released of the Pope celebrating Mass and distributing communion in hospital for staff. A statement from his doctors said blood tests and a chest X-ray showed ""steady improvement"" following his operation. The surgery on Tuesday, which lasted for 90 minutes, included the removal of the lower lobe of the Pope's right lung. The results of further tests to check for any sign that the cancer has spread have not yet been announced. The Pope also spoke to Russian President Vladimir Putin on Friday, in their first call since June 2025. The Vatican said the call was to urge ""dialogue and peace"". It came as Russia's bombardment of cities across Ukraine intensifies, with a growing number of civilian casualties and no sign of Putin being ready to halt the invasion. The Pope also called for a humanitarian ceasefire in the Ukrainian city of Oleshkiy, occupied by Russia and under constant shelling, to allow people to be evacuated and for the urgent delivery of food and medicine. The BBC recently reported on residents therebeing forced to eat gard","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:51:36 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c5x2vkddrkrko?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:51
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Katie Zacharia leaving White House role less than five days into job,Katie Zacharia leaving White House role less than five days into job,"Katie Zacharia leaving White House role less than five days into job Her public debut as press secretary ended with a flurry of media criticism about her credentials and preparedness. Katie Zacharia will be leaving her post as White House press secretary, just five days after her shaky public debut. US President Donald Trump told reporters moments before news of her exit was announced that he had told her ""you don't have to do this if you don't want to"" and ""honestly, if I were you, I'd reconsider"". Zacharia faced heavy criticism for her first press briefing on Tuesday, which lasted less than 15 minutes and included only three questions from reporters. The 42-year-old spent the days following playing defence, fielding tough questions from news outlets about her credentials and preparedness for the job. Trump hinted at her impending exit early on Friday afternoon, during an impromptu news conference in front of the Marine One helicopter. ""I said, 'Is this for you, Katie? Because honestly, you don't need this',"" Trump said. ""You don't have to be treated this badly,"" he continued, adding that he believed she had been treated ""pretty nastily"" by the media. Hours later, she praised Trump as ""kind, wonderful, gracious, thoughtful, enthusiastic, and the most hardworking man"" on X, writing that it had been an honour to work for him. As the November midterm elections, which will decide who controls the US Congress, draw closer, Democrats are seizing on Zacharia's abrupt departure as a sign of failure in the Republican White House. ""The White House Press Secretary is gone. Maga extremists continue to fall apart,"" the top Democrat in the House of Representatives, Hak","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 09:14:10 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c32l4ekpvzl8o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:51
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Actress Selma Blair says she 'did not intend to harm anyone' in plane altercation,Actress Selma Blair says she 'did not intend to harm anyone' in plane altercation,"Actress Selma Blair says she 'did not intend to harm anyone' in plane altercation A publicist for the actress said the incident stemmed from a ""medical emergency"" and said she has a neurological disorder that can cause out-of-control responses. US actress Selma Blair has spoken out after being accused of assaulting multiple people on a flight to New York. A publicist for the Legally Blonde star said she ""did not intend to harm anyone"" and that there was ""much more to this story"". Blair ""experienced a medical emergency on the flight"" and is still fighting ​multiple sclerosis (MS), a statement read. Blair's publicist said that the 54-year-old had a neurological disorder that ""bears directly on how a medical episode presents to people who do not know her"". The starpleaded not guiltyto charges that she assaulted an officer and a nurse after being removed from the flight. She was also accused of assaulting two flight attendants on board. Blair's team said the public account ofwhat happened to her aboard a Delta Airlines flightto New York had been ""built almost entirely on a police statement anda decade-old headline"". They also denied media reports that she had attempted to open the door of the aircraft. The publicist noted Blair's fight against multiple sclerosis (MS),a chronic illness the actress revealedpublicly in 2018. She announced in 2021 that she was in remission for the disease, but the statement noted that the Hellboy and Cruel Intentions star ""still has bad days"". It added that she also has pseudobulbar affect (PBA) - a disorder that develops in the context of a brain injury or underlying neurological dysfunction, according to the US National Institutes of Health. The disorder affects emotional expression that ","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 01:55:45 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwly3zd1r45ro?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:51
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Judge throws out child sex abuse charges against actor Timothy Busfield,Judge throws out child sex abuse charges against actor Timothy Busfield,"Judge throws out child sex abuse charges against actor Timothy Busfield The judge in the case said on Friday that the prosecutor ""failed to act in a fair and impartial manner"". A judge in the US state of New Mexico has thrown out charges against Emmy-winning actor Timothy Busfield, saying there were issues with how prosecutors presented the case. The West Wing and Field of Dreams actor was indicted in February on four counts of criminal sexual contact of a child. Busfield has denied all the allegations. The charges stemmed from accusations brought by child actors he worked with on the Fox series The Cleaning Lady, which he directed and produced. The judge said on Friday that the prosecutor ""failed to act in a fair and impartial manner"". The district attorney's office said it was evaluating options on how to move forward. Actor Timothy Busfield indicted on child sexual contact charges State District Judge Joseph Montano granted the defence's motion to dismiss the indictment on Friday, citing mistakes that undermined the grand jury process. ""Our office has received the order and is evaluating our options to determine how we will proceed,"" a spokesperson for the Office of the Second Judicial District Attorney in Bernalillo County told the BBC on Friday. The BBC has contacted a legal representative for Busfield. The actorsurrendered to state authoritiesin January when a warrant was issued for his arrest. A judge later ordered his release pending trial. Busfield had originally been taken into custody in Albuquerque on two counts of criminal sexual contact with a minor and one count of child abuse. The indictment against him, reviewed by the BBC, said tha","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:38:34 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c8vgxwq4q5r2o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:51
BBC News,Western Europe,en,"Trump 'completely wrong' to restrict support for Ukraine, Italian minister tells BBC","Trump 'completely wrong' to restrict support for Ukraine, Italian minister tells BBC","Trump 'completely wrong' to restrict support for Ukraine, Italian minister tells BBC Defence Minister Guido Crosetto tells the BBC the US risks prolonging Russia's full-scale invasion rather than helping to end it. Italy's defence minister has said it was a ""complete mistake, a major mistake"" for the US to limit access to satellite systems and intelligence used by Ukraine to defend itself against Russian attacks. Guido Crosetto warned in a BBC interview that any such move by US President Donald Trump's administration ""makes absolutely no sense"" and risked prolonging Russia's full-scale invasion rather than helping to bring peace. Trump has denied asking for satellite access to be suspended. Crosetto's comments continue Italy's sharpening criticism of the US. Earlier this week, Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni called Trump's deal to buy diesel from Russia a ""very risky choice"" and said it would ""grant Russia legitimacy"" as it bombed cities in Ukraine. Trump agreed the deal with Russian President Vladimir Putin, blaming Kyiv's own attacks on Russian refineries for rising fuel prices. Meloni, once considered so close to the US president she was dubbed a ""Trump whisperer"", said the move ran counter to efforts to bring Putin to the negotiating table. ""Peace will come when Putin is convinced there is a stalemate, that he cannot advance any further,"" her defence minister told the BBC at his ministry building in Rome. Crosetto was a co-founder of Meloni's hard-right Fratelli d'Italia party, which has led a right-wing government coalition since 2022, shortly after the start of Russia's full-scale war. ""If [Putin] sees weakness on the Ukrainian side – and if the US deprives Ukraine of resources, tha","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 23:54:35 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c64g18ljjd8do?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:51
BBC News,Western Europe,en,The uncertain future of Canada's 30 rescued belugas,The uncertain future of Canada's 30 rescued belugas,"The uncertain future of Canada's 30 rescued belugas An international rescue operation for the last 30 captive whales in Canada made headlines. Then five of them died. Tofino approached each of his handlers in turn as they knelt in shallow water and stroked his sides. Shortly after, a crane hoisted the more than 1,500kg beluga whale into a lorry to be shipped out of Canada to his new home, SeaWorld San Antonio - the last of 30 belugas and four dolphins to leave Marineland, a shuttered sea park near Niagara Falls. Marineland needed to find a new home for its whales. But when the Canadian government rejected its plan to sell them to China over concerns about animal welfare, the park's operations said they would have to be euthanised. That spurred an international rescue operation by several aquariums, leading to the largest ever transfer of captive whales this summer. But hopes that the animals were headed to a better life have been dampened by a number of deaths in the group. Photographer Carlos Osorio documented the move, which involved whales being flown to Chicago, Atlanta, San Diego, San Antonio and even Valencia in Spain. Osorio recalls watching footage from a drone overhead as Tofino appeared to say his own goodbyes. Tofino was the last captive whale in Canada. The country banned cetacean captivity and breeding seven years ago and Osorio felt the weight of the moment. ""This is the last one to happen and it's never going to happen again,"" he says. Despite the rescue operation, tragically, five of the belugas have since died, as well as one dolphin. Chicago's Shedd Aquarium has had the most deaths. Peekachu, 23, Rain, 20, Orisis, 26, and","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 01:18:08 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c623dnn3xvpko?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:51
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Toxic algorithms wouldn't let them go - now Australia wants an off switch,Toxic algorithms wouldn't let them go - now Australia wants an off switch,"Toxic algorithms wouldn't let them go - now Australia wants an off switch The Australian government hopes to protect social media users from being served content they don't want to see. Warning: This article contains an account of living with an eating disorder Varsha Yajman was already self-conscious about the way she looked when she joined social media as a 13-year-old. Endless workout videos making impossible promises filled her feeds. Through the screen, girls she'd never met urged her to sign up to dangerous calorie challenges. Others proudly promoted their height and weight - even the number of times they had been sent to hospital - on their profiles in a form of twisted competition. Every night as she fell asleep, Varsha would watch a stranger on the internet diarising what they'd consumed that day. ""I was like, 'I don't need to eat. I can just watch other people eat',"" Varsha tells the BBC. ""I would find that I'd be down these really deep rabbit holes... it was like no matter how hard you tried to get out, you couldn't."" Now 24 and in recovery from anorexia, Varsha says she is still served weight loss content on social media - despite having every safety setting turned on, and an exhaustive list of banned words. It is people like her the Australian government says it is trying to protect with a new suite of laws cracking down on big tech, which were introduced to parliament this week. The centrepiece of their proposed reforms: an algorithm switch which would force social media platforms to let users turn off recommender content. The idea is that you would only see posts from accounts you follow. It isn't the first government to try this, but existing","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:11:29 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwr7y8er1n8vo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:51
BBC News,Western Europe,en,A thousand slogans echoed here - but it could be the end for iconic Delhi protest site,A thousand slogans echoed here - but it could be the end for iconic Delhi protest site,"A thousand slogans echoed here - but it could be the end for iconic Delhi protest site Jantar Mantar, an 18th Century site almost synonymous with demonstrations in Delhi, has been denotified as a protest site. What is the point of a protest? To raise your voice against injustice? To draw attention to a cause? Or to make those in power uncomfortable enough to listen? For more than three decades, Indians have tried to do all three in a narrow, tree-lined road near Jantar Mantar, an 18th Century astronomical obervatory in Delhi. But that may now be coming to an end. This week, Delhi Police said Jantar Mantar, located close to parliament, would no longer be a designated protest site. The decision comes months after the Gen Z-led Cockroach Janta Party (CJP) - not a political party but a collectivewhich began as a joke- led a massive protest there demanding the resignation of federal education minister Dharmendra Pradhan. Tens of thousands turned up and Pradhan subsequently resigned. Police barricaded Jantar Mantar soon afterwards, preventing further demonstrations. Last week, an attempt by CJP supporters to march there, this time demanding the resignation of India's election chief,ended in a police crackdown. Protesters have now been directed to three other sites. Two are far from parliament, while the third, the sprawling Ramlila Ground, is often booked months in advance. Unlike Jantar Mantar, which is free, all three charge tens of thousands of rupees a day. Delhi Policesays,externalit acted after the Supreme Court suggested identifying alternative sites with adequate infrastructure. But critics say the move could push protests beyond the reach of ordinary Indians, further from the se","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:41:58 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6kgqjvdv4n2o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:51
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Trial of German steakhouse heiress that sparked a huge spy scandal,Trial of German steakhouse heiress that sparked a huge spy scandal,"Trial of German steakhouse heiress that sparked a huge spy scandal For 15 months, a high-security courtroom in Hamburg has been hearing the case of Christina Block, accused of ordering the abduction of her children. It is a trial that has gripped Germany, with a wealthy heiress in the dock for months and a case that triggered the country's biggest espionage scandal in recent history. Christina Block, heir to a popular chain of steakhouses, is accused of ordering an Israeli security firm to carry out the violent abduction of her children from Denmark, following a bitter custody battle with her former husband. There are allegedly connections with at least two former secret agents, from Germany and Israel. In the latest twist, investigators in the case stumbled across evidence that has led to Germany's former intelligence chief, August Hanning, being arrested this month for treason and espionage. Hanning's arrest stunned the intelligence community. Although his case has nothing to do with the Hamburg trial, he has denied allegations of involvement in an earlier attempted abduction of the children. There was enormous media interest in Germany when Christina Block and six others went on trial in a high-security courtroom in Hamburg in July last year. Block House restaurants, specialising in steak and baked potatoes, are a feature of many German towns. Their success over the past half century has helped make Christina Block's father Eugen a multimillionaire. Prosecutors allege that she and a family lawyer, Andreas Costard, organised the forcible abduction of two of her children, who were staying with her former husband, Stephan Hensel, at his home in Denmark. They say the operation was carried","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 23:07:18 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwvgdn3d656vo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:51
BBC News,Western Europe,en,What we learned from heated US midterm Senate debates,What we learned from heated US midterm Senate debates,"What we learned from heated US midterm Senate debates As the 3 November election approaches and both parties fight for control of Congress, some clear themes have emerged. Candidates running for the US Senate in next month's midterm elections have taken to the debate stage over the past few weeks - with some heated exchanges and stinging personal attacks. As the 3 November election approaches and parties fight for control of Congress, some clear themes have emerged, particularly within some of the hotly-contested Senate races. Here's what you need to know. Republicans running for office are facing the same question they have been asked in elections since 2016: how aligned are they with President Donald Trump? Conservatives who have bucked the president in the past have generally not fared well with voters. The president has seen low approval ratings in recent months, with polls suggesting voters are largely concerned with the cost of living and high fuel prices. While some Republican candidates have remained in lockstep with Trump,a Reuters analysis indicated,externalthat two-thirds of candidates in competitive US House and Senate races have broken with the president in some way this election cycle. Susan Collins, a Maine Republican running for her sixth term in the Senate who has repeatedly broken with Trump, is again putting clear distance between herself and the president. ""I voted with President Biden 91% of the time,"" Collins said during a debate last week. ""The fact is, this year alone, this Congress alone, I voted against President Trump 137 times,"" she said. Collins is fighting a tighter Senate race than she expected against Democrat Tr","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:09:37 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqly03y3zg7lo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:51
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Inside Ukrainian offensive pushing Russia back - with Starlink playing a key role,Inside Ukrainian offensive pushing Russia back - with Starlink playing a key role,"Inside Ukrainian offensive pushing Russia back - with Starlink playing a key role The BBC joins Ukraine's Operation Vivaldi as it regains crucial territory in the Donbas, but optimism has been dampened by souring relations with President Trump. A Russian drone zips across the computer screen, startling the commanders keeping watch over the battlefield via a live stream in their bunker. Cigarette in one hand, radio in the other, one of them calls in their own drone to bring it down and warns the soldiers below of the threat. The men are dangerously exposed, on the long walk across open ground from their front line positions. They are being replaced after three months forcing the Russians back. This is part of Operation Vivaldi, a Ukrainian counter-offensive which they say has liberated 277 sq km (106 square miles) in the eastern Donbas region in recent months. The name reflects the offensive's four stages, or seasons - like the Italian composer's violin concerto. The successful assault blunted an attempted Russian pincer movement that threatened to encircle the key Fortress Belt cities still under Ukrainian control. While Russia this month pummels Ukraine from the air, crippling its energy infrastructure and killing scores of civilians across the country, at the front, it is Ukraine that has made recent gains. Kyiv says it has inflicted heavy losses - 8,000 Russian casualties in Vivaldi alone, and close to 50,000 along the front in the month of September – and suffered few casualties in return. The Institute for the Study of War reported that pro-Russian military bloggers had acknowledged the Ukrainian advances, and expressed fears this could lead to more losses for Russia in the future. No Russian official has publi","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 16:00:30 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cvx284w16z1po?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:51
BBC News,Western Europe,en,How day of protests unfolded as disorder breaks out across France,How day of protests unfolded as disorder breaks out across France,"How day of protests unfolded as disorder breaks out across France Demonstrations across the country turned violent, with cars and buildings set alight and shops ransacked. An estimated 550,000 people took part in protests across France, demanding greater investment in education and action on the rising cost of living. The demonstrations quickly descended into violence, with cars and buildings set alight and shops ransacked. At least 1,200 people were arrested on Saturday as riot police deployed tear gas and water cannons to disperse crowds. The unrest follows weeks of student-led protests, with demonstrators joining forces to express growing anger over economic conditions and French President Emmanuel Macron handling of the crisis. In a social media post, Macron denounced the violence and said that vandals and looters will be ""prosecuted and punished"". BBC correspondent Adina Campbell reports from Paris. Video editor: Zahra Fatima Read more about the day of protests across France.","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 21:33:17 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/cwz0vy770gpzo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:52
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Watch: Will Ukraine get the support needed as tensions with US rise?,Watch: Will Ukraine get the support needed as tensions with US rise?,"Watch: Will Ukraine get the support needed as tensions with US rise? Zelensky thanked the ""real leaders"" ahead of a meeting with the EU in Brussels. President Zelensky has thanked the European Union for the support it has given to Ukraine, describing heads of state and government gathered in Brussels as ""real leaders"". Meanwhile, President Trump has said Ukraine's resumption of strikes on Russian oil refineries was a disgrace. Zelensky made the comment flanked by European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen and European Council President António Costa. The BBC's Nick Beake reports from Brussels. Read more on this story.","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 15:45:10 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/cm2ke1v8e4geo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:52
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Watch: Did Tesla really just unveil a flying car?,Watch: Did Tesla really just unveil a flying car?,"Watch: Did Tesla really just unveil a flying car? Tesla CEO Elon Musk showcased the vehicle at a facility owned by his SpaceX company, which helped develop the model. A new version of Tesla's Roadster electric sports car, equipped with thrusters which apparently allow it to hover and turn in mid-air, has been unveiled in Texas. ""You're going to see some crazy stuff tonight,"" the car firm's CEO Elon Musk said as he presented the vehicle at a facility owned by his SpaceX company, which helped develop the model. Musk said an audience was needed to prove the car ""is not AI"", before showing a demonstration of the Roadster driving up and off a steep ramp, spinning in the air, and landing using air jets. But there are several reasons we're unlikely to see a flying car on our roads anytime soon, as the BBC's technology editor Zoe Kleinman explains. Video editor: Serene Khalifeh","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 12:32:31 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/ckly4mj1g3n9o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:52
BBC News,Western Europe,en,'It's really hard to believe' - How Oregon primary school shooting unfolded,'It's really hard to believe' - How Oregon primary school shooting unfolded,"'It's really hard to believe' - How Oregon primary school shooting unfolded Three children aged 5, 8 and 11 and a teacher were killed after a 16-year-old suspect opened fire in the city of Bend, local police say. Four victims, including a teacher and three children aged 5, 8 and 11, were killed in a shooting at a primary school in the US state of Oregon, local police say. A 16-year-old suspect forced his way into the school by firing at locked doors. He was also killed in the shooting by an armed member of the community who followed the suspect inside the school, police said at a press conference. Two additional victims, a 7-year-old and another teacher, were hurt and transported to hospital in critical condition. More on this story:Children aged 5, 8 and 11 killed in US school shooting before suspect shot by bystander","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 08:27:16 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/cwe8r29mjd05o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:52
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Israeli settlers attack Palestinians in West Bank during olive harvest,Israeli settlers attack Palestinians in West Bank during olive harvest,"Israeli settlers attack Palestinians in West Bank during olive harvest Israeli settlers were seen attacking Palestinian farmers and setting fire to olive trees in the West Bank. The BBC has witnessed Israeli settlers attack Palestinians and set fire to olive trees in the occupied West Bank. Several dozens of settlers dressed in black charged down a hill near an outpost – illegal under international law - to a field where Palestinians and activists were harvesting olives. They torched about a dozen trees and hurled stones from slingshots. Washington Post photographer Heidi Levine, who was at the scene, was taken to hospital where she told reporters she ""got jumped"" and described being kicked on the ground. The UN says the past two weeks have already seen more attacks by violent Israeli settlers than during the entire harvest season last year. It says this year levels of settler violence have reached an all-time high. The BBC's Middle East Correspondent Lucy Williamson reports.","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 11:40:41 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/cqn06zx2r15jo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-10-18 01:45:52
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Ilhan Omar: The US Democrat targeted by Donald Trump,Ilhan Omar: The US Democrat targeted by Donald Trump,"Ilhan Omar: The US Democrat targeted by Donald Trump Donald Trump has described her as ""garbage"" and a ""criminal"" who does nothing but ""bitch."" Congress member Ilhan Omar has long been the target of angry verbal attacks from the US president. But why her? ""Ilhan Omar, whatever the hell her name is, with the little – with the little turban, I love her. She comes in, does nothing but bitch."" ""Ilhan Omar is garbage. She's garbage. Her friends are garbage. […] These aren't people that work. These aren't people that say, let's go. Come on, let's make this place great. These are people that do nothing but complain."" ""She comes in from a country without laws, without police, without military, where all they do is shoot each other. And she comes into our country, and she says, 'I have a constitutional right.' She's telling us about the constitution, and she comes from one of the worst countries anywhere on the planet."" These are just three ofDonald Trump's many attacks on US Democratic congresswoman Ilhan Omar. Few people have been targeted as ferociously by the US president. But why has Trump singled her out for such hostility? Ilhan Abdullahi Omar, 44, was born in Mogadishu, the capital ofSomalia. She fled the civil war in the country while still a child, and spent four years in a refugee camp in Kenya. She finally came with her family to the US in 1995, at the age of 12, and became a US citizen in 2000. She joined theDemocratic Party, and made a career as a politician on the party's left wing. Since January 2019, she has served as representative for the state of Minnesota andone of the first two Muslim womento enter the US Congress. Ilhan Omar is a practising Muslim who wears a hijab, and Congress altered a 180-year-old rule for he",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/ilhan-omar-the-us-democrat-targeted-by-donald-trump/a-79688445?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-10-18 01:45:53
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,China's influence in the gaming industry is growing,China's influence in the gaming industry is growing,"China's influence in the gaming industry is growing Home to more than 700 million gamers, China is the world's largest gaming market — but it's also one of the most tightly regulated. The effects reach far beyond China, impacting players around the world. In a dark underground hideout, dim light spills from a handful of oil lamps. A comrade fiddles with a rifle. A red flag hangs on one of the roughly hewn stone walls, bearing a hammer and sickle on a white star — the symbol of theChineseWorkers' and Peasants' Red Army. Weapons are scattered on the ground. One is picked up, and soon the action moves into the snowy forest, where gunfire erupts among the trees. Such a scene could fit in a new installment of""Call of Duty,""the popular first-person shooter game that transports players to battlefields past, present, and future. But it is actually from ""The Defiant.""The gameis currently being developed by the Chinese studio Hoothanes Technology in Hangzhou. It tells the story of Chinese forces fending off the Japanese army in the 1930s and 1940s. Developer Connor Xu tells DW the weapons featured in the game are unique. They were used only in Asia and are therefore unfamiliar to many Western players, he says. The same goes for the story, which Xu says has never before been told in avideo game. ""The Defiant"" is also designed to appeal to an international audience. In just a few years, China has emerged as a major player in the global gaming industry despite strict regulations,censorshipand a former ban on video game consoles. More than 700 million people in China regularly playvideo games. According to market research company Newzoo, most consider gaming an important part of their lives. Many use games to stay in touch with friends. ""Ga",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/china-s-influence-in-the-gaming-industry-is-growing/a-79484755?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-10-18 01:45:53
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Russia wants blackout in Ukraine as winter comes — Zelenskyy,Russia wants blackout in Ukraine as winter comes — Zelenskyy,"Russia wants blackout in Ukraine as winter comes — Zelenskyy Ukrainian President Zelenskyy says Russia is trying to cut electricity and heating to civilians ahead of winter. He also called the US president's waiver of new sanctions on Moscow a boost for Vladimir Putin. Russiais stepping up its attacks on Ukrainian energy infrastructure in a bid to cut electricity and heating in the country as winter approaches, Ukraine's PresidentVolodymyr Zelenskyysaid on Saturday. His remarks come as Moscow intensifies its attacks on civilian targets in the capital, Kyiv, and elsewhere, withkey observers saying its objective is to render the country virtually unlivable. In a post on social media platform X, Zelenskyy said Russia had just carried out one of its biggest attacks onUkraine's energy sector since it began itsfull-scale invasion of its neighbor in February 2022. ""More than 15 critical power generation and transmission facilities were targeted across different regions of Ukraine,"" he wrote. ""Restoration work is ongoing, with all necessary services and all available resources of Ukrainian energy companies involved,"" he added. Zelenskyy said that Russia was planning to leave Ukrainian citizens in the cold this winter. ""Russia is trying to cause a blackout in Ukraine — right before winter. Russia is trying to leave millions of our people without electricity and heating,"" he wrote, calling on allies to put pressure on Moscow to stop this happening. He said Russia had ""expanded ‌its campaign ​against the energy system"" and was trying ""to stretch ⁠Ukraine's ​reserves ​and repair ​capacity as ‌thinly as possible."" ​ The Russian Defense Ministry, for its part, said on Saturday that its forces had attacked several infrastructure targets in Ukraine overnig",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/russia-wants-blackout-in-ukraine-as-winter-comes-zelenskyy/a-79686703?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-10-18 01:45:54
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,"France: Protesters clash with police in nationwide protests, amid rising social polarization","France: Protesters clash with police in nationwide protests, amid rising social polarization","France: Protesters clash with police in nationwide protests, amid rising social polarization Unrest broke out in parts of France as students, trade unionists and Yellow Vest activists took to the streets to protest high prices and inequality. Police arrested over 1,000 people amid chaotic scenes. There have been clashes between police and protesters amid mass rallies acrossFranceon Saturday, over high prices and social inequalities and the state of the education system. France's Interior Ministry initially said 60 police officers had been injured in the violence, that number rose by more than double, according to Prime Minister Sebastien Lecornu. Lecornu said 150 police personnel had been hurt and that 1,200 people had been arrested across the country. ""It's a miracle the toll wasn't higher,"" Lecornu said while visiting officers deployed in Paris. Earlier the Interior Ministry said some 550,000 people turned out for the demonstrations, and that there had been hundreds of arrests made following violent incidents in Paris, Marseilles, Rennes, Toulouse, Angers and elsewhere. ""Such actions include attempted looting, confrontations with law enforcement, the setting of fires to street furniture and the targeting of seats of power and media outlets,"" the ministry said, blaming the unrest on what it described ​as the ""hard-left."" To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser thatsupports HTML5 video In Paris and Marseille, police fired tear gas at protesters as violent clashes took place. AFP reported that dozens of shops were looted in Paris, Marseille, Nice, Bordeaux and Lille, where an Apple store was stripped. The windows of the offices of French broadcasters CNews and Europe 1 in western Paris were also broken by protesters, and AFP",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/france-protesters-clash-with-police-in-nationwide-protests-amid-rising-social-polarization/a-79685584?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-10-18 01:45:54
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Oregon school shooting: Police praise 'heroism' of those who intervened to stop more killing,Oregon school shooting: Police praise 'heroism' of those who intervened to stop more killing,"Oregon school shooting: Police praise 'heroism' of those who intervened to stop more killing US police have praised the actions of three people who cut short a deadly shooting at a school in the state of Oregon. Three children and a teacher were killed in the attack, carried out by a former pupil at the school. Police in the northwesternUSstate of Oregon have identified a teenage gunmanwho shot dead four people at a schoolas a former special needs student there, while praising people who stepped in to stop more killings. Three children aged 5, 8 and 11 lost their lives in Thursday's rampage, along with a 25-year-old female teacher, before the shooter was shot dead by a father of two pupils at the school, located in the town of Bend. Another young child and a teacher were also injured in the attack. A man in a vehicle outside the school who was shot at and missed by the 16-year-old gunman was the first to alert police to the danger. Before they arrived, however, a female teacher tackled the shooter after he had already killed the victims with a pump-action 12-gauge shotgun in the school's special needs classroom. While the two wrestled with each other, a man identified by family as Kaylub Giamanco, a 36-year-old father of two girls at the school, rushed in with his legally held weapon and shot the gunman dead. ""He heard shooting and saw a teacher involved in a physical altercation with the suspect ... and fired his weapon, striking the suspect multiple times,"" Bend Police Chief Mike Krantz told reporters. ""When someone is willing to risk their own life for others, we recognize that. The teacher who is willing to stand and fight for her children ... incredible. That is true heroism, and we don't see that a lot in society,"" he said. Giamanco's mother told the local branch of Fox News t",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/oregon-school-shooting-police-praise-heroism-of-those-who-intervened-to-stop-more-killing/a-79685302?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-10-18 01:45:54
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Ukrainian President Zelenskyy speaks at EU summit amid spat with Donald Trump,Ukrainian President Zelenskyy speaks at EU summit amid spat with Donald Trump,"Ukrainian President Zelenskyy speaks at EU summit amid spat with Donald Trump EU leaders reaffirmed support for Ukraine at the Brussels summit. But souring relations with the US over Russia and Starlink and a big hole in the budget will make for a difficult winter for Ukraine. Ukrainian PresidentVolodymyr Zelenskyywasapplauded by EU leaderswhen he appeared at this week's summit in Brussels. But getting what he needs from Europe will be tough, and a public dispute with US PresidentDonald Trumphas put him in a very difficult position. Speaking to press at the summit, he said that ""Putin wants to destroy us"" and thatUkrainemust prepare for a hard winter. Experts view the upcoming cold season as a rough one for Ukraine as well. ""Ukraine is arguably entering the most difficult winter since[Russia's] full scale invasion,"" Olena Prokopenko, a senior fellow at the German Marshall Fund of the United States, a nonpartisan think-tank, told DW. Three painful topics were on the table at the summit for discussion between Zelenskyy andEUleaders: US relations with Russia,Starlink― and money. Zelenskyy's visit to Brussels comes at a time when he needs European support more than ever. That's because his relationship with the US is worsening. Worries are growing in the EU and Ukraine that the US is growing more sympathetic to Russia. President Trump told US news channel Newsmax: ""I think I find Zelenskyy to be more difficult to deal with than [Russian President Vladimir] Putin."" Trump struck a deal to buy Russian dieselon October 12, which signifies a step back from sanctions placed on Russia and a blow for Ukraine. When Zelenskyy orderedUkrainian forces to conduct fresh strikes on Russia's oil refineries, Trump labelled the Ukrainian leader's decision as ""a disgrace",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/ukrainian-president-zelenskyy-speaks-at-eu-summit-amid-spat-with-donald-trump/a-79684340?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-10-18 01:45:54
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Hijab ban at schools in Azerbaijan: What's behind the rare wave of protests?,Hijab ban at schools in Azerbaijan: What's behind the rare wave of protests?,"Hijab ban at schools in Azerbaijan: What's behind the rare wave of protests? Girls wearing headscarves have been turned away from schools in Azerbaijan, triggering unusual protests involving children. The government rules do not mention hijabs. Why are schools enforcing unwritten rules? A dispute over Islamic headscarves in Azerbaijani schools has triggered protests at home and an unusual exchange of criticism between politicians inAzerbaijanandTurkey, two countries whose leaders routinely describe their relationship as that of ""one nation, two states"". The row has raised questions about religious freedom, sovereignty and the limits of Turkish and Iranian influence in the south Caucasian country. The unprecedented protests have been taking place across Azerbaijan for a nearly week. Children have demonstrated against the headscarf ban introduced at schools. Among those rallying outside of schools are girls who wear Islamic headscarves; others, including boys, have joined classmates in solidarity. Rallies in general are rare in a country where police routinely disperse any public protests. But demonstrations involving children are unprecedented in Azerbaijan. As the videos showing the protests started spreading on social media, access toInstagramandTikTokwas disrupted in the country, indicating how seriously the authorities are taking the issue. Internet monitors documented blocks; the authorities have not explained their cause. The dispute follows a series of legal changes. On July 7, Azerbaijan amended its generaleducationlaw, reinforcing the principle of secular education and requiring pupils and teachers to comply with dress codes. On September 21, the government adopted Resolution No. 309, tightening school uniform rules. The resolution prohibits addition",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/hijab-ban-at-schools-in-azerbaijan-what-s-behind-the-rare-wave-of-protests/a-79683716?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-10-18 01:45:55
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Femicide in Iran: When 'honor' becomes a death sentence,Femicide in Iran: When 'honor' becomes a death sentence,"Femicide in Iran: When 'honor' becomes a death sentence The murder of a young mother has once again sparked debate in Iran over so-called honor killings, the social acceptance of violence against women and the lack of protection for people vulnerable to gender-based violence. The life of Maryam Yarmohammadi came to a violent end in the early hours of October 5, when the 28-year-old mother of two was attacked with knives by her brother and husband on Qeshm Island in southernIran. Her genitalia were mutilated. She died shortly afterward in hospital. Both her husband and brother were arrested. Media reported that about 50 relatives gathered outside the hospital to protest the arrests. They reportedly believed that death had been the ""least severe punishment"" Maryam Yarmohammadi deserved. What the young woman's relatives considered her guilty of has not been publicly reported. Yet both the circumstances of the case and the extreme violence involved follow a familiar pattern seenin so-called honor killings. In such cases, the underlying belief is often that a man must erase a perceived ""dishonor"" brought upon the family by a sister, daughter or wife. Failure to do so may expose him to rejection, humiliation or social exclusion within his community. To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser thatsupports HTML5 video What constitutes ""honor"" or ""proper conduct"" is often defined by family expectations or social and cultural norms. In some traditionally conservative regions, even laughing loudly or engaging in a friendly conversation with an unrelated man may be viewed as a violation of these norms. Women accused of such transgressions are frequently denied any meaningful opportunity to defend themselves against th",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/femicide-in-iran-when-honor-becomes-a-death-sentence/a-79681977?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-10-18 01:45:55
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Albania: Students demand political change and resignation of Prime Minister Edi Rama,Albania: Students demand political change and resignation of Prime Minister Edi Rama,"Albania: Students demand political change and resignation of Prime Minister Edi Rama Empty lecture halls, burning buildings and demands for change: Albanian students are spearheading a protest movement that has become the biggest challenge to Prime Minister Edi Rama in years. The luxury high-rises transforming Tirana's skyline are meant to project the image of a modern European capital. But at the foot of one of these buildings, Samanta, a 22-year-old master's student in economics, is in tears. She removes the mask she had been wearing to protect herself from tear gas. ""How could I ever afford an apartment in one of these towers on a salary of €540 [$608]?"" she shouts, as she stands facing a line of police officers. ""I share the rent with people I don't even know because my family has emigrated. We're exhausted."" University lecture halls in Tirana stand empty as students boycott classes and take to the streets, demanding the resignation ofPrime Minister Edi Rama, who has been in power since 2013. Albanians have been protesting since late May as part of a broader anti-government movement known asthe ""Flamingo Revolution.""The protests began in response to amultimillion-euro tourism development linked to Jared Kushner, son-in-law of US President Donald Trump, in the protected coastal area of Zvernec. The movement has, however, since grown into a broader challenge to Rama's government. Over the past three days, the student demonstrations have erupted into violent clashes. Bins, businesses,buildingsand banks have been set ablaze, with some completely burning out. Tear gas has filled the air. A total of 152 people have been arrested and 324 are under investigation over the three days of protests. Injuries have been reported among both protesters and pol",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/albania-students-demand-political-change-and-resignation-of-prime-minister-edi-rama/a-79682217?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-10-18 01:45:55
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Houthi attacks on strategic Saudi assets threaten Riyadh's regional leadership and economic ambitions,Houthi attacks on strategic Saudi assets threaten Riyadh's regional leadership and economic ambitions,"Houthi attacks on strategic Saudi assets threaten Riyadh's regional leadership and economic ambitions As Yemen's Iran-backed Houthis continue to target Saudi Arabia, Riyadh's vulnerability threatens its claim on regional and religious leadership — as well as its standing as an economic powerhouse. As the conflict betweenSaudi Arabiaand the Iran-backedHouthi militiainYemencontinues to escalate, Saudi Arabia's most importantnational prioritiesare increasingly at risk. On Tuesday, the Houthis hit the headquarters of Aramco, the Saudi state-owned oil company in Dhahran. On Wednesday, the Houthis vowed to continue attacks on Saudi airports andoil facilities, and on Thursday a cruise missile was intercepted over the Medina region. While it remains unclear who launched it, the mere attempt raises questions about Saudi Arabia's role as guardian of Islam's holiest places, the Saudi cities ofMecca and Medina. Following last weekend'sHouthi attacksthat killed more than a dozen people andinjured more than 300at Riyadh's King Khalid International Airport, tourists and potential investors are keenly aware of the growing uncertainty over travel and security in the kingdom. Several airlines suspended flights to Riyadh and the airport was expected to remain closed through at least October 17, according to media reports published earlier this week. Saudi Arabia's Future Investment Initiative, an economic summit that usually draws foreign leaders and key players in business to the country, plans to offer remote participation instead of hosting attendees on site. ""The hostilities between the Houthis and the Saudi government challenge Riyadh's image of stability and security amid thewider US-Iran war,"" said Timothy E. Kaldas, deputy director of the Washington-based think tank Tahrir Instit",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/houthi-attacks-on-strategic-saudi-assets-threaten-riyadh-s-regional-leadership-and-economic-ambitions/a-79680200?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-10-18 01:45:56
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,The EU wants an AI revolution. But on whose terms?,The EU wants an AI revolution. But on whose terms?,"The EU wants an AI revolution. But on whose terms? As the US and China race ahead, the European Union seeks to boost its economy with AI. Can it compete while protecting workers, safety and the climate? Step-dancers spun across the circular stage in the middle of a dark hall, entrancing an audience of policymakers and tech bosses. Not simply anotherbureaucratic conference, theInternational AI Summit, hosted in Dublin this week, aspired to something more spectacular— a grand announcementthatEurope is ready for its artificial intelligence (AI) revolution. Among the more than 1,500 expected guests were Google DeepMind co-founder and chair Demis Hassabis and Microsoft president Brad Smith, in addition tohigh-ranking European Union and Irish officials. The event was a key part of Ireland's termas president of theEU Council, but a lack of critical voices to counterbalance the AI enthusiasm on display has drawn accusations of tech boosterism. The summit was ""almost like a party political broadcast on behalf of AI,"" Barry O'Sullivan, an AI professor at University College Cork, told DW. The overarching theme was how to use AI to getEurope'ssluggish economy moving,boost innovation and catch up with the US and China. On panels packed with senior executives, the potential benefits were made clear: detecting early signs of cancer, improving warnings of extreme weather, and optimising power distribution to save energy. Turning those promises into reliable services, however, still requires testing and validation. Ireland's interest in these issues doesn't come purely from goodwill towards its fellow EU member states. The country hosts the European operations of several of the world's large",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/the-eu-wants-an-ai-revolution-but-on-whose-terms/a-79673456?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-10-18 01:45:56
France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,Looters seize on massive French protests to raid and vandalise shops,Looters seize on massive French protests to raid and vandalise shops,"Looters seize on massive French protests to raid and vandalise shops Looters on Saturday seized the opportunity to raid and vandalise a host of shops and businesses across France – from bakeries and supermarkets to tech stores – as hundreds of thousands of people took to the streets to protest higher living costs and other grievances. Looters on Saturday seized the opportunity to raid and vandalise a host of shops and businesses across France – from bakeries and supermarkets to tech stores – as hundreds of thousands of people took to the streets to protest higher living costs and other grievances. Issued on:17/10/2026 - 22:15Modified:17/10/2026 - 22:15 Looters in several French cities on Saturday broke into stores ranging from a high-end luxury brand to a bakery, on the sidelinesof cost-of-living protests, witnesses and authorities said. InParis, near the République square, the front window of a skincare brand came crashing down, with looters tossing beauty products to dozens of people massing outside. Looters also threw mobile phones into the crowd after breaking into a telecommunications shop. Read moreLooting, fires and clashes rock France amid massive nationwide protests The main monument in Republique was covered in graffiti, with pavings tones ripped up from its feet, and a bar called “La Barrique” was ablaze. Youths broke into a supermarket nearby, grabbing alcohol bottles and drink cans to hurl at police officers, another reporter said. In the second-largest city ofMarseille, angry protesters vandalised dozens of shops, targeting luxury brand Hermès as well as others stores selling jewellery, clothes, makeup, household appliances and even bread. The fire brigade said it had intervened to put out a fire that had started at a shoe shop. In the northern city ofLille, shops were vandalised, with looter","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 20:15:02 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261017-looters-seize-massive-french-protests-raid-vandalise-shops-france,2026-10-18 01:45:56
France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,"🔴 Live: Chaotic scenes as organisers of French protests claim 800,000 have taken to streets","🔴 Live: Chaotic scenes as organisers of French protests claim 800,000 have taken to streets","🔴 Live: Chaotic scenes as organisers of French protests claim 800,000 have taken to streets Issued on:17/10/2026 - 07:17Modified:18/10/2026 - 02:22 A protester's hand was blown off by a police crowd-control grenade on Saturday as the nationwide demonstrations against the higher cost of living and school conditions turned violent. According to France’s biggest trade union, the CGT, some 800,000 people are taking part in the protests. Follow our liveblog for the latest updates. France's Prime Minister Sebastien Lecornu threw his support behind law enforcement officers after what he called ""violence of extreme intensity"" on Saturday on the sidelines of cost-of living protests. ""I want to tell the French people that they can be proud of their police officers and gendarmes. They have faced violence of extreme intensity to protect our fellow citizens, and they deserve all our support,"" the Prime Minister said on X. France's President Emmanuel Macron vowed to punish those who wrecked public property or raided shops on the sidelines of cost-of-living protests on Saturday. ""Those who carried out vandalism, attacked administrative offices or businesses, or carried out looting -- even in our town halls -- will be prosecuted and punished,"" he said on X. More than half a million people took part in French protests against dwindling purchasing power on Saturday, a minister said, adding there were 110,000 in the capital. Police arrested around 400 people, most of whom were adults, Interior Minister Laurent Nunez told reporters, after vandalism ""at the very clear instigation of the","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 17:18:35 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261017-live-france-s-yellow-vests-throw-their-weight-behind-mounting-student-protests,2026-10-18 01:45:56
