France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,Will Paramount’s creation of Skydance test France’s cultural exception?,Will Paramount’s creation of Skydance test France’s cultural exception?,"Will Paramount’s creation of Skydance test France’s cultural exception? Skydance’s $110 billion creation tests whether France’s cultural exception can still compel a leveraged global media giant to fund, protect and prioritise French creativity, or merely treat it as a compliance cost. Skydance’s $110 billion creation tests whether France’s cultural exception can still compel a leveraged global media giant to fund, protect and prioritise French creativity, or merely treat it as a compliance cost. Issued on:17/10/2026 - 15:43 Thecompletionthis month of Paramount’s $110 billion acquisition of Warner Bros. Discovery has created Skydance: amedia behemoth that, as a result of the transaction, comprises some of Hollywood’s most recognisable studios, including Warner Brothers, Paramount Pictures, HBO, Paramount +, a vast television portfolio, as well as news platforms,CNNand CBS. The takeover was months in the making and garnered much public attention. What started as a tête-à-tête bidding war at the end of 2025 againstNetflixculminated in a federal ruling against the 12 US states that tried to block the transaction on antitrust grounds at the end of September – just a week before final approval. Read moreParamount completes takeover of Warner Bros, creating media behemoth Skydance As film industry opponents feared job cuts, media groups worried about the editorial independence of a news platform under David Ellison, whose family has close ties to Republican US PresidentDonald Trump. But from a French perspective, the development may cultivate a narrative that is less a question of whether yet another media giant can succeed in presenting true competition against the likes of Netflix or Canal+, and more about how a heavily indebted, consolidated global studio can ","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:43:00 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261017-will-paramount-creation-of-skydance-test-france-cultural-exception,2026-10-18 01:45:58
France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,Fires and clashes rock France amid massive nationwide protests,Fires and clashes rock France amid massive nationwide protests,"Fires and clashes rock France amid massive nationwide protests Protesters clashed with police in Paris and other French cities on Saturday as hundreds of thousands took to the streets to protest higher living costs and the declining conditions in schools and other public services. Several town halls were broken into – and in at least one instance set on fire – and many shops were vandalised and ransacked. One protester had his hand torn off by what appeared to be a crowd-control grenade used by police. Protesters clashed with police in Paris and other French cities on Saturday as hundreds of thousands took to the streets to protest higher living costs and the declining conditions in schools and other public services. Several town halls were broken into – and in at least one instance set on fire – and many shops were vandalised and ransacked. One protester had his hand torn off by what appeared to be a crowd-control grenade used by police. Issued on:17/10/2026 - 14:31Modified:17/10/2026 - 22:59 Police and protestersclashed violently in Paris and other French citiesSaturday on the sidelines of nationwide marches against soaring inflation, with one demonstrator's hand blown off, multiple blazes set and looters breaking into dozens of stores to strip them bare. The widespread unrest followsweeks of student protestsand deepens political uncertainty in France as itreadies for electionsin six months' time to replace PresidentEmmanuel Macron. Macron and his government were criticised by many of the demonstrators, who numbered 500,000 across the country, according to the CGT union. Authorities did not immediately give their estimate of numbers. Police used tear gas and water cannon against masked protesters in Paris and other cities who were throwing projectiles and aiming fireworks. Several parked cars were torched in the capital, and more than 130 arrests made. Read more'Finish what we started': After student protests, France braces for return of the Yellow Vests In the so","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 12:31:24 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261017-looting-fires-and-clashes-rock-france-amid-massive-nationwide-protests,2026-10-18 01:45:58
France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,"🔴 Live: Tens of thousands join protests across France, clashes reported in several cities","🔴 Live: Tens of thousands join protests across France, clashes reported in several cities","🔴 Live: Tens of thousands join protests across France, clashes reported in several cities Issued on:17/10/2026 - 07:17Modified:18/10/2026 - 02:22 A protester's hand was blown off by a police crowd-control grenade on Saturday as the nationwide demonstrations against the higher cost of living and school conditions turned violent. According to France’s biggest trade union, the CGT, some 800,000 people are taking part in the protests. Follow our liveblog for the latest updates. France's Prime Minister Sebastien Lecornu threw his support behind law enforcement officers after what he called ""violence of extreme intensity"" on Saturday on the sidelines of cost-of living protests. ""I want to tell the French people that they can be proud of their police officers and gendarmes. They have faced violence of extreme intensity to protect our fellow citizens, and they deserve all our support,"" the Prime Minister said on X. France's President Emmanuel Macron vowed to punish those who wrecked public property or raided shops on the sidelines of cost-of-living protests on Saturday. ""Those who carried out vandalism, attacked administrative offices or businesses, or carried out looting -- even in our town halls -- will be prosecuted and punished,"" he said on X. More than half a million people took part in French protests against dwindling purchasing power on Saturday, a minister said, adding there were 110,000 in the capital. Police arrested around 400 people, most of whom were adults, Interior Minister Laurent Nunez told reporters, after vandalism ""at the very clear instigation of the","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 12:30:33 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261017-live-france-s-yellow-vests-throw-their-weight-behind-mounting-student-protests,2026-10-18 01:45:58
