Al Jazeera (EN),Middle East (English),en,At least 17 killed in stampede at religious gathering in India,At least 17 killed in stampede at religious gathering in India,"At least 17 killed in stampede at religious gathering in India Incident took place at festival in north Kolkata, in India&#039;s West Bengal state, amid big crowds and rainfall, police said. Save Share A “stampede” during a Hindu religious festival in eastern India has killed at least 17 people and injured 25, officials said. A seven-year-old girl was among the fatalities in Saturday’s disaster in north Kolkata in West Bengal, the state’s Chief Minister Suvendu Adhikari told reporters. “Six bodies have so far been identified,” Adhikari said. Police said the injured were taken to hospital from a Durga Puja pandal, a temporary pavilion housing idols of the goddess Durga at a festival which attracts thousands of ⁠⁠⁠⁠devotees. A police statement said the stampede happened in the evening due to “heavy crowds of pandal hoppers amid rainfall”. A senior police official at the scene told the AFP news agency that an announcement caused “commotion” after it started raining and the “crowd waiting outside the pandal [pavilion] was pushing, pulling and rushing forward and fell on the ground, leading to the stampede.” Prime Minister Narendra Modi described the incident as “saddening.” “Condolences to the bereaved families. Wishing the injured a speedy recovery. The West Bengal Government is providing all possible help to the affected families,” Modi wrote on X. ⁠Modi’s office announced a ⁠compensation payment of 200,000 rupees ($2,106) for the next of kin of those who died and ‌50,000 rupees ($527) each for the injured. Interior Minister Amit Shah said the “administration is ensuring that all possible medical facilities are being provided to the injured.” Deadly crowd incidents","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 19:48:54 +0000",https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/17/at-least-17-killed-in-stampede-at-religious-gathering-in-india?traffic_source=rss,2026-10-18 01:46:03
Al Jazeera (EN),Middle East (English),en,Albanian police fire rubber bullets as protests against Rama escalate,Albanian police fire rubber bullets as protests against Rama escalate,"Albanian police fire rubber bullets as protests against Rama escalate Five days of clashes rock Tirana as students join a wider movement demanding Prime Minister Edi Rama&#039;s resignation. Save Share Police inAlbaniahave fired rubber bullets at antigovernment protesters in the capital, Tirana, as violent clashes intensify over demands for Prime Minister Edi Rama to resign. The clashes continued on Saturday for afifth consecutiveday after high school and university students joined a broader movement against Rama, whose governing centre-left Socialist Party has held power since 2013. The unrest has brought growing anger over education, unemployment and alleged corruption into a wider confrontation with the government. Protesters threw stones, petrol bombs and fireworks at police near Rama’s office and elsewhere in central Tirana. Some set cars and rubbish bins alight, creating burning barricades across streets. Police responded with tear gas, water cannon and baton charges as demonstrators pushed against security lines. The AFP news agency reported that officers used rubber bullets for the first time since the demonstrations began. National police chief Skender Hita confirmed their use at a news conference on Saturday, saying they had been deployed “in a limited manner”. The Ministry of Health and Social Welfare said nine protesters and four police officers received medical treatment on Saturday. Hita said 167 people had been arrested over five days of unrest, while 132 officers had been injured. Seven police officers were also suspended pending investigations into allegations that they had exceeded their authority. Rama, who is serving his fourth term and has re","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 19:26:13 +0000",https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/17/albanian-police-fire-rubber-bullets-as-protests-against-rama-escalate?traffic_source=rss,2026-10-18 01:46:03
