Al Jazeera (EN),Middle East (English),en,Israeli drone attacks kill six Palestinians across Gaza despite ‘ceasefire’,Israeli drone attacks kill six Palestinians across Gaza despite ‘ceasefire’,"Israeli drone attacks kill six Palestinians across Gaza despite ‘ceasefire’ Attacks hit several areas as UNICEF reports more than 340 children killed since &#039;ceasefire&#039; began a year ago. Save Share Israeli drone attacks havekilledat least six Palestinians across the Gaza Strip, according to Palestinian medical and emergency workers, as deadly raids continue despite a year-old “ceasefire”. The attacks on Saturday were in northern, central and southern Gaza, with one of the latest reported deaths near Jabalia refugee camp. A Palestinian man was killed and several others wounded when an Israeli drone struck a group of civilians near al-Faluja cemetery, west of Jabalia, reported the Palestinian Wafa news agency, citing medical sources. One Palestinian was killed and several injured when an Israeli drone struck the al-Mawasi area of Rafah, Wafa said. Earlier, two people werekilled and others woundedin an attack in the Tuffah neighbourhood of northeast Gaza City, according to staff at al-Shifa Hospital. Witnesses told Al Jazeera that the people hit were returning from the burial of an earlier attack victim. Another person was killed in a drone attack in eastern Gaza City, al-Shifa Hospital said. In central Gaza, staff at Al-Aqsa Martyrs Hospital reported one person killed and another injured in Deir el-Balah. In an update issued on Saturday, Gaza’s Health Ministry said 1,511 Palestinians had been killed and 5,375 injured since the United States-brokered “ceasefire” was announced on October 10, 2025. In an update covering the preceding 48 hours, the ministry reported six deaths and 43 injuries across the strip. It said 74,469 Palestinians had been killed and 175,492","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 18:26:59 +0000",https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/17/israeli-drone-attacks-kill-six-palestinians-across-gaza-despite-ceasefire?traffic_source=rss,2026-10-18 01:46:04
Al Jazeera (EN),Middle East (English),en,India and West Indies denied a thrilling finale as rain hits final T20,India and West Indies denied a thrilling finale as rain hits final T20,"India and West Indies denied a thrilling finale as rain hits final T20 India reached 118-5 after seven overs with Naman Dhir hitting 24 off six balls before rain forced an end to the game. Save Share India and the West Indies were forced to settle fora 2-2 stalematein their five-match Twenty20 International series after Saturday’s deciding match lasted just 48 minutes before it met a watery end. At Bengaluru’s M Chinnaswamy Stadium, where play began an hour and 45 minutes behind schedule and the match was reduced to nine overs a side, India had reached 118-5 in 7.4 overs when the rain returned, forcing the umpires to call off play with not even a five-overs-a-side shootout possible. The stadium was hosting its first international fixture since the October 2024 Test match between India and New Zealand and more than 20,000 expectant fans braved the inclement weather. Their patience was rewarded when India’s batters went on the rampage after being put in by West Indies captain Shai Hope, with Ishan Kishan, Sanju Samson and the impressive Naman Dhir – with an unbeaten 24 off six balls – leading the way. India lashed eight fours and seven sixes in their limited batting stint, with Kishan bringing up the 100th six of the series by swinging Romario Shepherd over long leg and out of the stadium. But then, the skies opened up again, emphatically washing away any prospect of a decisive outcome.","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 17:42:01 +0000",https://www.aljazeera.com/sports/2026/10/17/india-and-west-indies-denied-thrilling-finale-as-rain-hits-final-t20?traffic_source=rss,2026-10-18 01:46:04
Al Jazeera (EN),Middle East (English),en,Pope Leo returns to Vatican after lung cancer surgery,Pope Leo returns to Vatican after lung cancer surgery,"Pope Leo returns to Vatican after lung cancer surgery Doctors say they are seeing &#039;continued improvement&#039; after the US-born pope had a lump removed from his lung on Tuesday. Save Share Pope Leo has returned to the Vatican, four days after receiving surgery for lung cancer in Rome. The pope said he was feeling “much better” when asked about his health as he was driven away from the Gemelli University Hospital against cries of “holiness” from onlookers on Saturday. Leo had the lower lobe of his right lung removed on Tuesday. The surgery confirmed he had adenocarcinoma, which is the most common type of lung cancer. He is set to resume some duties on Sunday, when he greets the faithful with the Catholic Church’s traditional Angelus blessing. Doctors have said tests confirmed his “continued improvement”. Since having surgery, the pope has had a phone call with Russian President Vladimir Putin, held meetings with senior aides and celebrated mass in a makeshift hospital chapel. Leo thanked staff in the Rome hospital for the work they do every day “taking care of the sick in their fragility”, as well as for “what they had done for him”, a Vatican spokesperson said. Pope Leo’s next major engagement is a trip to Latin America in mid-November, giving him about three weeks to recover for an ambitious journey that includes a dozen flights and two helicopter trips. Leo is the first pope from the United States, hailing from Chicago. However, he has spent decades living and working across South America and is a naturalised Peruvian citizen.","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 17:37:52 +0000",https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/17/pope-leo-returns-to-vatican-after-lung-cancer-surgery?traffic_source=rss,2026-10-18 01:46:04
Al Jazeera (EN),Middle East (English),en,Trump faces bipartisan backlash over Russia sanctions waiver,Trump faces bipartisan backlash over Russia sanctions waiver,"Trump faces bipartisan backlash over Russia sanctions waiver Republicans and Democrats condemn Trump’s decision to suspend sanctions, saying it undermines support for Ukraine. Save Share United States President Donald Trump has drawn criticism from Democrats and Republicans afterwaiving sanctionstargeting Russia and countries that buy its oil and gas, days before the restrictions were due to take effect under a law he signed last month. In an executive order signed on Friday, Trump said the waiver was in the US national interest. He did not specify when the restrictions might be introduced, saying they would remain suspended until he or senior officials determined further action was appropriate. The move has been condemned by both Democrats and Republicans. Republican Senator Thom Tillis of North Carolina called the decision “beyond irresponsible”, accusing the administration of capitulating to Russian President Vladimir Putin, who launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022. “What part of enforcing a law passed 86-11 by the Senate does the President and his advisers not understand?” Tillis wrote on social media on Saturday. Republican Senator John Cornyn of Texas also criticised the move, saying the late Republican Senator Lindsey Graham of South Carolina, who championed the legislation, “would be appalled”. Representative Don Beyer, a Virginia Democrat, said Trump’s move betrayed Ukraine and disregarded Congress’s intent to punish Russia for its invasion. Democratic Senator Elizabeth Warren of Massachusetts accused Trump of “thumbing his nose” at Congress and enabling Putin to continue his war against Ukraine, urging colleagues to take acti","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 17:24:07 +0000",https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/17/trump-faces-bipartisan-backlash-over-russia-sanctions-waiver?traffic_source=rss,2026-10-18 01:46:04
