Al Jazeera (EN),Middle East (English),en,Why are Latin American countries building closer ties with Israel?,Why are Latin American countries building closer ties with Israel?,"Why are Latin American countries building closer ties with Israel? A wave of new Latin American leaders reverses measures meant to isolate Israel after its genocidal war on Gaza. Save Share When Israeli Foreign Minister Gideon Saar announced on October 8 that Colombia would bemovingits embassy to Jerusalem, he made sure to point out how important the development was for his country’s diplomatic narrative, and that it heralded warming relations with Latin American governments. “We will work hard because this is the diplomatic battle over the truth,” Saar said. “And the truth is, Jerusalem was always the capital of one nation.” Brazil, Saar went on to say, might soon join Colombia and the growing list of Latin American countries that had moved their embassies to Jerusalem, the disputed city claimed by Israelis and Palestinians as their capital. Latin America’s relationship with Israel is shifting, as right-wing parties come into power in the region and pro-Israel politicians use their support for the country to rally voters. Brazil, the largest country in the region, is in the midst of apresidential election campaignin which the relationship with Israel has become a key talking point. President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva, a left-wing ardent supporter of Palestine, is locked in a close contest with right-wing rival Senator Flavio Bolsonaro. He has sported Israeli flags at campaign rallies and played up his friendship with Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu. The pair are scheduled to take part in a run-off election after Bolsonaro performed surprisinglystronglyin the first -round, apparently raising hopes for a detente with Israeli officials like th","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 16:54:44 +0000",https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/17/why-are-latin-american-countries-building-closer-ties-with-israel?t