The Hindu,South Asia,en,Drone strikes in the Black Sea raise fears of Russian escalation to test NATO's response,Drone strikes in the Black Sea raise fears of Russian escalation to test NATO's response,"Drone strikes in the Black Sea raise fears of Russian escalation to test NATO's response The attacks in the two countries' exclusive economic zones threaten a global shipping route and have left two people dead, with at least 25 still missing. They also run the risk of drawing other countries directly into the war for Ukraine You are logged in Loading... You don’t have any Active Subscription. Subscribed with another email? Logout and Login with that one. Your active subscription(s) Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Products you've access to Additional Subscription Benefits Account Settings Need help with your subscription? Updated- October 18, 2026 06:41 am IST - Sofia (Bulgaria) Image used for representational purposes only. | Photo Credit: Getty Images/iStockphoto A string of drone attacks on at least seven cargo vessels inthe Black Seathis month, off the coasts of NATO members Romania and Bulgaria, has some members of the alliance wondering if Russian President Vladimir Putin is seeking escalation to test their support for Ukraine in its war against Moscow's full-scale invasion. Also Read |Double standards: On the Trump diesel deal with Russia The attacks in the two countries' exclusive economic zones threaten a global shipping route and have left two people dead, with at least 25 still missing. They also run the risk of drawing other countries directly into the war for Ukraine. Throughout the war, Russia has repeatedly bombarded Ukraine's Black Sea and Danube River ports, leaving Kyiv increasingly reliant on alternative routes to get its grain exports to markets in Africa and West Asia. Russian drones have repeatedly violated Romanian airspace near some of Ukraine's Danube ports. In the latest incidents, fire","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 06:41:34 +0530",https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/drone-strikes-in-the-black-sea-raise-fears-of-russian-escalation-to-test-natos-response/article71591367.ece,2026-10-18 01:49:25
The Hindu,South Asia,en,Ukraine abandons truce and attacks Russian oil refinery as Zelenskyy says he feels betrayed by Trump,Ukraine abandons truce and attacks Russian oil refinery as Zelenskyy says he feels betrayed by Trump,"Ukraine abandons truce and attacks Russian oil refinery as Zelenskyy says he feels betrayed by Trump Ukraine attacked a Russian oil refinery, abandoning a brief truce involving energy-related targets that it said Moscow did not heed You are logged in Loading... You don’t have any Active Subscription. Subscribed with another email? Logout and Login with that one. Your active subscription(s) Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Products you've access to Additional Subscription Benefits Account Settings Need help with your subscription? Updated- October 18, 2026 06:41 am IST - KYIV Ukraine's President Volodymyr Zelenskyy. File | Photo Credit: AP Ukraine attacked a Russian oil refinery on Thursday (October 16, 2026), abandoning a brief truce involving energy-related targets that it said Moscow did not heed, as Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy said he felt betrayed byU.S. President Donald Trump. The drone strike started a fire at the refinery about 300 kilometers (180 miles) southeast of Moscow in Russia’s Ryazan region, according to the Russian Defense Ministry and Ryazan Gov. Pavel Malkov. Russia’s Astra online news outlet posted what it said was a photograph of the blaze. The strike could further complicate Kyiv’s relations with Trump, which have soured again in recent days amid deepening tensions between Ukraine and Washington. Mr. Zelenskyy said he was outraged by Russia’s continuing aerial bombardment of energy infrastructure in apparent defiance of Trump’s demand on Sunday for both sides to halt those attacks. Despite going along with Mr. Trump’s demand for a truce, Mr. Zelenskyy complained in recent days that Ukra","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 06:41:19 +0530",https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/ukraine-abandons-truce-and-attacks-russian-oil-refinery-as-zelenskyy-says-he-feels-betrayed-by-trump/article71591412.ece,2026-10-18 01:49:25
The Hindu,South Asia,en,Why the GCC-EU summit was recently postponed | Explained,Why the GCC-EU summit was recently postponed | Explained,"Why the GCC-EU summit was recently postponed | Explained Economic relations, climate change, energy and research opportunities were some of the aspects of the partnership that were expected to be discussed during the summit You are logged in Loading... You don’t have any Active Subscription. Subscribed with another email? Logout and Login with that one. Your active subscription(s) Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Products you've access to Additional Subscription Benefits Account Settings Need help with your subscription? Updated- October 18, 2026 05:44 am IST The Secretary-General of the Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC) is H.E. Jasem Mohamed Al-Budaiwi. Photo credit: X/@GCCSG The story so far:The Gulf Cooperation Council and the European Union on Thursday (October 15, 2026) announced that the GCC-EU summit scheduled to be held in Riyadh on October 24 has been pushed to a later date. While new dates have not yet been announced, the GCC Secretariat has said that, along with the European Union, it will work towards establishing the appropriate conditions for convening the summit. The Gulf Cooperation Council comprises the UAE, Bahrain, Saudi Arabia, Oman, Qatar and Kuwait. This political and economic alliance of six Arabian Gulf countries had planned the summit with the 27 member states of the European Union to further trade and regional relations. Although an agreement for cooperation between the GCC and the EU go back to 1989, it was only in 2024 that a summit was first held between the representatives. Until then, a joint cooperation committee had annual meetings to strengthen the partnership. This year will mark","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 05:44:37 +0530",https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/why-the-gcc-eu-summit-was-recently-postponed-explained/article71590081.ece,2026-10-18 01:49:26
The Hindu,South Asia,en,Four killed in Indonesia battling fires causing toxic haze,Four killed in Indonesia battling fires causing toxic haze,"Four killed in Indonesia battling fires causing toxic haze The impact of the months-long fires has prompted Singapore's leader to vow legal action against any companies responsible for the blazes that have made regional air quality among the world's worst. You are logged in Loading... You don’t have any Active Subscription. Subscribed with another email? Logout and Login with that one. Your active subscription(s) Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Products you've access to Additional Subscription Benefits Account Settings Need help with your subscription? Updated- October 18, 2026 05:39 am IST - Jakarta Four people died on Indonesia's Java island fighting the blazes that have been sending toxic smoke across Southeast Asia, closing schools and fuelling a surge of respiratory complaints in the region, authorities said on Friday (October 16, 2026). The impact of the months-long fires has prompted Singapore's leader to vow legal action against any companies responsible for the blazes that have made regional air quality among the world's worst. Singapore again topped IQAir's live ranking of the world's most polluted major cities on Friday (October 16), with Kuala Lumpur second and Jakarta sixth. ""Where warranted, we will take action under Singapore's Transboundary Haze Pollution Act, and also explore joint action with other countries,"" said Singapore Prime Minister Lawrence Wong, without giving further details. Singapore has no plantations or forests of its own, but several companies with concessions in Indonesia are reportedly based in the city-state. Legal experts have cautioned that pursuing companies linked to the smoke is challenging, with inves","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 05:39:45 +0530",https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/four-killed-in-indonesia-battling-fires-causing-toxic-haze/article71591244.ece,2026-10-18 01:49:26
