Times of India,India (Mainstream),en,Tear Gas At France Protests: Police clash with crowds over rising costs; authorities fear unrest,Tear Gas At France Protests: Police clash with crowds over rising costs; authorities fear unrest,"Tear Gas At France Protests: Police clash with crowds over rising costs; authorities fear unrest <img align=""left"" border=""0"" hspace=""10"" src=""https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135012687.cms"" style=""margin-top: 3px; margin-right: 5px;"" />Nationwide protests erupted in France against soaring living costs and a decline in social welfare. Major cities like Paris and Marseille witnessed intense confrontations between law enforcement and demonstrators as tensions flared. With thousands taking to the streets, authorities are wary of possible chaos. Originating in northern suburbs where public service grievances peaked, both governmental and opposition factions are blaming each other for the unrest.",2026-10-17T17:02:24+05:30,https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/europe/tear-gas-fired-as-thousands-protest-rising-cost-of-living-across-france/articleshow/135012687.cms,2026-10-18 01:50:45
Zee News,India (Right Wing),en,"Imran Khan facing secret exile deal like Nawaz Sharif and Benazir Bhutto, claims opposition leader","Imran Khan facing secret exile deal like Nawaz Sharif and Benazir Bhutto, claims opposition leader","Imran Khan facing secret exile deal like Nawaz Sharif and Benazir Bhutto, claims opposition leader The allegation has come at a time when Imran Khan's Pakistan Tehreek-e-Insaf (PTI) party is fighting to regain control of Khyber Pakhtunkhwa, a province in northwestern Pakistan along the Afghan border. Subhrajit is a journalist with over three years of newsroom experience, covering national and international geopolitics with a sharp focus on India–China–Pakistan affairs. He has spent more than two years with India’s largest television news agency, ANI, and over a year with DD News. He holds a Master’s degree in Mass Communication. He can be reached atsubhrajit.roy@india.com Imran Khan is in prison, and his party has now lost the only provincial government it controlled in Pakistan. As his supporters fight Islamabad’s takeover of Khyber Pakhtunkhwa, opposition leader Mahmood Khan Achakzai has alleged that Khan is being pressed to accept an exile-style deal. The claim comes before a court hearing on whether the takeover itself was lawful. Also Read:Imran Khan moves Bombay High Court against 'Leave India' notice over anti-CEC protest Opposition leader Achakzai made the claim at a news conference at the Peshawar High Court on Friday. He alleged that Imran Khan was being pressured to accept a deal similar to those involving former prime ministers Nawaz Sharif and Benazir Bhutto, who spent years abroad after leaving office. Achakzai is the leader of the opposition in Pakistan’s National Assembly, the lower house of Parliament. He also heads the Pakhtunkhwa Milli Awami Party (PkMAP) and an alliance of opposition parties called Tehreek-i-Tahafuz-Ayin-i-Pakistan (TTAP). Achakzai did not explain who was pressuring Khan or disclose the terms of any proposed agreement. There has been n","Saturday, October 17, 2026, 20:04  +0530",https://zeenews.india.com/world/imran-khan-last-stronghold-pti-exile-claim-pakistan-3076271.html,2026-10-18 01:50:45
Zee News,India (Right Wing),en,Why did Katie Zacharia leave as White House Press Secretary after just five days?,Why did Katie Zacharia leave as White House Press Secretary after just five days?,"Why did Katie Zacharia leave as White House Press Secretary after just five days? Katie Zacharia has left her role as White House press secretary after just five days, praising Donald Trump as “kind and thoughtful” amid conflicting reports over whether she was fired or moved to a new role. The bureau represents a collective of experienced journalists, editors, and correspondents who work across television, digital, and social platforms to deliver timely, accurate, and wide-ranging news coverage. Zee Media Bureau's coverage spans politics, business, economy, technology, health, entertainment, and global affairs. The bureau follows a collaborative newsroom model, where stories are researched, verified, and published by a team. This ensures speed, editorial consistency, and adherence to journalistic standards, particularly for breaking news and developing stories. Katie Zacharia has left her position as White House press secretary after just five days, in what US media reports describe as the shortest tenure in the role’s history. Confirming her departure, Zacharia praised US President Donald Trump, calling him “kind and thoughtful” and saying it had been an honour to work for him. “President Trump is a kind, wonderful, gracious, thoughtful, enthusiastic, and hardworking man. I wish more Americans appreciated him. No one will ever make America great the way he has and continues to,” Zacharia wrote in a post on X, adding, “It was an honour to work for him."" Her departure was reported on Friday, with US media offering differing accounts of the circumstances. CNN, citing advisers to the President, reported that Trump had fired her. NBC News, quoting a senior administration official, said she was “transitioning into a new role” that would be announced later. No succes","Saturday, October 17, 2026, 18:11  +0530",https://zeenews.india.com/world/why-did-katie-zacharia-leave-as-white-house-press-secretary-after-just-five-days-3076269.html,2026-10-18 01:50:45
Zee News,India (Right Wing),en,"‘Arbitrary, unverified reports make lose credibility’: India rejects UN expert’s claims on youth protests","‘Arbitrary, unverified reports make lose credibility’: India rejects UN expert’s claims on youth protests","‘Arbitrary, unverified reports make lose credibility’: India rejects UN expert’s claims on youth protests Speaking at a meeting of the UN General Assembly’s Third Committee in New York on Friday, Indian diplomat Anuj Swarup said reports based on unverifiable information were damaging the credibility of the world body and its institutions. The bureau represents a collective of experienced journalists, editors, and correspondents who work across television, digital, and social platforms to deliver timely, accurate, and wide-ranging news coverage. Zee Media Bureau's coverage spans politics, business, economy, technology, health, entertainment, and global affairs. The bureau follows a collaborative newsroom model, where stories are researched, verified, and published by a team. This ensures speed, editorial consistency, and adherence to journalistic standards, particularly for breaking news and developing stories. India has strongly rejected a report by a United Nations special rapporteur on freedom of peaceful assembly and association, accusing the expert of relying on anonymous and unverified claims to make sweeping allegations about the country’s young population. Speaking at a meeting of the UN General Assembly’s Third Committee in New York on Friday, Indian diplomat Anuj Swarup said reports based on unverifiable information were damaging the credibility of the world body and its institutions. “A sweeping charge against the world's largest youth population, resting on anonymous and unverified claims is not evidence,” Swarup said, taking exception to the report by Special Rapporteur Gina Romero on the Rights to Freedom of Peaceful Assembly and of Association. “This level of arbitrary report writing is precisely the reason that the United Nations and its institutions have lost credibility,” he added. Describing ","Saturday, October 17, 2026, 16:19  +0530",https://zeenews.india.com/india/arbitrary-unverified-reports-make-lose-credibility-india-rejects-un-expert-s-claims-on-youth-protests-3076264.html,2026-10-18 01:50:46
Zee News,India (Right Wing),en,Trump waives new Russia sanctions; Putin’s envoy calls move ‘historic moment’,Trump waives new Russia sanctions; Putin’s envoy calls move ‘historic moment’,"Trump waives new Russia sanctions; Putin’s envoy calls move ‘historic moment’ His remarks came hours after Trump signed an executive order waiving key sanctions and tariffs targeting Russia, days before the measures were due to take effect under legislation he signed last month. The restrictions included duties on Russian goods and penalties for countries buying Russian oil and gas. The bureau represents a collective of experienced journalists, editors, and correspondents who work across television, digital, and social platforms to deliver timely, accurate, and wide-ranging news coverage. Zee Media Bureau's coverage spans politics, business, economy, technology, health, entertainment, and global affairs. The bureau follows a collaborative newsroom model, where stories are researched, verified, and published by a team. This ensures speed, editorial consistency, and adherence to journalistic standards, particularly for breaking news and developing stories. Russian presidential envoy Kirill Dmitriev on Saturday described US President Donald Trump’s decision to waive a new package of Russia-related sanctions as a “historic moment”, saying Washington should prioritise dialogue and cooperation over confrontation with Moscow. “The Trump administration’s suspension of the Graham Act is an important and truly historic moment,” Dmitriev, an envoy for Russian President Vladimir Putin, wrote on X. His remarks came hours after Trump signed an executive order waiving key sanctions and tariffs targeting Russia, days before the measures were due to take effect under legislation he signed last month. The restrictions included duties on Russian goods and penalties for countries buying Russian oil and gas. “Instead of sanctions and confrontation, we need dialogue, mutually beneficial cooperation, and shared responsibility for global stability,” Dmitriev added. Trump Waives","Saturday, October 17, 2026, 15:59  +0530",https://zeenews.india.com/world/trump-waives-new-russia-sanctions-putin-s-envoy-calls-move-historic-moment-3076259.html,2026-10-18 01:50:46
Zee News,India (Right Wing),en,From hijabs to high kicks: Inside Iran's secretive all-female Ninja academy,From hijabs to high kicks: Inside Iran's secretive all-female Ninja academy,"From hijabs to high kicks: Inside Iran's secretive all-female Ninja academy Iran's Kunoichi ninja school in Tehran has trained over 30,000 women in traditional ninjutsu, self-defense, and acrobatic stunts since its founding in 1989. With over 11 years of experience in covering breaking news across national and international events, I bring a strong, fast-paced journalistic approach to every story. I am deeply passionate about real-time reporting, live blogs, and on-ground coverage that captures unfolding events with accuracy and impact. With more than 8 years of experience leading dynamic news teams, I have worked with some of India’s biggest media organisations, including India Today, India TV, ABP, and others. My career spans coverage of major political, social, and global developments. I am also UGC NET qualified, adding academic strength to my professional expertise. The Kunoichi school of ninjas in Iran has trained more than 30,000 women in advanced martial arts, acrobatics, and combat techniques. Established in 1989 by Akbar Faraji as the coach of the school and currently running under the National Martial Arts Federation, the school endows Iranian women with top-notch self-defense skills, agility, and discipline. Due to rigorous public exhibitions and viral clips on social media, the institution is getting much attention worldwide for the strong movement of women being active in athletics. The program is very challenging, as it involves learning acrobatic tricks, working with a staff, and coordination exercises that develop explosive power and reflexes. Ninjas in Tehran, Iran Around 30,000 women have graduated from Iran’s Kunoichi ninja school since 1989. https://t.co/WlEXabQ4SJ Acrobatic tricks: ","Saturday, October 17, 2026, 15:06  +0530",https://zeenews.india.com/world/iran-kunoichi-ninja-women-warriors-martial-arts-academy-3076255.html,2026-10-18 01:50:46
Zee News,India (Right Wing),en,5.7 Magnitude earthquake shakes Khuzdar in Pakistan's Balochistan: Shallow 10 km depth causes severe shaking,5.7 Magnitude earthquake shakes Khuzdar in Pakistan's Balochistan: Shallow 10 km depth causes severe shaking,"5.7 Magnitude earthquake shakes Khuzdar in Pakistan's Balochistan: Shallow 10 km depth causes severe shaking Centered 48 kilometers west-northwest of Khuzdar at a focal depth of just 10 kilometers, the tremors triggered widespread shaking across nearby districts including Kharan and Kalat. With over 11 years of experience in covering breaking news across national and international events, I bring a strong, fast-paced journalistic approach to every story. I am deeply passionate about real-time reporting, live blogs, and on-ground coverage that captures unfolding events with accuracy and impact. With more than 8 years of experience leading dynamic news teams, I have worked with some of India’s biggest media organisations, including India Today, India TV, ABP, and others. My career spans coverage of major political, social, and global developments. I am also UGC NET qualified, adding academic strength to my professional expertise. A powerful earthquake, measuring 5.7 on the Richter scale, occurred in Balochistan, Pakistan, on Saturday morning, October 17, 2026, around 10:06 AM. The Pakistan Meteorological Department (PMD) data identified the epicenter approximately 48 kilometers west-northwest of Khuzdar at 28.02°N 66.20°E. The quake occurred at only 10 kilometers depth, emitting enormous mechanical energy close to the surface and leading to considerable ground shaking in Khuzdar, Kharan, and Kalat. Quakes taking place at shallower focal depths (under 70 kilometers) transfer much more vibrational energy directly to the surface than do the deep-seated ones. Seismic waves traveled to Khuzdar's downtown in an estimated 14 seconds since the break of a fault. As deep quakes lose force while transferring their vibrations upward, often 10 kilometers is enough for sharp s","Saturday, October 17, 2026, 11:25  +0530",https://zeenews.india.com/world/balochistan-khuzdar-earthquake-magnitude-5-7-depth-10km-3076241.html,2026-10-18 01:50:47
Zee News,India (Right Wing),en,"Trump suspends prospective 100% tariffs on Russian oil imports, granting major relief to India","Trump suspends prospective 100% tariffs on Russian oil imports, granting major relief to India","Trump suspends prospective 100% tariffs on Russian oil imports, granting major relief to India US President Donald Trump officially issued an executive waiver under the Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act, putting prospective tariffs of up to 100% on hold for major buyers of Russian crude, including India. With over 11 years of experience in covering breaking news across national and international events, I bring a strong, fast-paced journalistic approach to every story. I am deeply passionate about real-time reporting, live blogs, and on-ground coverage that captures unfolding events with accuracy and impact. With more than 8 years of experience leading dynamic news teams, I have worked with some of India’s biggest media organisations, including India Today, India TV, ABP, and others. My career spans coverage of major political, social, and global developments. I am also UGC NET qualified, adding academic strength to my professional expertise. US President Donald Trump officially exempted the imposition of heavy secondary sanctions on foreign consumers of Russian energy, one month after he signed the Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026 (SRIA) into law. Trump's executive waiver stops the prospect of up to 100% duties on goods coming from the world's five largest buyers of Russian crude oil and natural gas, giving immediate relief to major trading partners such as India and China. Trump acted on his executive powers under Section 115 of the Graham Act, which allows the president to waive sanctions, tariffs, and trade bans if they are deemed vital for the country's national security and economic stability. ""I have submitted to the Congress a certification that the issuance of such a waiver is in the national interests of the United States,"" Trump stated in t","Saturday, October 17, 2026, 09:35  +0530",https://zeenews.india.com/india/trump-suspends-tariffs-russian-oil-imports-india-relief-3076235.html,2026-10-18 01:50:47
Zee News,India (Right Wing),en,"Saudi Arabia should accept Israel’s help against Houthis, Abraham Accords deal possible, says Trump","Saudi Arabia should accept Israel’s help against Houthis, Abraham Accords deal possible, says Trump","Saudi Arabia should accept Israel’s help against Houthis, Abraham Accords deal possible, says Trump Israel and Saudi Arabia were considered close to reaching an agreement to normalise relations before Hamas’s October 7, 2023, attack. However, Riyadh has consistently maintained that normalisation with Israel would require a clear pathway towards Palestinian statehood. The bureau represents a collective of experienced journalists, editors, and correspondents who work across television, digital, and social platforms to deliver timely, accurate, and wide-ranging news coverage. Zee Media Bureau's coverage spans politics, business, economy, technology, health, entertainment, and global affairs. The bureau follows a collaborative newsroom model, where stories are researched, verified, and published by a team. This ensures speed, editorial consistency, and adherence to journalistic standards, particularly for breaking news and developing stories. US President Donald Trump has urged Saudi Arabia to accept Israel’s offer of assistance in its fight against Houthi rebels, describing Israeli support as “very helpful” amid escalating hostilities in the region. Asked by reporters at the White House ahead of a rally in Georgia whether Saudi Arabia should accept Israel’s offer, Trump replied, “Yeah, I do, sure. Israel has been very helpful to Saudi Arabia”. Trump also suggested that Saudi Arabia could join the Abraham Accords, the 2020 agreements aimed at normalising relations between Israel and several Arab countries, saying the prospect was “maybe more than ever”. Asked whether a deal with Saudi Arabia under the framework was back on the table, Trump said, “Yeah, I think so. Yeah, I think so. Maybe more than ever. The Abraham Accords is one of our great achievements. And, yeah, I would say maybe more than ever. But Saudi Arabia is very happy to have Isr","Saturday, October 17, 2026, 06:36  +0530",https://zeenews.india.com/world/saudi-arabia-should-accept-israel-s-help-against-houthis-abraham-accords-deal-possible-says-trump-3076231.html,2026-10-18 01:50:47
Zee News,India (Right Wing),en,Emirati Royal Mahrah Al Nahyan sentenced in London over cocaine collapse and child neglect,Emirati Royal Mahrah Al Nahyan sentenced in London over cocaine collapse and child neglect,"Emirati Royal Mahrah Al Nahyan sentenced in London over cocaine collapse and child neglect Mahrah Al Nahyan, a 32-year-old Emirati royal related to Abu Dhabi's ruling family by marriage, was sentenced to a two-year community order and fined £6,114 by Southwark Crown Court after pleading guilty to child neglect. With over 11 years of experience in covering breaking news across national and international events, I bring a strong, fast-paced journalistic approach to every story. I am deeply passionate about real-time reporting, live blogs, and on-ground coverage that captures unfolding events with accuracy and impact. With more than 8 years of experience leading dynamic news teams, I have worked with some of India’s biggest media organisations, including India Today, India TV, ABP, and others. My career spans coverage of major political, social, and global developments. I am also UGC NET qualified, adding academic strength to my professional expertise. Mahrah Al Nahyan, a 32-year-old Emirati royal related by marriage to Abu Dhabi’s ruling family, was sentenced to a two-year community order after pleading guilty to child neglect at Southwark Crown Court. The conviction follows a June incident where London bystanders found her passed out cold against a pavement railing while a barefoot three-year-old child wandered near oncoming traffic. Prosecutor Rio Pahlavanpour told the court that concerned pedestrians stepped in after spotting the barefoot toddler playing with a scooter perilously close to moving vehicles in west London. Nearby, Al Nahyan and an unidentified man lay unconscious on the pavement for approximately 30 minutes. Court records revealed that when emergency workers revived Al Nahyan, she dismissed bystanders' concerns, asking: ""This is not a slaughterhouse, why do you care s","Friday, October 16, 2026, 14:45  +0530",https://zeenews.india.com/world/emirati-royal-mahrah-al-nahyan-pleads-guilty-child-neglect-london-3076187.html,2026-10-18 01:50:47
Zee News,India (Right Wing),en,Magnitude 7.0 earthquake hits Russia's Kamchatka Peninsula: No tsunami threat reported,Magnitude 7.0 earthquake hits Russia's Kamchatka Peninsula: No tsunami threat reported,"Magnitude 7.0 earthquake hits Russia's Kamchatka Peninsula: No tsunami threat reported A powerful magnitude 7.0 earthquake struck Russia's Kamchatka Peninsula at a depth of 140 kilometers, centered 123 km southwest of Vilyuchinsk. No tsunami warnings were issued, and no immediate casualties or major damages were reported following the deep seismic event. With over 11 years of experience in covering breaking news across national and international events, I bring a strong, fast-paced journalistic approach to every story. I am deeply passionate about real-time reporting, live blogs, and on-ground coverage that captures unfolding events with accuracy and impact. With more than 8 years of experience leading dynamic news teams, I have worked with some of India’s biggest media organisations, including India Today, India TV, ABP, and others. My career spans coverage of major political, social, and global developments. I am also UGC NET qualified, adding academic strength to my professional expertise. A powerful magnitude 7.0 earthquake struck off the coast of Russia's Kamchatka Peninsula at 3:35 PM local time. The European-Mediterranean Seismological Centre (EMSC) confirmed the epicenter was located approximately 123 kilometers southwest of Vilyuchinsk in Russia's Far East, at a depth of 140 kilometers. Due to the significant depth of the focus, authorities issued no tsunami warnings, and emergency agencies reported no immediate casualties or severe structural damage. The US National Tsunami Warning Center evaluated the seismic data immediately following the tremors and confirmed zero tsunami danger for surrounding coastal territories. Deep-focus earthquakes, while widely felt across large surface areas, typically displace less sea floor than shallow quakes, significantly lowering the risk of destructive ocean waves. Local emergency ","Friday, October 16, 2026, 10:40  +0530",https://zeenews.india.com/world/russia-kamchatka-peninsula-magnitude-7-earthquake-japan-alert-3076167.html,2026-10-18 01:50:47
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Trump govt plans tougher US worksite crackdown, targets employers hiring undocumented workers","Trump govt plans tougher US worksite crackdown, targets employers hiring undocumented workers","Trump govt plans tougher US worksite crackdown, targets employers hiring undocumented workers A Homeland Security Investigations draft would expand the focus of workplace immigration enforcement beyond unauthorized workers to employers. US border czar Tom Homan has signaled tougher workplaceimmigrationenforcement, saying a plan prepared by Homeland Security Investigations (HSI) has cleared an initial stage and could soon reach Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin for review. The proposed plan would target not only unauthorizedworkersbut also employers who knowingly hire them and individuals who use fraudulent identification documents, Homan said. “I just saw the draft from HSI last week,” Homan said, adding that Mullin would probably review it within the following week or the week after. He expressed support for the proposed measures, saying workplace enforcement was necessary to discourage illegalemployment. Homan's remarks suggest theTrumpadministration is looking to expand accountability beyond workers found at job sites. ALSO READ:More US universities may face J-1 visa probes as nine, including Harvard, face scrutiny: Trump govt Homan said the proposed approach would focus onemployers who knowingly hire workers without legal authorization, alongside people who use fraudulent identification to misuse Social Security numbers. “It's not just about arresting aliens. It's about holding employers accountable,” he said, arguing that businesses should not be allowed to continue employing unauthorized workers. The approach reflects a broader enforcement strategy in which federal authorities investigate both unauthorized employment and potential violations by businesses. ExistingUSlaw requires employers to","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 06:33:54 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/trump-govt-plans-tougher-us-worksite-crackdown-targets-employers-hiring-undocumented-workers-101792284801259.html,2026-10-18 01:50:47
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Republican lawmaker hits out at Trump over Russia sanctions waiver, says he is 'prolonging the war, not ending it'","Republican lawmaker hits out at Trump over Russia sanctions waiver, says he is 'prolonging the war, not ending it'","Republican lawmaker hits out at Trump over Russia sanctions waiver, says he is 'prolonging the war, not ending it' Trump has blocked key parts of a new Russia sanctions law, and leaders in both parties and Ukraine’s Zelensky have criticized the move. US PresidentDonald Trumphas blocked key parts of aRussiasanctions law just two days before they were due to take effect. He signed an executive order on Friday evening waiving key sanctions and tariffs meant to pressure Russia to end its war inUkraine, which began in February 2022. The move comes as a row with Ukrainian PresidentVolodymyr Zelenskydeepens. The decision drew criticism from lawmakers in both parties and from Zelensky, while a senior Russian official praised it. As perNewsweek, Trump signed the Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026 into law on September 18. The Friday executive order waives several sanctions provisions and extra tariffs allowed under the law. The order reads: “I am hereby waiving the application of such sanctions, restrictions, and duties provided for in the Graham Act. I have submitted to the Congress a certification that the issuance of such a waiver at this time is in the national interests of the United States.” According to reports, the law allows a 500 percent tariff on goods imported from Russia, subject to statutory exceptions. It also allows possible 100 percent tariffs on goods from the five largest importers of Russian-origin crude oil and natural gas if they make new purchases on or after October 18. It also provides for 100 percent tariffs on goods from the five leading facilitators of Russian oil sanctions evasion. The waiver means these extra duties, which were set to start Sunday, are not automatically imposed ","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 05:56:38 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/republican-lawmaker-hits-out-at-trump-over-russia-sanctions-waiver-says-he-is-prolonging-the-war-not-ending-it-101792278953171.html,2026-10-18 01:50:47
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Shooting near Augusta's Laney High School leaves 3 injured,Shooting near Augusta's Laney High School leaves 3 injured,"Shooting near Augusta's Laney High School leaves 3 injured Three people were shot in the tailgate area of Laney High School in Augusta, and they are in hospital. Three people wereshotnear Laney High School inAugusta,Georgia, on October 17. As per Newsnation, the Richmond County Sheriff’s Office responded to reports of ashootingat the school in Augusta, Georgia, on Saturday According to the Post and Courier, deputies responded to “the tailgate area” of Laney High School at 1339 Laney Walker Blvd. The incident happened in the school’s tailgating area. The school had hosted its homecoming football game against Josey. Also read: 'Doomsday Glacier' melting faster than expected, study warns As perNews Nation, investigators said at least three people had gunshot wounds. They were taken to local hospitals for treatment. According to a release by Sgt Walter McNeil at 7:04pm, the current conditions of the three were not available. Also read:Oregon school shooter's mother Shauntai May Nickerson says son was a 'good boy' There is no word yet on a suspect. The release says: “Investigators are still working to piece together the circumstances surrounding the incident."" Here is the statement from Haley Lacuesta cited byWRDW: We are deeply concerned by the shooting that occurred in the parking lot at Lucy C. Laney High School following today’s homecoming football game. Our thoughts are with those injured. Their families and everyone affected by this incident. Based on the information we have at this time, the incident involved adults, and no students were involved. We can confirm that all student athletes, cheerleaders, band members, and students par","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 05:40:15 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/shooting-near-laney-high-school-in-augusta-georgia-leaves-3-injured-101792281572022.html,2026-10-18 01:50:48
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,'Ran for my life': Witnesses recall truck striking people at Wichita Stryker Sports Complex,'Ran for my life': Witnesses recall truck striking people at Wichita Stryker Sports Complex,"'Ran for my life': Witnesses recall truck striking people at Wichita Stryker Sports Complex Witnesses recount a truck striking a crowd at Wichita’s Stryker Sports Complex, leaving two dead, including a child, and several injured. Witnesses have described the frightening moments when a truck drove through youth soccer fields at the Stryker Sports Complex in Wichita,Kansas, on Saturday, October 17. Two people, including a child, were killed and several others were injured, police said. People commenting during a YouTube livestream said the truck came close to players before stopping near another field. A witness quoted by local media said the vehicle travelled around 100 to 200 yards through the fields before stopping. The driver was taken into custody, and police said there was no further threat to the public. The circumstances surrounding the incident remain under investigation. One person who identified themselves as a goalkeeper at the complex described how close the vehicle came to them in comments during a YouTube livestream by12newskwch. “The car was 30 feet from me. It started to turn and tried to hit me,” one person who identified themselves as a goalkeeper wrote in a YouTube livestream chat. The same commenter added, “Me too it stopped right before my field I heard screams and honks and like thuds and ran for my life.” Another commenter wrote, “Because my dad and the other teams dads yanked the doors open and were trying to stop them he stopped right before me.” A third commenter, who said they had heard from a friend at the scene, wrote, “I heard from my friend who was there, there are little entrances in gates, the truck went through those gates and plowed through the fields about 100-200 ya","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 05:37:58 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/ran-for-my-life-witnesses-recall-truck-striking-people-at-wichita-stryker-sports-complex-101792279815304.html,2026-10-18 01:50:48
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Trump hosts Katie Zacharia's family at Oval Office, surprises children with gifts","Trump hosts Katie Zacharia's family at Oval Office, surprises children with gifts","Trump hosts Katie Zacharia's family at Oval Office, surprises children with gifts Katie Zacharia shared an Oval Office family photo, thanking Trump for gifts to her children on her son’s birthday. Katie Zacharia thanked US PresidentDonald Trumpfor hosting her family in the Oval Office and giving gifts to her children on her son’s birthday, days after she left her role as White House press secretary. Zacharia shared the message onXon Saturday, October 17, alongside a photograph of herself with Trump and her family. Her post came after her brief tenure ended amid criticism of her first press briefing and questions about her professional credentials. “Thank you, President Trump, for hosting my family and me in the Oval Office and for the generous gifts you gave my children on my son’s birthday. It was an unforgettable honor,” she wrote. The post highlighted a family visit to the Oval Office and came shortly after Trump publicly suggested that she reconsider staying in the role. Also Read:'It was an honor to work for Trump': Katie Zacharia after quitting White House job in 5 days In another post, Zacharia had expressed strong support for Trump, praising his leadership and describing her time working for him as an honour. “President Trump is a kind, wonderful, gracious, thoughtful, enthusiastic, and hardworking man. I wish more Americans appreciated him. No one will ever make America great the way he has and continues to. It was an honor to work for him,” she wrote. Her comments came as her sudden departure came to light following a turbulent first week as theWhite House’schief spokesperson. Zacharia’s first press briefing took place on October 13 and lasted about 14 minute","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 04:50:33 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/trump-hosts-katie-zacharias-family-at-oval-office-surprises-children-with-gifts-101792275054978.html,2026-10-18 01:50:48
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Wichita Stryker Sports Complex: What we know about the incident that left multiple people injured,Wichita Stryker Sports Complex: What we know about the incident that left multiple people injured,"Wichita Stryker Sports Complex: What we know about the incident that left multiple people injured According to local news outlet KWCH, a witness reported that a black pickup truck drove through youth soccer fields and struck multiple people. Update (6:34 p.m. EDT): A vehicle drove onto a youth soccer field at the Stryker Sports Complex in Wichita, Kansas, on Saturday, killing a child and an adult, police said. Several others were injured in the incident. The truck's driver was taken into custody, authorities said. Also Read:GoFundMe launched for 7-year-old Saylor Graham after Oregon school shooting tragedy Update (5:47 p.m. EDT): Wichita police have confirmed a ""mass casualty incident"" at the Stryker Sports Complex in Wichita, Kansas, after reports that a vehicle struck multiple people on the soccer fields. ""WPD is investigating a mass casualty incident involving a vehicle. There is no further ongoing threat at this time. We will provide further updates when we have additional information,"" Wichita police said in a post on X. The number of victims has not been confirmed. Also Read:Shooting at NYC subway station leaves man critically injured Initial report: Multiple people were reportedly injured in an incident at the Stryker Sports Complex in Wichita, Kansas, on Saturday. According to local news outletKWCH, a witness reported that a black pickup truck drove through youth soccer fields and struck multiple people. Emergency responders were reportedly providing medical assistance at the scene. As of the latest report available, officials had not confirmed the full number of injured people or the severity of their injuries. The inaugural KSHSAA Girls Flag Football State Championship, scheduled to take place at the St","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 02:57:26 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/wichita-stryker-sports-complex-what-we-know-about-the-incident-that-left-multiple-people-injured-101792272050744.html,2026-10-18 01:50:48
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Search continues after boat capsizes off Baja California coast. 16 rescued, but others missing","Search continues after boat capsizes off Baja California coast. 16 rescued, but others missing","Search continues after boat capsizes off Baja California coast. 16 rescued, but others missing Search continues after boat capsizes off Baja California coast. 16 rescued, but others missing Authorities are continuing the search for 11 people believed to be missing after a boat departing from Mexico capsized off the coast of Baja California. 16 Mexican nationals were already rescued on Friday, including one who was treated for shark bites. The U.S. Coast Guard and Navy are searching the waters using multiple aircraft and boats, along with assistance from the Mexican Navy. An additional helicopter will be deployed later today, according to the Coast Guard. The boat capsized in an area frequented by smugglers and did not happen in U.S. waters, according to the Coast Guard. Survivors told officials there were 27 people on board. “We were able to help, and that’s why we launched our assets,"" said Petty Officer Charlie Valor on Saturday. The region is popular with fishing and leisure boats, but fatal attempts to transport migrants from Mexico to the U.S. have occurred in the past. The Mexican consulate in San Diego said all those rescued were Mexican nationals. The Coast Guard said Customs and Border Protection was investigating and interviewing the survivors, but the agency did not immediately respond to requests for updates. If survivors were witnesses to a crime, they may be eligible for humanitarian parole and permitted to remain in the country for a limited period. Rescuers were searching the area with helicopters, a fixed-wing plane, a cutter and a Navy combat ship on Saturday. The efforts remain ongoing. A mayday call was transmitted from someone on the boat ar","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 02:38:16 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/search-continues-after-boat-capsizes-off-baja-california-coast-16-rescued-but-others-missing-101792271296590.html,2026-10-18 01:50:48
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Fisherman, 71, killed by 12-foot alligator alongside Florida lake","Fisherman, 71, killed by 12-foot alligator alongside Florida lake","Fisherman, 71, killed by 12-foot alligator alongside Florida lake Fisherman, 71, killed by 12-foot alligator alongside Florida lake A 71-year-old man fishing on the shore of a Florida lake was killed Friday by a nearly 12-foot alligator, the second fatal attack reported this year. Steven Hamilton of Winter Garden was standing on the shore of Lake Apopka at Dr. Bradford Memorial Park, 15 miles northwest of Orlando, when he was attacked around 5 p.m., the Winter Garden Police Department said. The presence of other active alligators in the water complicated any chance of rescuing Hamilton, whose body was recovered about two hours later, Winter Garden Fire Chief Jose Gainzatold FOX35. Police Capt. Michael Mason said authorities were not alerted in advance that an alligator was near the lake's shore, possibly because it “has tons of alligators in it.” He said police launched a joint investigation with the Florida Fish & Wildlife Conservation Commission, whose trappers captured and killed the alligator. Mason reminded people to be aware of their surroundings and respect wildlife. “Keep your distance,” he said. “It looks like it’s docile, it looks like your Florida emblem, but it can be dangerous.” After a 31-year-old Oviedo woman, Brittany Clark, was killed by a 13-foot alligator near her home in June, Republican U.S. Rep. Greg Steube introduced a resolution calling for the Comptroller General and U.S. Department of the Interior to study the impacts of the American alligator's listing as “threatened due to similarity of appearance” on Florida's ability to remove nuisance alligators from residential and commerci","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 02:35:15 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/fisherman-71-killed-by-12-foot-alligator-alongside-florida-lake-101792271116335.html,2026-10-18 01:50:48
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Prominent Guatemalan anti-corruption lawyer killed in shooting,Prominent Guatemalan anti-corruption lawyer killed in shooting,"Prominent Guatemalan anti-corruption lawyer killed in shooting GUATEMALA-VIOLENCE/ (PIX, TV):Prominent Guatemalan anti-corruption lawyer killed in shooting * Police detain two men after attack and seize carbine-style firearm and motorcycle * President Arevalo says he orders government to coordinate with authorities to solve crime * Solorzano Foppa led Guatemala's tax authority from 2016 to 2018 By Sofia Menchu Oct 17 - Guatemalan lawyer Juan Francisco Solorzano Foppa, known for his high-profile fight against corruption in the Central American country, died after being shot on Saturday by two men while visiting a sports center in Guatemala City, a crime condemned by President Bernardo Arevalo. Solorzano Foppa was a 43-year-old former prosecutor and anti-corruption lawyer who denounced wrongdoing by some of Guatemala's most powerful politicians, business leaders and companies, and in 2023 briefly joined the defense team of a prominent journalist. Shortly after the attack, Guatemalan police reported that they had detained two men and seized a carbine-style firearm and a motorcycle. According to the police, the arrests and seizure of the weapon were in connection with the case. ""I strongly condemn the cowardly murder of former prosecutor Juan Francisco Solorzano Foppa. Both the direct perpetrators and those who ordered the crime must face the full weight of the law,"" Arevalo said on his official X account. He added that he had instructed his government to coordinate with the relevant authorities to solve the crime. ""Those who believe that terror can intimidate the people of Guatemala and the government I lead are mistaken. In honor ","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 02:11:52 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/prominent-guatemalan-anti-corruption-lawyer-killed-in-shooting-101792269712542.html,2026-10-18 01:50:48
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"France protests: Looting, fires and vandalism reported in Paris, Lyon and Limoges","France protests: Looting, fires and vandalism reported in Paris, Lyon and Limoges","France protests: Looting, fires and vandalism reported in Paris, Lyon and Limoges Protests over rising living costs turned violent in France on Saturday, with fires, looting and about 400 arrests. Violent clashes broke out between police andprotestersinParisand otherFrenchcities on Saturday, as huge crowds marched against rising living costs. According to AFP, around 550,000 people took part, according to the interior ministry, while the CGT union said 800,000 turned out. About 400 arrests were made. According to AFP, police used tear gas and water cannon against masked protesters in Paris and other cities. As per the report, some protesters threw objects and aimed fireworks at police. Several parked cars were burned in the capital, and smoke could be seen over the center. Protesters set a tourist office on fire in the center of Lyon. They also broke into town halls in Marseille and Lille, and into a police compound in Bordeaux, and damaged the buildings before leaving. In Limoges, flames reached the roof of the town hall after a group attacked it following protests. Dozens of shops were looted in Paris, Marseille, Nice,Bordeauxand Lille. An Apple store in Lille was stripped bare. As per AFP, more than 300 protesters tried to walk up the Champs-Elysees, which is home to flagship luxury stores. Police fired tear gas to block them. Paris mayor Emmanuel Gregoire said “entire streets have been devastated” in the capital. Also read:Zelensky cries 'betrayal' again after his team exposes his anti-west ‘lie’ According to a police source who spoke to AFP, a male protester in the southern city of Saint-Etienne had his hand blown off by a police crowd-control grenade. This follow","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 02:06:23 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/france-protests-looting-fires-and-vandalism-reported-in-paris-lyon-and-limoges-101792264210664.html,2026-10-18 01:50:48
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Lebanon president asks French FM for help preventing security vacuum in south,Lebanon president asks French FM for help preventing security vacuum in south,"Lebanon president asks French FM for help preventing security vacuum in south Lebanon president asks French FM for help preventing security vacuum in south Lebanese President Joseph Aoun met visiting French Foreign Minister Jean-Noel Barrot on Saturday, saying he would welcome any effort to prevent a security vacuum in the south if UN peacekeepers withdraw as planned at the end of the year. Barrot is in Beirut for a two-day visit to discuss ""mechanisms for an international presence"" in the south the scene of repeated conflicts between Israel and Hezbollah after the mandate of the United Nations Interim Force in Lebanon expires. In a statement, Aoun reiterated Lebanon's desire to extend UNIFIL's mandate, but added, ""if this proves impossible, Lebanon welcomes any arrangement that ensures an international presence in the south, prevents a vacuum, and avoids placing the Lebanese army in direct contact with the Israeli army"". Israel still occupies a band of territory in southern Lebanon after their latest fight with Hezbollah, sparked by rocket attacks by the group in March at the outset of the Middle East war. Under pressure from the United States, Israel's key ally, the UN Security Ceouncil voted in August 2025 to end UNIFIL's mandate on December 31, 2026. France and Italy announced in June that they intended to establish a multinational force following the end of UNIFIL's mission. Before Barrot's arrival in Beirut, the French foreign ministry said France was ""ready to fully play its part"" in preventing a security vacuum and supporting the Lebanese army's redeployment to the south. Israel and Lebanon struck a framework agreement i","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 01:32:14 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/lebanon-president-asks-french-fm-for-help-preventing-security-vacuum-in-south-101792267335245.html,2026-10-18 01:50:49
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Ukrainian foreign minister says Russian-held town is a 'death trap' with starving residents,Ukrainian foreign minister says Russian-held town is a 'death trap' with starving residents,"Ukrainian foreign minister says Russian-held town is a 'death trap' with starving residents UKRAINE-CRISIS/OLESHKY (PIX):Ukrainian foreign minister says Russian-held town is a 'death trap' with starving residents Oct 17 - Ukrainian Foreign Minister Andrii Sybiha said on Saturday that Russian occupying forces were turning the Ukrainian town of Oleshky into a ""death trap"" gripped by starvation, and urged international organisations to help evacuate its residents. Sybiha made his comments on X a day after Russian President Vladimir Putin pledged, in a conversation with Pope Leo, to cooperate in delivering vital supplies into the town in southern Ukraine's Kherson region. ""Russians are trying to turn the Ukrainian town of Oleshky into a death trap. The hospital has run out of medicine. Dozens of bodies lie in the streets or in homes, as Russians forbid residents from burying them,"" Sybiha wrote. ""Families are starving, surviving on leftover garden vegetables, preserves and even snails ... Ukraine has repeatedly called for safe evacuation and international humanitarian access. These appeals cannot remain unanswered."" The Kremlin acknowledged in September that Oleshky had critical problems with food and medical supplies but said Russia's military was working to resolve them. Ukraine's military has twice used drones to deliver food and medicine to the town. Sybiha urged the United Nations, the International Committee of the Red Cross and other organisations to ""secure safe evacuation corridors and immediate humanitarian access."" Oleshky lies on the east side of the Dnipro River in Kherson region, which Russian forces mostly occupied soon after the 2022 invasion. A Ukrainian counteroffensive ","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:59:46 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/ukrainian-foreign-minister-says-russian-held-town-is-a-death-trap-with-starving-residents-101792265386957.html,2026-10-18 01:50:49
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Desperate civilians risk their lives along mined roads to escape from a Russian-held city,Desperate civilians risk their lives along mined roads to escape from a Russian-held city,"Desperate civilians risk their lives along mined roads to escape from a Russian-held city Desperate civilians risk their lives along mined roads to escape from a Russian-held city Food and medical supplies have been scarce for months in Oleshky, which sits on the Dnipro River that separates Ukrainian forces from the city’sRussian occupiers.There’s no electricity, heat or running water. Roads leading from town are mined and dotted with burned-out cars. Menacing drones buzz overhead. One desperate resident watched the front-line city slide further toward a humanitarian crisis as its trapped population dwindled to fewer than 2,000 people. She decided it was time to escape via what's been dubbed “the road of death.” So the 40-year-old former hairdresser walked — for 20 kilometers , along the partially mined road — fearing that “every step could have been my last,"" she said. Once a city of about 24,000, Oleshky was captured by Moscow’s forces on thefirst day of their full-scale invasionof Ukraine in 2022. Its buildings were shelled and burned, and its infrastructure damaged. Further calamity struck in June 2023when floodwaters inundated the cityafter the Kakhovka Dam collapsed from an explosionthat an Associated Press investigation foundwas likely caused by the Russian military, killing hundreds of residents. And now those still left in the battered city along Ukraine's southeastern front face death from hunger or disease as another harsh winter looms, with no agreement in sight for even a humanitarian evacuation. “There is nothing left in Oleshky,” said the woman, who fled in August and spoke to The Associated Press on condition of anonymity because she fe","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:57:45 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/desperate-civilians-risk-their-lives-along-mined-roads-to-escape-from-a-russian-held-city-101792265265819.html,2026-10-18 01:50:49
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"California ‘No Kings’ protests draw crowds in San Francisco, Los Angeles, Santa Barbara, San Diego and Santa Monica","California ‘No Kings’ protests draw crowds in San Francisco, Los Angeles, Santa Barbara, San Diego and Santa Monica","California ‘No Kings’ protests draw crowds in San Francisco, Los Angeles, Santa Barbara, San Diego and Santa Monica ‘No Kings’ rallies across Southern California urged early voting and election participation ahead of the November 3 midterm elections. ‘No Kings’ demonstrations took place across Southern California and other parts of the United States on Saturday, October 17, as organisers urged eligible voters to prepare for the November 3 midterm elections. The rallies focused on early voting and election protection, while demonstrators also raised concerns about PresidentDonald Trump’sadministration and federal immigration enforcement. According to USA TODAY, nearly 3,000 events were planned nationwide, including more than 300 inCalifornia. The latest campaign, called ‘No Kings: Vote Early’, marked a shift from earlier demonstrations by putting voter participation at the centre of the day’s activities. In the South Bay area of Los Angeles County, around 50 protesters walked along the boardwalk connecting Manhattan Beach and Hermosa Beach. A larger rally was scheduled at Gloria Molina Grand Park in downtown Los Angeles, with a march planned through the surrounding area. Also Read:US justice department gives California 48 hours to flag alleged noncitizen voters: Report Previous ‘No Kings’ demonstrations, including rallies in June 2025, October 2025 and March 2026, focused on opposition to Trump and concerns about his administration’s policies. The October 17 events added a stronger focus on helping people understand their voting options and make plans to cast their ballots early. The No Kings coalition described the campaign as a nonpartisan voter participation effort. Activities included rallies, neighbourhood events and ","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:42:35 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/california-no-kings-protests-draw-crowds-in-san-francisco-los-angeles-santa-barbara-san-diego-and-santa-monica-101792263984687.html,2026-10-18 01:50:49
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Two I-275 ramps in Ohio to close this weekend for bridge repairs: Which routes are affected?,Two I-275 ramps in Ohio to close this weekend for bridge repairs: Which routes are affected?,"Two I-275 ramps in Ohio to close this weekend for bridge repairs: Which routes are affected? Two ramps at the I-275 and SR 4 interchange in Ohio will close from Friday night until Monday morning for bridge repairs. Two ramps at the Interstate 275 and state Route 4 interchange in Hamilton County,Ohio, will be closed this weekend forbridgerepairs. TheOhioDepartment of Transportation (ODOT) has announced the closures. As per Local12, the ramp from southbound SR 4 to eastbound I-275 will close. The ramp from northbound SR 4 to westbound I-275 will also close. The closures will begin at 8pm on Friday. As per the report, the restrictions are scheduled to stay in place until 6am on Monday, October 19. Also read:Shooting at NYC subway station leaves man critically injured As per Local12, the closures are part of a project to heat-straighten beams and replace cross frames. According to the report, this work is being done beneath the I-275 bridge over SR 4, which is also known as Springdale Pike. Also read:5-year-old Rhoam Summer, youngest Oregon school shooting victim, remembered as a 'very happy child'; GoFundMe launched Drivers on southbound SR 4 who want to go to eastbound I-275 should use westbound I-275 and the Winton Road interchange to turn around. Drivers on northbound SR 4 who want to go to westbound I-275 can take eastbound I-275 to the SR 747 interchange. According to the government announcement, drivers in downtown Knoxville in Knox County should also be ready for road work that will have a significant impact on traffic. As per the notice, crews will inspect several bridges in the downtown area from Monday, October 19, to Wednesday, October 28. The inspections will take place daily a","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:06:20 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/two-i-275-ramps-in-ohio-to-close-this-weekend-for-bridge-repairs-which-routes-are-affected-101792260764775.html,2026-10-18 01:50:49
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Shooting at NYC subway station leaves man critically injured,Shooting at NYC subway station leaves man critically injured,"Shooting at NYC subway station leaves man critically injured A 34-year-old man was critically injured in a shooting aboard a northbound E train in Manhattan, disrupting subway services. A man was critically injured after being shot aboard a northbound E train at the Seventh Avenue and 53rd Street subway station in Manhattan,New York City, on Saturday, October 17, 2026, according to the New York Police Department (NYPD). Emergency medical services took the victim to a hospital, where he was in critical condition. The shooting was reported at around 12:21 p.m. after a 911 call, according toFOX 5 New York. Police have not released the man's name or age. Authorities have also not confirmed what led to the gunfire or publicly identified a suspect in the reports available so far. The investigation is ongoing. Also Read:Oregon teacher Meghan Lambert killed in school shooting was a beloved local volleyball coach: 'She loved teaching' The New York Post reported that the victim was 34 years old and had been shot in the chest. According to the newspaper, two male suspects fired shots from inside the northbound Etraintowards the platform. The man received CPR on the platform before being taken to Bellevue Hospital in cardiac arrest, the New York Post reported, citing police sources. FOX 5 New York also confirmed that the victim was in critical condition. Police have not released the victim’s identity. Authorities have also not confirmed whether anyone has been arrested or provided a description of the suspected shooters. The reason for the shooting has not been established. Also Read:Man stopped the Oregon elementary school shooter has 2 daughters at the school, his mom","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 23:14:19 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/nyc-subway-station-shooting-today-man-shot-aboard-northbound-e-train-in-manhattan-101792256959684.html,2026-10-18 01:50:49
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"'Reward for Putin': Zelensky fears US sanctions relief to Russia, diesel deal not the end of it","'Reward for Putin': Zelensky fears US sanctions relief to Russia, diesel deal not the end of it","'Reward for Putin': Zelensky fears US sanctions relief to Russia, diesel deal not the end of it The Ukrainian President also spoke fondly of late US senator Lindsey Graham, who worked endlessly on the bill targeting Russia before he died this summer. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky on Saturday said that US President Donald Trump's recent sanctions waiver to Russia would only encourage it to continue attacks on Ukraine. He was referring to Trump's decision to waive a new package of sanctions against Russia. “Sanctions relief will continue. I think the diesel deal and the move regarding the Lindsey Graham Act are not the end of it. Unfortunately,” he said in a post on X, referring to a deal under which Russia would supply diesel in exchange for relief from US sanctions. He also spoke fondly of late US senator Lindsey Graham, who worked endlessly on the bill targeting Russia before he died this summer. “He devoted his life to America. We had an excellent relationship with him. And shortly before the tragedy, Lindsey was in Ukraine. He always supported us very strongly and pushed for as much assistance to Ukraine as possible,” Zelensky wrote. Trump signed the Lindsey O Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026 in mid-September. However, on Saturday he decided to waive some sanctions against Russia just as they were to take effect under the law. According to news agency AP, an executive order signed by Trump said the waiver was in the national interests of the United States. “The decision to undo all of this, to block all of it, seems wrong to me. And it definitely helps only the Russians. It only fuels their satisfaction and their desire to keep killing people. This certainly does not stop the war,” Zelensky said.","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 23:05:14 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/reward-for-putin-zelensky-fears-us-sanctions-relief-to-russia-diesel-deal-not-the-end-of-it-101792252174760.html,2026-10-18 01:50:49
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,'Beyond irresponsible': Trump's decision to waive Russian sanctions faces Congress backlash,'Beyond irresponsible': Trump's decision to waive Russian sanctions faces Congress backlash,"'Beyond irresponsible': Trump's decision to waive Russian sanctions faces Congress backlash Trump's actions come as Ukraine is bracing for a stark winter confronting Russia's attacks on its energy infrastructure to heat homes and keep the lights on. President Donald Trump's abrupt decision to waive sanctions on Russia days before they were to take effect drew outrage Saturday from members of Congress, which had overwhelmingly passed the legislation in a bipartisan display of American support for Ukraine in the war with Russia. Lawmakers from both parties blasted the executive order late Friday, the latest in a series of steps that the Republican president has taken drawing the United States closer to Russia's Vladimir Putin. Trump recently announced a diesel deal with Putin to import Russian energy in a long-shot effort to curb high gas prices in the U.S. that are roiling the midterm elections on Nov. 3. Also Read |Trump waives some Russia-related sanctions that threatened 100% tariffs on India, China “Beyond irresponsible,” said Sen. Thom Tillis, R-N.C., on social media, hours after Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy expressed hope that the sanctions that were supposed to take effect Sunday targeting Russian's economy could be revived. “What part of enforcing a law passed 86-11 by the Senate does the President and his advisors not understand?” Tillis said. “It’s unconscionable that this administration is capitulating to a Russian dictator who illegally invaded Ukraine.” Tillis said that if Trump wants to actually bring down energy prices, he should resolve the Iran war, which is largely blamed for the spike at the pump, and “not by doing a solid for Vladimir Putin.” Republican Sen. John Cornyn of Texas said “Lindse","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 21:27:49 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/beyond-irresponsible-trumps-decision-to-waive-russian-sanctions-faces-congress-backlash-101792251590557.html,2026-10-18 01:50:49
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,'Will keep with me till I die': Smit Machchhar recalls colleague's bravery amid flydubai horror,'Will keep with me till I die': Smit Machchhar recalls colleague's bravery amid flydubai horror,"'Will keep with me till I die': Smit Machchhar recalls colleague's bravery amid flydubai horror Recovering in a hospital weeks after the incident, Machchhar is trying to get over those moments inside the cockpit when he had been attacked by Omani co-pilot. Captain Smit Machchhar of flydubai flight that narrowly escaped a major aviation tragedy on September 30 has recalled the moment when he was hit by theOmani co-pilotinside the cockpit in an alleged bid to crash the plane over Israel skies. “I realised for a moment that I wouldn't survive,” CaptainMachchhar, who has been hailed a herofor averting a tragedy, can be heard saying in the trailer of his interview with Sky News Arabia. This is for the first time that Machchhar has spoken to English media after the flydubai incident. The Indian brave heart pilot also lauded his other colleague who took over the control of the plane after he had collapsed on the floor upon beinghit with a crash axe. The plane plunged nearly 14,000 ft in less than 30 seconds. “My colleague who took control and saved the day is commendable, It is something that I will keep with me till I die,” he said. Also read:Air France to follow Israel’s ban on airline crew from Pakistan, 30 other countries after flydubai incident: Report Recovering in a hospital, weeks after the incident, Machchhar said he is trying to get over those moments inside the cockpit when he had been attacked by Omani co-pilot Hammam al-Hammami. “There was a moment when I told myself ‘Smit, do something. You can’t die like this',” he can be heard saying in the interview. “There was a moment when I realised that if something is not done now, then..” he said, leaving the sentence incomplete. The rogue co-pilot was then tackled by some of th","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 20:42:27 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/captain-smit-machchhar-recalls-colleagues-bravery-amid-flydubai-flight-omani-co-pilot-israel-tel-aviv-101792247439524.html,2026-10-18 01:50:49
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"CEC stir reaches London as protests, counter-protest held at Parliament Square; Watch","CEC stir reaches London as protests, counter-protest held at Parliament Square; Watch","CEC stir reaches London as protests, counter-protest held at Parliament Square; Watch In a video of the crowd at London's Parliament Square, protesters can be seen raising flags and posters while chanting slogans. A crowd gathered at Parliament Square, London, UK, on Saturday, reportedly to protest against Chief Election Commissioner (CEC) Gyanesh Kumar over the alleged irregularities in the Special Intensive Revision (SIR). They were faced with supporters of the CEC, who also assembled at the spot to hold a counter-protest. Follow live updates of the CJP protesthere. People supporting and opposing the CEC and the Cockroach Janta Party gathered at the Parliament Square, according to news agencyANI. In a video of the crowd at London's Parliament Square, protesters can be seen raising flags and posters while chanting slogans. According to an X post shared by the Students' Federation of India, United Kingdom, the organisation gathered outside the High Commission of India, and wrote, ""LONDON STANDS WITH INDIA!"" The protesters demanded the resignation of CEC Gyanesh Kumar, according to the post. Also Read |‘Why was Jantar Mantar shut?’ Protesters defy curbs to join CJP march in Delhi The demonstrations also resonated internationally, as members of the Indian diaspora in London staged a counter-protest in support of Prime Minister Narendra Modi on Friday, opposing protests by the Congress-backed National Students' Union of India (NSUI) and other like-minded groups outside the Indian High Commission. The Cockroach Janta Party and the Opposition INDIA bloc have been protesting over the ongoing voter roll revision in the country. The controversy centres on alleged irregularities in the Election","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 20:29:30 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/cec-stir-reaches-london-as-protests-counter-protest-held-at-parliament-square-watch-101792242725549.html,2026-10-18 01:50:50
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,A former ally asks why Donald Trump abandoned his own legacyled Story,A former ally asks why Donald Trump abandoned his own legacyled Story,"A former ally asks why Donald Trump abandoned his own legacyled Story The president is undermining the few advantages Republicans still have, writes Marc Short MIDTERM ELECTIONS are a referendum on the party in power, and Republicans took power promising to defeat inflation, restore a roaring economy and usher in a new American golden age. But they have not delivered, and on November 3rd voters will punish them at the ballot box. The first Trump administration brought a new style but largely embraced traditional free-market policies: lowering taxes, cutting regulations and encouraging the government to leave the American people alone and let them prosper. The result—over the first three years of the administration, before covid-19 struck—was almost 7m new jobs, a $6,000 average increase in the income of middle-class families and an unemployment rate of 3.5%, a 50-year low. Had it not been for the pandemic, Donald Trump may have won re-election in 2020 on the strength of the economy. I was the White House director of legislative affairs in the first Trump administration, leading our congressional outreach, and I will always be proud of our conservative record. When he returned to the White House in January 2025, however, Mr Trump embraced some of his most protectionist and populist economic instincts. Needless trade wars with Canada and other allies have pummelled American farmers, including in key swing states. Six of the Senate swing seats alone account for more than a fifth of America’s $880bn a year in goods-and-services trade with Canada. But all Americans have suffered from the uncertainty and higher prices brought on by the pr","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 18:30:16 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/a-former-ally-asks-why-donald-trump-abandoned-his-own-legacyled-story-101792238059514.html,2026-10-18 01:50:50
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"London Beer Flood: How a brewery disaster caused deluge, killing 8 people 200 years ago","London Beer Flood: How a brewery disaster caused deluge, killing 8 people 200 years ago","London Beer Flood: How a brewery disaster caused deluge, killing 8 people 200 years ago The London Beer Flood claimed eight lives, with women and children among the victims. Imagine a wave of beer crashing through a neighbourhood, smashing into homes and burying people under rubble. On October 17, 1814,Londonwitnessed precisely such a disaster. A giant beer vat burst at a brewery, unleashing thousands of barrels of porter into nearby streets. The incident, remembered as the London Beer Flood, killed eight people and became one of the strangest industrial disasters in British history. The disaster unfolded at Meux & Co's Horse Shoe Brewery, near the junction of Tottenham Court Road andOxford Street, in central London. The brewery stood close to St Giles, a densely populated and impoverished neighbourhood where many families lived in cramped homes and basement rooms. At the centre of the incident was an enormous wooden vat, about 22 feet (6.7 metres) tall, containing fermenting porter, a dark beer that was popular in London at the time. The vessel was reinforced with heavy iron hoops to hold its wooden structure together. On the afternoon of October 17, a brewery storehouse clerk noticed that one of the iron hoops had slipped off the vat. Such incidents had occurred before, and the problem was not treated as an immediate emergency. The vat eventually burst, releasing its contents with tremendous force. The escaping beer damaged neighbouring vessels, setting off a chain reaction that released even more liquid. The torrent smashed through part of the brewery's brick wall and surged into the surrounding streets and homes. The amount released is not co","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 18:00:06 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/london-beer-flood-how-a-brewery-disaster-caused-deluge-killing-8-people-200-years-ago-101792230493473.html,2026-10-18 01:50:50
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,'Phantom farter' mystery takes over Zelensky's press briefing at EU summit: ‘Journalists forced to flee’,'Phantom farter' mystery takes over Zelensky's press briefing at EU summit: ‘Journalists forced to flee’,"'Phantom farter' mystery takes over Zelensky's press briefing at EU summit: ‘Journalists forced to flee’ Sharing the ‘phantom farter’ report on X, political economy analyst and correspondent Shankkar Aiyar described it as ""hilarious."" A bizarre incident cast a shadow over the high-profile European Union summit in Brussels when journalists waiting for Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky's press briefing encountered what was described as a ""mystery"". ""While waiting for Volodymyr Zelensky, reporters are trying to solve a mystery: Who keeps farting in the European Council press briefing room,"" Politico Europe said on X, sharing a report. Also Read |'We're done': Ukraine's Zelensky says won't follow Trump's demand of no strike on Russian refineries Sharing the report on X, political economy analyst and correspondent Shankkar Aiyar described it as ""hilarious"". ""Who is the phantom farter,"" he wrote, sharing the Politico report in the post. ""Reporters waiting for President Volodymyr Zelensky in the European Council press briefing room have repeatedly been forced to deal with a fetid farter in their midst,"" it added. Also Read |Macron slams 'very politicised' EU leader debate on migration ""It isn’t the first time either, with several journalists (including this correspondent) forced to flee a diplomatic briefing last night after an eye-watering emission,"" it continued. ""Everyone is running on nothing but coffee and the fear of missing out on a briefing, so we suppose it is to be expected. Still, one can’t help but wish the mystery malodorer would take themselves outside and sort it out before people pass out,"" it further read. Also Read |EU, Gulf leaders postpone Riyadh summit as Houthi attacks on Saudi Arabia d","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 17:06:31 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/phantom-farter-mystery-takes-over-zelenskys-press-briefing-at-eu-summit-journalists-forced-to-flee-101792230459466.html,2026-10-18 01:50:50
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Katie Zacharia was interrupted mid-interview, then came her exit as Trump's press secretary","Katie Zacharia was interrupted mid-interview, then came her exit as Trump's press secretary","Katie Zacharia was interrupted mid-interview, then came her exit as Trump's press secretary A report has revealed the moment Katie Zacharia was called in for a chat with US Vice President JD Vance and subsequently told she is being let go. Katie Zacharia is out as White House press secretary, less than one week into assuming the key role. Her appointment had come months after Karoline Leavitt, a long time adviser of US President Donald Trump, left the post in August. A report has now revealed the moment she was called in for a chat withUS Vice President JD Vanceand subsequently told she is being let go. According to Wall Street Journal, Zacharia was seated for an interview with the publication when she was interrupted by Jason Miller, a senior adviser to Trump. “JD wanted to chat with us real quick,” Miller said referring to the vice president, WSJ said in a report. She never returned to continue the interview or her job as Trump's press secretary, the publication further said. It was not clear in the report what Miller told Zacharia or what conversation did she have with the vice president. However, according to an AP report, Zacharia had dinner with JD Vance and his family on Friday evening. Katie Zacharia was reportedly feeling weary at around midday on Friday, before her exit. “I’m just running on no sleep and high adrenaline and probably too much caffeine and no food,” she was quoted as saying by WSJ. Zacharia had been under scrutiny ever since her inaugural briefing as the press secretary. She took questions from just three reporters during her nearly 15-minute media interaction on Monday. Also Read:Laura Loomer eyes White House press secretary role after Katie Zacharia's exit She later traveled with Trum","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 16:13:17 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/jd-wanted-quick-chat-katie-zacharia-was-interrupted-by-trumps-adviser-mid-interview-before-white-house-exit-101792227755066.html,2026-10-18 01:50:50
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Saudi executes 5 nationals over 'terrorism-related' offences,Saudi executes 5 nationals over 'terrorism-related' offences,"Saudi executes 5 nationals over 'terrorism-related' offences Those executed were accused of joining a foreign organisation designated as terrorist, attacking security forces and supporting attacks inside Saudi Arabia. Saudi Arabia executed five people Saturday on terrorism charges, the ministry of interior said, bringing the total number of death sentences carried out this year to 188, according to an AFP tally based on official statements. The five Saudi nationals were convicted of ""joining a foreign terrorist organisation"", opening fire on security forces and ""providing support"" for ""terrorist operations inside the kingdom"". Their executions were carried out in the Eastern Province, home to most of Saudi Arabia's Shia Muslim minority, which has long complained of marginalisation in the Sunni-majority country. Since January, the kingdom has put 15 people to death after terror convictions, most of them in the east, compared to 40 in 2025. The latest executions come as Saudi Arabia faces daily missile and drone attacks from the Houthis in Yemen, where Riyadh militarily backs the internationally recognised government in its long-running civil war against the Iran-backed group. According to Amnesty International, Saudi Arabia was the world's third-most prolific executioner between 2022 and 2025, behind China and Iran. The kingdom considers the death penalty necessary to maintain public order, and insists it is only applied when all appeals have been exhausted. Follow the latest breaking news, major developments and agenda-setting stories from India and around the world with the newsdesk at Hindustan Times. Operating round the clock, the desk brings together experienced editors, reporters and ","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 16:08:46 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/saudi-executes-5-nationals-over-terrorism-related-offences-101792233084307.html,2026-10-18 01:50:50
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,UK author releases second instalment of Indian-Irish cosy crime mystery,UK author releases second instalment of Indian-Irish cosy crime mystery,"UK author releases second instalment of Indian-Irish cosy crime mystery UK author releases second instalment of Indian-Irish cosy crime mystery London, A UK-based author's second instalment of an Indian-Irish cosy crime mystery series was launched in London recently, with its India release set for next week. Paul Waters, from Belfast in Northern Ireland, fell in love with India when he fell in love with his Delhi-born wife Aneysha. He went on to set 'Murder in Moonlight Square' in his wife's city and has now followed up the success of his unusual crime-fighting duo of an Irish nun and Delhi hotel proprietor with 'Death At the Elephant House'. ""The books have two main characters: one of them is a semi-retired Irish teaching nun called Sister Agatha Murphy of the Sisters of the Amazing Grace, nicknamed the Saggies, and her partner in crime solving Avtar Mehta proprietor of the historic Delhi Haveli Hotel in Chandni Chowk,"" Waters told PTI. ""In book one, they meet accidentally over a sneaky smoke behind his hotel and get into all sorts of trouble with the police, spies, a disappearing guest and a secret killer while maintaining their sense of humour. ""In book two, the world's most unlikely crime solving duo returns to figure out a deadly locked room mystery involving one of Delhi's richest families, a charismatic gangster, a police siege and a cigarette-stealing elephant,"" he said. The storyline and characters of Waters' mystery series, a sub-genre referred to as cosy crime for their puzzle solving characteristics, draw heavily from reality. His Irish family has links with India through a real-life aunt Agatha, who was ","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 14:32:09 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/uk-author-releases-second-instalment-of-indian-irish-cosy-crime-mystery-101792227730343.html,2026-10-18 01:50:50
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Trump mocks popularity of the name ‘Mohammad’, common among Muslims","Trump mocks popularity of the name ‘Mohammad’, common among Muslims","Trump mocks popularity of the name ‘Mohammad’, common among Muslims Mohammad is one of the most common male names among Muslims, who often name their sons after the Prophet Mohammad, the founder of Islam. US PresidentDonald Trumpmade a joke about the popularity of the name Mohammad in the Muslim world as he mockedIran'spresidency during a campaign speech on Friday. ""You know, if you're in doubt about somebody's name from the Middle East, just say 'Mohammad,'"" Trump told a crowd in Dalton, Georgia. ""You have about a 65% chance. 'Hello, Mohammad.' I've done it for years now. I've never been wrong."" Mohammad is one of the most common male names among Muslims, who often name their sons after the Prophet Mohammad, the founder of Islam. Also Read IUS lawmakers write to Trump's secretary of state over Delhi detentions as India rejects ‘interference’ Trump made the comment as he was discussing Iran during a rally in a hangar at Dalton Municipal Airport in Georgia, where he campaigned for Republican political candidates ahead of the November 3 midterm elections. ""Iran is the only country where nobody wants to be president,"" Trump said, then pretended to ask several people named Mohammad whether they wanted the job. Masoud Pezeshkian has been president of Iran since 2024. The Georgia crowd gave the joke a muted response. Rights advocates and Muslim advocacy groups have repeatedly criticized Trump for rhetoric that they say demonizes and mocks Muslims, immigrants and ethnic minorities. He hasescalated such rhetoric during the midterm campaign season, particularly against Somali and Muslim Americans including Muslim Democratic‌US Senate candidate Abdul El-Sayed and RepresentativeIlhan Oma","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:40:37 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/donald-trump-mohammad-muslims-us-midterms-2026-islamophobia-georgia-republicans-rally-101792224033443.html,2026-10-18 01:50:50
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"US Congressman claims H-1B holders from India, China replacing American graduates with cheap labour","US Congressman claims H-1B holders from India, China replacing American graduates with cheap labour","US Congressman claims H-1B holders from India, China replacing American graduates with cheap labour Riley Moore also claimed that H-1B visa holders bring family members to the US, linking the programme to what he described as chain migration. US Congressman Riley Moore has alleged that H-1 B visa holders are replacing American computer science graduates, claiming employers are turning to foreign workers willing to accept lower wages. In a post on X accompanying a snippet of his television interview, Moore called theH-1Band Optional Practical Training (OPT) programmes “SCAMS” and alleged that they undercut and replace American workers with cheaper foreign labour. “H-1B and OPT programs are SCAMS that undercut and replace American workers with cheap foreign labor,” he wrote. “650k Americans graduated with computer science related degrees, but they’re being replaced by the 500k H1B visa holders who are mostly from China and India,” he added. “We had about 650,000 kids, give or take, graduate with computer science, computer programming degrees in this country last year, and then issued over half a million H-1B visas,” Moore said during a television interview with Scott Jennings onThe Scott Jennings Show, broadcast on theSalem News Channel (SNC). “I mean, it's crazy. And at the end of the day, they're willing to do these jobs at a lower rate, cheaper labor, and so all of our kids are getting replaced in this,” he alleged. Moore criticised the H-1B visa programme, arguing that it was not working as intended and was instead bringing in cheaper labour. “Sold on H-1B visa program is that we're going to bring the best and the brightest talent in the United States to fill these jobs that we're not able to fill. That is not h","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:36:48 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-congressman-claims-h-1b-holders-from-india-china-replacing-american-graduates-with-cheap-labour-101792213471112.html,2026-10-18 01:50:50
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Supertanker carrying 2 million barrels of oil hits naval mine, explodes in Strait of Hormuz","Supertanker carrying 2 million barrels of oil hits naval mine, explodes in Strait of Hormuz","Supertanker carrying 2 million barrels of oil hits naval mine, explodes in Strait of Hormuz Attacks around the waterway have increased in recent weeks. Iran has also intensified its strikes in response to the US blockade of its ports. A supertanker carrying more than 2 million barrels ofcrude oilexploded and caught fire after striking a naval mine while passing through theStrait of Hormuz, Iran'sRevolutionary Guardssaid on Saturday. The IRGC said the vessel was attempting to cross the strategic waterway illegally using a route ""not authorised byTehran"", Iran’s state-owned Press TV reported. In a statement, the IRGC alleged that the tanker had deliberately switched off its positioning and radar systems in an “attempt to deceive” Iranian maritime authorities. The IRGC also warned all vessels against using the waterway, saying the Strait of Hormuz remained closed under Iran's sovereign decisions and maritime regulations. ""The Strait of Hormuz is closed, and the consequence of defying Iranian regulations in the Strait of Hormuz is burning in the fire of trust in the terrorist US army,"" it said. Attacks around the waterway have increased in recent weeks. Iran has also intensified its strikes in response to the US blockade of its ports and stricter sanctions imposed last month. Rising threats to commercial shipping in and around the Strait of Hormuz have heightened security concerns in the region. Before the war, the waterway handled about a fifth of global oil and liquefied natural gas trade. Tehran has sought to tighten its control over the strait since US and Israeli strikes on Iran on February 28. It has restricted maritime traffic in an attempt to gain leverage over Washington. Iran is increasingly using ne","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:28:56 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/tanker-carrying-over-2-million-barrels-of-crude-oil-hits-mine-in-hormuz-catches-fire-irans-guards-say-101792219288746.html,2026-10-18 01:50:51
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,‘Truly historic moment’: Putin's envoy hails Trump's move to waive some sanctions against Russia,‘Truly historic moment’: Putin's envoy hails Trump's move to waive some sanctions against Russia,"‘Truly historic moment’: Putin's envoy hails Trump's move to waive some sanctions against Russia His remarks came hours after Trump signed an executive order waiving key Russia-related sanctions and tariffs days before they were to take effect under a law. Russian presidential envoy Kirill Dmitriev on Saturday described US PresidentDonald Trump'sdecision to waive a new package of sanctions againstRussiaas a ""historic moment"". ""The Trump administration's suspension of the Graham Act is an important and truly historic moment,"" Dmitriev, an envoy for Russian PresidentVladimir Putin, said on X. His remarks came hours after Trump signed an executive order waiving key Russia-relatedUS sanctionsand tariffs days before they were to take effect under a law he signed last month, including duties on the country's goods and penalties for buying its oil and gas. “Instead of sanctions and confrontation, we need dialogue, mutually beneficial cooperation, and shared responsibility for global stability,” Dmitriev wrote. The Lindsey Graham Act authorised Trump to impose duties of up to 100 per cent on major buyers of Russian oil and gas. Trump signed the legislation into law last month. His decision to waive the sanctions came after he struck a deal with Putin to ease restrictions on Russia in exchange for Moscow releasing large quantities of diesel into global markets. The move came as Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky urged EU leaders to provide nearly $80 billion in additional funding next year to support Kyiv's war effort. He maintained that he was defending his country as Trump's support for Ukraine declined. Under an agreement reached last week, Trump said Russia would immediately supply more than 300,000 tons of diesel. Another 500,0","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 12:57:23 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/putin-envoy-kirill-dmitriev-hails-trump-decision-on-waiving-some-russia-sanctions-calls-it-historic-moment-101792221321563.html,2026-10-18 01:50:51
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Susie Wiles helped arrange Katie Zacharia’s Fox News interview before her exit: Report,Susie Wiles helped arrange Katie Zacharia’s Fox News interview before her exit: Report,"Susie Wiles helped arrange Katie Zacharia’s Fox News interview before her exit: Report White House chief of staff Susie Wiles reportedly helped arrange Katie Zacharia’s Fox News interview shortly before she was removed as press secretary. White Housechief of staff Susie Wiles helped arrange Katie Zacharia’s appearance on Fox News shortly before the new White House press secretary was removed from her position, Politicoreportedon Saturday, citing two people familiar with internal discussions. The interview with host Sean Hannity aired on Thursday night and focused onZacharia’s qualifications and her troubled start in the role. She was ousted on Friday after just five days as press secretary. One person familiar with the White House’s internal dynamics described the appearance as a “sanctioned hit” on Zacharia, who was reportedly not Wiles’ preferred choice for the position. ALSO READ:'It was an honor to work for Trump': Katie Zacharia after quitting White House job in 5 days Another source alleged that Wiles and White House spokesperson Danielle Alvarez had sent Zacharia into a difficult interview with Hannity, with whom Wiles is said to have a friendly relationship. However, a senior White House official disputed the suggestion that Wiles had orchestrated the interview to undermine Zacharia, saying the chief of staff did not need to arrange such an encounter. White House communications director Steven Cheung also dismissed the reporting as speculation, whileFox NewsMedia said Hannity independently decides whom he interviews and what questions he asks. During the interview, Hannity questioned Zacharia about her first White House press briefing, which he described as widely criticized. The briefing lasted 14 min","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 11:30:07 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/susie-wiles-helped-arrange-katie-zacharia-s-fox-news-interview-before-her-exit-report-101792215849593.html,2026-10-18 01:50:51
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Can social media bans protect young people’s mental health? Research suggests bans aren’t the answer,Can social media bans protect young people’s mental health? Research suggests bans aren’t the answer,"Can social media bans protect young people’s mental health? Research suggests bans aren’t the answer Can social media bans protect young people’s mental health? Research suggests bans aren’t the answer Pittsburgh , The year 2026 has been an expensive one for social media platforms. Since March, YouTube and Meta, the parent company of Instagram and Facebook, have settled or paid out in multiple lawsuits to the tune of more than USD 17 billion for allegedly acting negligently, deliberately designing their platforms to hook kids and misleading the public about safety and privacy. If these suits are any indication of how states and the public are starting to view social media platforms, more may follow. On top of these lawsuits, social media platforms are facing a loss of business as bans for minors are accelerating and spreading worldwide. Australia was one of the first with a ban on kids under 16 on social media starting in December 2025, followed soon after by the UK with a similar prohibition. The current list of countries looking to ban minors from these platforms tops more than 20, from Greece to the United Arab Emirates to Canada. The proposed and enacted bans around the world vary in terms of age restriction, parental consent rules and age verification methods. Different countries are looking at restricting different platforms, but most include a mix of Snapchat, YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, Facebook and X. These bans aren't necessarily for mental health reasons only some are motivated by political concerns about foreign-owned platforms, and others aim to protect young people's privacy. But nearly every government banning social media platforms for young people mentions ","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 11:23:21 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/can-social-media-bans-protect-young-people-s-mental-health-research-suggests-bans-aren-t-the-answer-101792216401774.html,2026-10-18 01:50:51
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"India hits back at UN rapporteur over youth protests, calls report ‘hearsay, unverified claims’","India hits back at UN rapporteur over youth protests, calls report ‘hearsay, unverified claims’","India hits back at UN rapporteur over youth protests, calls report ‘hearsay, unverified claims’ First secretary Anuj Swarup defended India's constitutional safeguards, blaming arbitrary UN reports for its loss of credibility. India strongly rebutted allegations by UN special rapporteur Gina Romero that young people in the country face retaliation or stigmatisation for speaking out on mental health and gender justice. Delivering India’s statement at the 81st Session of theUNGAThird Committee, first secretary Anuj Swarup defended the country’s constitutional safeguards for freedom of expression and peaceful assembly, calling the report’s approach arbitrary. “In India, these freedoms, together with freedom of speech and expression, are guaranteed to every citizen under Article 19 of our Constitution, and are protected by an independent judiciary, a free press, and an empowered National Human Rights Commission,” Swarup said in the Interactive Dialogue with the Special Rapporteur on the rights to freedom of peaceful assembly and of association. The UN report comes amid protests in India demanding the resignation of Chief Election Commissioner (CEC) Gyanesh Kumar over voter roll revision. The Indian delegate further highlighted that the report is “merely citing unnamed submissions in the footnote.” ""This level of arbitrary report writing is precisely the reason that the United Nations and its institutions have lost credibility,” he added. ALSO READ:Indian diplomat's reaction to Pakistani PM's ‘victory’ claim after Operation Sindoor goes viral | Watch video ""A sweeping charge against the world's largest youth population resting on anonymous and unverified claims is not evidence. It is hearsay, dressed up","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 11:21:53 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/india-hits-back-at-un-rapporteur-gina-romero-over-youth-protests-calls-report-hearsay-unverified-claims-101792213647192.html,2026-10-18 01:50:51
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"In 1904, a British duke sent six Himalayan tahr to New Zealand for hunting; five survived, and the population later grew to tens of thousands","In 1904, a British duke sent six Himalayan tahr to New Zealand for hunting; five survived, and the population later grew to tens of thousands","In 1904, a British duke sent six Himalayan tahr to New Zealand for hunting; five survived, and the population later grew to tens of thousands In 1904, a British duke gave New Zealand six Himalayan tahr to hunt. A long-term study found their grazing affected alpine plant cover and tussock height. Sometimes a gift comes with a very long tail. In 1904, a British duke sent six Himalayan tahr to New Zealand so people could hunt them. A long-termstudypublished in the journalBiological Invasionsin 2017 tracked what those animals did to the mountains. Its authors, Jennyffer Cruz and colleagues, examined plots that were monitored for more than two decades. Here is what they found. The Duke of Bedford gave six tahr to the New Zealand government in 1904. TheDepartment of Conservation(DOC) says tahr are goat-like animals from the central Himalayan ranges of India and Nepal. They were brought in as a hunting resource, according to the study. Historical sources state that only five of the six animals made it through the trip. They were released near Mount Cook, in the Southern Alps, where they found themselves a nice home. At the time of the study, tahr occupied about 9,600 square kilometers of the Southern Alps. There was no linear growth in the numbers. According to the study, some areas once held up to 30 tahr per square kilometer. It also reports that commercial helicopter hunting from 1970 cut the population from about 50,000 to about 2,000 by 1983, within an estimated 6,000 square kilometers of the Southern Alps. Numbers have increased since then. DOC estimated about 29,800 tahr in the seven management units on publicconservationland in 2023, with a wide range of about 22,100 to 40,150. Counting is difficult because tahr live in steep, remote terrain. DOC says New Zealand’s ","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 11:01:20 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/in-1904-a-british-duke-sent-six-himalayan-tahr-to-new-zealand-for-hunting-five-survived-and-the-population-later-grew-to-tens-of-thousands-101791457371038.html,2026-10-18 01:50:51
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,US daylight saving time 2026: What we know on November clock change and Trump’s stalled plan,US daylight saving time 2026: What we know on November clock change and Trump’s stalled plan,"US daylight saving time 2026: What we know on November clock change and Trump’s stalled plan Daylight saving time ends November 1, 2026, in most of the United States.&nbsp; Americans will turn their clocks back by one hour on November 1, 2026, as daylight saving time ends across most of theUnited States, with Congress yet to approve a proposal backed by PresidentDonald Trumpto eliminate the twice-yearly clock change. The change will take effect at 2 am local time on Sunday, November 1, when clocks move back to 1 am. The shift marks a return to standard time, giving people an extra hour overnight but bringing earlier sunsets and more daylight during the morning. Under current federal law, daylight saving time begins on the second Sunday in March and ends on the first Sunday in November. Unless Congress changes the law, the existing schedule will remain in place. Also read:'It was an honor to work for Trump': Katie Zacharia after quitting White House job in 5 days The change continues because theSunshine Protection Act, which would make daylight saving time permanent, has stalled in the Senate. The US House of Representatives passed the legislation by a 308-117 vote on July 14. However, the Senate has yet to approve it, leaving the current system unchanged. The bill’s progress can be tracked through Congress.gov. Trumphas repeatedly urged lawmakers to end clock changes, arguing that the practice is inconvenient and costly. In an October 2poston Truth Social, he called on Senate Republicans to advance the legislation, describing the existing system as “ridiculous”. However, the proposal faces opposition from lawmakers concerned that permanent dayli","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:47:38 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/us-daylight-saving-time-2026-what-we-know-on-november-clock-change-and-trump-s-stalled-plan-101792213169086.html,2026-10-18 01:50:51
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"In 1868, Hong Kong gifted Hawaii 8 axis deer in goodwill; their descendants now number about 60,000 and damage crops, native plants, and forests","In 1868, Hong Kong gifted Hawaii 8 axis deer in goodwill; their descendants now number about 60,000 and damage crops, native plants, and forests","In 1868, Hong Kong gifted Hawaii 8 axis deer in goodwill; their descendants now number about 60,000 and damage crops, native plants, and forests Hawaii's axis deer began as a small gift. Today they strip bark, trample soil, eat crops, and the herds keep growing. Here is what it means for the islands. Eight axis deer, a goodwill gift from Hong Kong, were released on Molokaʻi in 1868. Today their descendants number in the tens of thousands, and they eat crops and native plants and damage forests. According to the U.S. Geological Surveyfact sheet, Hawaii’s native plants evolved without grazing animals, which can make them vulnerable to grazing. Axis deer, also known as chital, are native to India, Sri Lanka, and Nepal. Some were later transferred to Lānaʻi in 1920, while others were released on Maui in 1959, the fact sheet says. Before people arrived in Hawaii, the islands had no native land mammals except bats; there were no deer, sheep, or cattle. So when hooved animals came, the damage was quick. Introduced animals have damaged native plants by browsing, trampling, and bark stripping, the fact sheet says. Since 1778, about 10 percent of native Hawaiian plants have gone extinct, in part due to nonnative species. Axis deer eat crops, plants native to Hawaii, and ornamental plants. They also strip bark from trees. During severe drought, they are known to even eat the bark of trees. Bucks damage trees by rubbing bark with their antlers, killing mature trees. Deer can cause soil erosion along their trails, and cars frequently collide with them on roads. Reports from Maui have long noted deer in lower, dry forest areas, especially duringdrought, when they leave to look for water and green grass. On east Molokaʻi, the fact sheet says browsing and packed-down soil from feral pigs","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:46:23 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/in-1868-hong-kong-gifted-hawaii-8-axis-deer-in-goodwill-their-descendants-now-number-about-60-000-and-damage-crops-native-plants-and-forests-101791454511949.html,2026-10-18 01:50:51
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"In Kenya, a tree planted to halt desertification has reportedly spread across 2 million hectares; experts say about 75% of the country is at risk","In Kenya, a tree planted to halt desertification has reportedly spread across 2 million hectares; experts say about 75% of the country is at risk","In Kenya, a tree planted to halt desertification has reportedly spread across 2 million hectares; experts say about 75% of the country is at risk Mathenge trees were planted in Kenya to hold back the desert. Now they reportedly cover 2 million hectares, and about 75% of the country could host them. We have all been there where we tried solving one problem but created a much larger one. That is what has happened in parts of Kenya. The mathenge tree was planted to hold back desertification. According to astudyby Mbaabu and colleagues published inRemote Sensing, the tree's coverage in the lowlands of Baringo County rose from 882 hectares in 1988 to 18,792 hectares in 2016. For herder John Lmakato, the spread of mathenge trees indirectly cost him most of his livestock, as he tells it. John Lmakato lives at Lerata, a village at the base of Mount Ololokwe in Samburu County. He recalls a time when the rangelands were covered with grass, and his animals roamed free. Now, he says, the land is overgrown with mathenge trees and cattle cannot eat them. And so his animals had to travel farther for grass. A few years ago, his cattle wandered into a conservation area in Laikipia, an area known for conflicts over land access between Indigenous pastoralists and commercial ranchers. He lost 193 cattle. Only seven of the 200 he once owned are still around. Some of his cows, he says, were shot dead. The trees did not kill the animals directly; the search for grass is what pushed his herd into that conflict zone. The mathenge tree, scientifically known as Prosopis juliflora (now Neltuma juliflora), is native to the Americas. It spread all over East Africa in the 1970s. It was regarded as a solution to creeping desertification, providingtreecover, soil protection, fuel, and animal feed. The ","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:34:52 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/in-kenya-a-tree-planted-to-halt-desertification-has-reportedly-spread-across-2-million-hectares-experts-say-about-75-of-the-country-is-at-risk-101791452417936.html,2026-10-18 01:50:52
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"In 2018, California began turning 1,187 acres of Delta dairy and grazing land back into tidal marsh to create a safe home for young salmon","In 2018, California began turning 1,187 acres of Delta dairy and grazing land back into tidal marsh to create a safe home for young salmon","In 2018, California began turning 1,187 acres of Delta dairy and grazing land back into tidal marsh to create a safe home for young salmon In 2018, California began turning 1,187 acres of Delta dairy and grazing land into tidal marsh to give young salmon and endangered fish a safe refuge. All young creatures need a safe place to grow up, and baby salmon are no exception. One project in California’s Delta is using the tide to create that habitat. A 2024paperpublished inSan Francisco Estuary and Watershed Sciencenotes that tidal wetland restoration for at-risk fish has gained momentum over the past decade. Dutch Slough, a site near Oakley, is one such effort. In 2018, theCalifornia Department of Water Resources(DWR) and its state and local partners celebrated the groundbreaking of Dutch Slough in Oakley, which DWR described as the Delta's largest tidal wetlands restoration project at the time. That title has since passed to the 3,400-acre Lookout Slough project in Solano County, which DWR said was completed in September 2024. It's a part of California EcoRestore. The effort, involving several agencies, was intended to advance at least 30,000 acres of habitat restoration in the Delta by 2020, and DWR's 2020 summary said it was on track to exceed that goal. At Dutch Slough, 1,187 acres of former dairy and grazing land are being converted to tidal marsh. The goal is to increase survival rates of endangered fish and wildlife. Tidal marshes need land that slopes gently upward. So in May 2018, DWR began to move dirt from higher ground to lower ground. This allows water to flow in from the Delta channels and flow out with the daily tides. Grading of the first two parcels was finished in late 2019, and revegetation followed. The DWR planned to breach the levees in late ","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:23:52 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/in-2018-california-began-turning-1-187-acres-of-delta-dairy-and-grazing-land-back-into-tidal-marsh-to-create-a-safe-home-for-young-salmon-101791371729724.html,2026-10-18 01:50:52
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Earth’s oldest evidence of life is written in stone – but it’s at risk of disappearing,Earth’s oldest evidence of life is written in stone – but it’s at risk of disappearing,"Earth’s oldest evidence of life is written in stone – but it’s at risk of disappearing Earth’s oldest evidence of life is written in stone – but it’s at risk of disappearing Melbourne, Today, our planet is teeming with life, but it has not always looked the same. Long before forests reached skyward, animals walked on land, and the rise and fall of dinosaurs, our planet was shaped by a much smaller, more unassuming form of life. And we can still find traces of it today. These ancient fossils, the earliest visible evidence of life on Earth, are known as microbialites. Western Australia contains an extraordinary record of microbialites: from the oldest known examples, preserved in rocks almost 3.5 billion years old in the Pilbara, to living examples in the UNESCO World Heritage area of Shark Bay. Our review of 50 years of microbialite research in Western Australia, published today in the journal Alcheringa, summarises what this archive can tell us about the evolution and resilience of life on Earth. Despite a fossil record that spans billions of years, microbialites are surprisingly fragile. Fossil sites remain vulnerable to over-collection and degradation, while living microbialites are facing growing pressures. At sites such as Lake Clifton and Shark Bay, some events may have already caused irreparable damage. One such example was in May 2024, when dozens of aurora chasers trampled 2,000-year-old thrombolites in a restricted area at Lake Clifton. Earth's oldest evidence of life Microbialites are mounded structures that come in many shapes and sizes. They form when communities of microbes either precipitate minerals, or trap and bind sediment. Type","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:15:28 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/earths-oldest-evidence-of-life-is-written-in-stone-but-it-s-at-risk-of-disappearing-101792212328536.html,2026-10-18 01:50:52
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"‘Sucking up to Putin’: US politicians slam Trump over ‘craven, cowardly’ Russia sanctions waiver","‘Sucking up to Putin’: US politicians slam Trump over ‘craven, cowardly’ Russia sanctions waiver","‘Sucking up to Putin’: US politicians slam Trump over ‘craven, cowardly’ Russia sanctions waiver Trump's decision to waive the sanctions follows a deal with Putin under which Russia would supply diesel in exchange for relief from US sanctions. Several US lawmakers and politicians have accused PresidentDonald Trumpof “surrendering” to Russian President Vladimir Putin after he signed anexecutive order waiving sweeping sanctions and tariffsaimed at Russia’s energy revenues. Senator Richard Blumenthal, who helped draft the Lindsey Graham Act along with late Senator Lindsey Graham, said Trump was turning his back on ""the majority of Americans, their representatives in both houses of Congress, and our European allies.” “These waivers have nothing to do with our national security, and everything to do with sucking up to Vladimir Putin. I hope my Republican colleagues will join me in condemning this craven, cowardly action,"" he wrote on X. The legislation authorised Trump to impose duties of up to 100 per cent on major buyers of Russian oil and gas. His decision to waive the sanctions follows a deal with Putin under which Russia would supply diesel in exchange for relief from US sanctions. Republican Representative Don Bacon criticised Trump’s decision, saying the US President was “prolonging the war, not ending it”. Democratic Representative Don Beyer called the move Trump’s “latest surrender to Putin”. He said it “defies clear congressional intent of legislation designed to punish Russia for its illegal war of aggression.” “Trump has proven he will twist this law and abuse the powers it conferred on him, and while he has let the tariff powers lie for the moment, the law as written also empowers Trump to weaponize new tra","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:13:17 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/sucking-up-to-putin-us-politicians-slam-trump-over-craven-cowardly-russia-sanctions-waiver-101792209849242.html,2026-10-18 01:50:52
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,‘Very disturbed’: Rubio urges Israel to crack down on West Bank settler attacks,‘Very disturbed’: Rubio urges Israel to crack down on West Bank settler attacks,"‘Very disturbed’: Rubio urges Israel to crack down on West Bank settler attacks US calls for tougher action after settlers attacked Washington Post photojournalist Heidi Levine and Palestinian civilians in the occupied West Bank US calls for tougher action after settlers attacked Washington Post photojournalist Heidi Levine and Palestinian civilians in the occupied West Bank The United States wants Israel to do more to deter attacks committed by settlers in the West Bank, US Secretary of State Marco Rubio said on Saturday. ”We’re very disturbed by those images and what we’re seeing,” Rubio said. ”We’ve asked the Israelis and continue to ask them to crack down on that stuff.” His comments to reporters came after the widespread dissemination of video showing multiple individuals attacking Heidi Levine, an American photojournalist with the Washington Post. Levine was one of at least two people injured in the Friday incident, which took place as Israeli settlers attacked Palestinian civilians. European ‌diplomats and a French mayor also were attacked in the occupied West Bank, Belgium’s foreign minister said. The US State Department, Rubio said, has inquired about the event that included Levine’s attack. Rubio, who spoke to reporters before boarding a flight to Uzbekistan, said he expects to hear back from the US Embassy in Jerusalem by Sunday. Israeli settlers in the West Bank have been involved in at least 170 attacks on Palestinians since September 27, around the start of the annual olive harvest, according to Yesh Din, an Israeli settlements monitoring group. (This is an agency story. Except for the headline, the story has not been edited by Firstpost staff.)","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 06:37:58 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/very-disturbed-rubio-urges-israel-to-crack-down-on-west-bank-settler-attacks-14053602.html,2026-10-18 01:50:52
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"France protests turn violent over school grievances, rising costs; more than 1,200 arrested","France protests turn violent over school grievances, rising costs; more than 1,200 arrested","France protests turn violent over school grievances, rising costs; more than 1,200 arrested Demonstrations over school conditions and student grievances widen into a nationwide movement against inequality, rising prices and deteriorating public services Demonstrations over school conditions and student grievances widen into a nationwide movement against inequality, rising prices and deteriorating public services More than 1,200 people were arrested in France on Saturday after demonstrations over school conditions and student grievances escalated into clashes with police, as anger over rising living costs, inequality and deteriorating public services fuelled nationwide protests. Police fired teargas at demonstrators in several cities as hundreds of thousands of people took to the streets. The protests, initially driven by concerns over teacher shortages, dilapidated school buildings and police violence, drew support from other groups frustrated with high prices and the weakening social welfare system. Hundreds of thousands of people took to the streets across the country, and tensions erupted on the fringes of the nationwide demonstrations over absent teachers, decrepit school buildings and police violence. The Guardian reported that Interior Minister Laurent Nuñez said late on Saturday that 400 people had been arrested, most of them adults who were not part of the high school protests. He said the official demonstration had been hijacked bycasseurs or rioters, “opportunistic delinquents who had seen a chance to pillage and loot … we will find them and deal with them firmly”. Nuñez said the government had no plans to declare a state of emergency but added, ""I very strongly condemn the violence we have seen."" In Marseille, pro","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 06:24:52 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/france-protests-turn-violent-over-school-grievances-rising-costs-more-than-1200-arrested-14053596.html,2026-10-18 01:50:53
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Israeli settlers attack European diplomats, French mayor in West Bank","Israeli settlers attack European diplomats, French mayor in West Bank","Israeli settlers attack European diplomats, French mayor in West Bank Israeli settlers on Saturday attacked a convoy of European diplomats in the West Bank and beat up a visiting French mayor, witnesses said, a day after an American photographer was similarly set upon Israeli settlers on Saturday attacked a convoy of European diplomats in the West Bank and beat up a visiting French mayor, witnesses said, a day after an American photographer was similarly set upon Israeli settlers on Saturday attacked a convoy of European diplomats in the West Bank and beat up a visiting French mayor, witnesses said, a day after an American photographer was similarly set upon as violence escalates ahead of bitterly fought elections. The 78-year-old mayor said masked settlers yelled defamatory comments about France, kicked him and hit him with iron bars as he joined Palestinian olive farmers in the West Bank alongside the top French and Belgian representatives in Jerusalem and Polish diplomats. ""I think that if settlers are behaving like this, there is a tolerance on the part of the Israeli government,"" Bernard Baudoux, mayor of the northern French town of Aulnoye-Aymeries, told AFP after he was attacked. ""The whole world needs to know what's happening,"" he said. ""For me, I'll go back to my country and everything will be fine. Those who continue to suffer the most are those who are here."" Baudoux, who was wearing a sash with the French tricolour, was seen collapsed on the side of the car after the attack but he did not need hospital care. French Foreign Minister Jean-Noel Barrot said in a statement that Israel must ""urgently halt criminal violence"" and arrest those responsible for attacking Baudoux, with his Belgian counterpart issuing a similar call. The at","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 05:37:10 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/israeli-settlers-attack-european-diplomats-french-mayor-in-west-bank-14053598.html,2026-10-18 01:50:53
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,‘Blows kept coming’: Indian Flydubai captain Smit Machchhar recounts fight for survival during mid-air cockpit attack,‘Blows kept coming’: Indian Flydubai captain Smit Machchhar recounts fight for survival during mid-air cockpit attack,"‘Blows kept coming’: Indian Flydubai captain Smit Machchhar recounts fight for survival during mid-air cockpit attack Captain Smit Machchhar says he was repeatedly struck on the head and lost consciousness as the Dubai–Tel Aviv flight plunged, before he managed to open the cockpit door and others helped bring the situation under control. Captain Smit Machchhar says he was repeatedly struck on the head and lost consciousness as the Dubai–Tel Aviv flight plunged, before he managed to open the cockpit door and others helped bring the situation under control. Indian Flydubai captain Smit Machchhar has recounted how he fought to survive an alleged attack by his co-pilot inside the cockpit, describing repeated blows to his head, moments of unconsciousness and the terrifying sensation of the aircraft plunging towards the ground. In an interview with The National from his hospital bed in Abu Dhabi, Machchhar said he initially believed the first blow had been caused by a malfunction inside the aircraft. It was only when the attacks continued that he realised the person striking him was his own co-pilot. “The first time when I was hit … my thought was something's gone horribly wrong with the aircraft structure, which has fallen on my head,” he told The National. He said the blows came in quick succession, leaving him struggling to protect himself. He attempted to free himself from his seat belt and block the strikes, but his vision deteriorated and he suffered serious injuries to his head and arm. Speaking separately toSky News Arabia, Machchhar recalled that the incident began while he was checking flight information on his computer. He said the co-pilot had been praying behind him shortly before the attack. Machchhar chose not to turn around while his colleague prayed, describing the cockpit of the Boeing 737 as a re","Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:18:09 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/blows-kept-coming-indian-flydubai-captain-smit-machchhar-recounts-fight-for-survival-during-mid-air-cockpit-attack-14053593.html,2026-10-18 01:50:53
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Looting, pillaging, attacks on police: Protests turn violent across France as tens of thousands take to streets","Looting, pillaging, attacks on police: Protests turn violent across France as tens of thousands take to streets","Looting, pillaging, attacks on police: Protests turn violent across France as tens of thousands take to streets Police and protesters clashed violently in Paris and other French cities Saturday on the sidelines of nationwide marches against soaring inflation, with one demonstrator's hand blown off, multiple blazes set and looters breaking into dozens of stores to strip them bare. Police and protesters clashed violently in Paris and other French cities Saturday on the sidelines of nationwide marches against soaring inflation, with one demonstrator's hand blown off, multiple blazes set and looters breaking into dozens of stores to strip them bare. Tens of thousands of protesters took to the streets across France on Saturday to demonstrate against the rising cost of living, with rallies turning violent in several cities. Demonstrators clashed with police, looted shops, set fires and stormed public buildings, while authorities reported more than 400 arrests nationwide. The widespread unrest follows weeks of student protests and deepens political uncertainty in France as it readies for elections in six months to replace President Emmanuel Macron. The demonstrations, which brought together students, trade unionists and supporters of the Yellow Vest movement, were marked by violence in several cities including Paris, Lyon, Marseille and Lille. Police used tear gas and water cannons against masked protesters in Paris and other cities who were throwing projectiles and aiming fireworks. Several parked cars were torched in the capital. A protester in the southern city of Saint-Étienne had a hand blown off by a police crowd-control grenade, a police source toldAFP. The source did not specify whether the device was a stun grenade or a tear gas grenade. The incident was the second such case in less than two weeks. On October 5, a 15-year-old student lost his left h","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 23:31:24 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/looting-pillaging-attacks-on-police-protests-turn-violent-across-france-as-tens-of-thousands-take-to-streets-14053582.html,2026-10-18 01:50:53
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,US midterms: Republicans turn to Trump for voter turnout but keep his name out of their ads,US midterms: Republicans turn to Trump for voter turnout but keep his name out of their ads,"US midterms: Republicans turn to Trump for voter turnout but keep his name out of their ads Trump's name is missing from several Republican campaign advertisements, including in states where candidates had prominently displayed his endorsement during the 2024 elections Trump's name is missing from several Republican campaign advertisements, including in states where candidates had prominently displayed his endorsement during the 2024 elections US President Donald Trump is travelling across the country to rally support for Republicans ahead of the November midterm elections. But while he wants to help his party retain control of Congress, many Republican candidates are reluctant to make him the face of their campaigns. According to aPoliticoreport, Trump's name is missing from several Republican campaign advertisements, including in states where candidates had prominently displayed his endorsement during the 2024 elections. Some are also weighing whether to appear alongside him at rallies, asconcerns grow over his falling popularity, the Iran war and the cost of living. The gap between the two parties is clear in their advertising spending. Democratic House and Senate candidates, along with outside groups, have spent $145 million on general election advertisements mentioning Trump, compared with just $8 million by Republicans, according to aPoliticoanalysis ofAdImpactdata. In 2024, Republicans spent $62 million on ads mentioning the president. The change is visible even in Republican strongholds such as Nebraska and Ohio, where candidates had used Trump's backing to their advantage in the last election. In 2024, Republican Senator Deb Fischer in Nebraska and Bernie Moreno in Ohio featured Trump's endorsements in several campaign advertisemen","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 22:01:37 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/united-states/us-midterms-republicans-turn-to-trump-for-voter-turnout-but-keep-his-name-out-of-their-ads-14053581.html,2026-10-18 01:50:53
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Ukraine faces grim question: Can it fight Russia without US backing as Trump waives Russia sanctions?,Ukraine faces grim question: Can it fight Russia without US backing as Trump waives Russia sanctions?,"Ukraine faces grim question: Can it fight Russia without US backing as Trump waives Russia sanctions? Zelenskyy warns that Washington’s decision to ease Russia-related sanctions rewards Putin, as disruptions to critical satellite services and strained ties with Trump raise fresh concerns over Ukraine’s ability to sustain its war effort. Zelenskyy warns that Washington’s decision to ease Russia-related sanctions rewards Putin, as disruptions to critical satellite services and strained ties with Trump raise fresh concerns over Ukraine’s ability to sustain its war effort. Ukraine is facing growing uncertainty over whether it can sustain its war against Russia without US backing, as President Donald Trump’s decision to waive key Russia-related sanctions and tensions over American satellite support deepen the rift between Washington and Kyiv. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy has warned that easing pressure on Moscow could encourage Russian President Vladimir Putin to continue the war, while expressing concern that the Trump administration could lift further sanctions on Russia. ""A US sanctions blackout, easing pressure on the aggressor,financial rewards for Putin-- all of this only encourages him to keep attacking,"" Zelenskyy said on social media, calling on allies to keep pressuring Russia. The developments come as Kyiv confronts disruptions to critical satellite services, questions over intelligence support and a renewed push to secure financial assistance from European allies. The widening rift has sparked concerns that Trump and Zelenskyy’s tumultuous relationship could lead to even worse outcomes for Kyiv. Trump could yet restrict the supply of weapons to Ukraine, follow through on threats to stop sharing battlefield intelligence, or strike more economic deals with Moscow. The recent disruption to US ","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 20:38:25 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/ukraine-faces-grim-question-can-it-fight-russia-without-us-backing-as-trump-waives-russia-sanctions-14053576.html,2026-10-18 01:50:53
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,‘You can’t die like this’: Flydubai pilot Smit Machchhar recounts cockpit attack in new interview,‘You can’t die like this’: Flydubai pilot Smit Machchhar recounts cockpit attack in new interview,"‘You can’t die like this’: Flydubai pilot Smit Machchhar recounts cockpit attack in new interview The latest clips offer another glimpse into the frightening incident, in which Machchhar was seriously injured but managed to regain access to the cockpit door and help others respond to the emergency. The latest clips offer another glimpse into the frightening incident, in which Machchhar was seriously injured but managed to regain access to the cockpit door and help others respond to the emergency. “I realise I have to do something. You can’t die like this. I can’t accept my death.” These are among the chilling words of Captain Smit Machchhar as he recalls the cockpit attack that nearly cost him his life while flying a Flydubai passenger aircraft. Promos of two interviews featuring Captain Machchhar have been released by UAE-based media outletsSky News ArabiaandThe National. In the clips, the pilot describes the moments when he was struck on the head, struggled to remain conscious and realised he had to fight to survive. Recalling theordeal, Machchhar described his response as a survival instinct that kicked in for both him and the passengers. “That was like a miracle,” he said in the UAE media interview. The latest clips offer another glimpse into thefrightening incident, in which Machchhar was seriously injured but managed to regain access to the cockpit door and help others respond to the emergency. In theSky News Arabiainterview, Machchhar recalled how the other pilot had wanted to pray. He said he turned his attention away out of respect while his colleague was praying. “I was doing my job, there was a hard hit on my head,” he said, describing the sudden attack. He recalled feeling as though something very powerful had struck him and said he was in and out of conscio","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 19:07:05 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/you-cant-die-like-this-flydubai-pilot-smit-machchhar-recounts-cockpit-attack-in-new-interview-14053575.html,2026-10-18 01:50:53
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,‘Circular firing squad’: Trump’s midterm strategy falters as aides blame each other,‘Circular firing squad’: Trump’s midterm strategy falters as aides blame each other,"‘Circular firing squad’: Trump’s midterm strategy falters as aides blame each other Trump is questioning campaign spending, redistricting and candidate choices as Republicans face the prospect of losing the House, with tensions mounting between key figures in his political operation. Trump is questioning campaign spending, redistricting and candidate choices as Republicans face the prospect of losing the House, with tensions mounting between key figures in his political operation. US President Donald Trump is turning his frustration over Republicans’ faltering midterm strategy towards his own aides, questioning campaign expenditure, redistricting decisions and candidate choices as his party fights to retain control of Congress,Reutersreported, citing three people familiar with internal discussions. The November 3 elections will determine whether Republicans retain their majorities in Congress, with opinion polls suggesting Democrats are positioned to win the House of Representatives while the Senate remains too close to call. A Republican defeat in the House would complicate Trump’s legislative agenda during the final two years of his presidency and give Democrats subpoena powers to investigate his administration. Although Trump is not on the ballot, he has repeatedly urged supporters to treat the election as if he were a candidate. He has embarked on an intensive campaign schedule but has largely avoided acknowledging his own role in shaping the strategy or the impact of his declining popularity on Republicans in competitive races. Much of the internal friction has centred on James Blair, the long-time aide who runs Trump’s super PAC MAGA Inc., and White House chief of staff Susie Wiles, who had largely escaped criticism until now. Much of the friction h","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 18:03:06 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/united-states/circular-firing-squad-trumps-midterm-strategy-falters-as-aides-blame-each-other-14053557.html,2026-10-18 01:50:53
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Zelenskyy says Trump’s Russia sanctions waiver ‘rewards’ Putin as Moscow targets Ukraine’s energy grid,Zelenskyy says Trump’s Russia sanctions waiver ‘rewards’ Putin as Moscow targets Ukraine’s energy grid,"Zelenskyy says Trump’s Russia sanctions waiver ‘rewards’ Putin as Moscow targets Ukraine’s energy grid Zelenskyy urged supporters Saturday to speak out in defence of new US sanctions against Russia, hours after President Donald Trump signed an executive order pausing a sweeping package of restrictions targeting the Russian economy. Zelenskyy urged supporters Saturday to speak out in defence of new US sanctions against Russia, hours after President Donald Trump signed an executive order pausing a sweeping package of restrictions targeting the Russian economy. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy has accused US President Donald Trump of rewarding Russian President Vladimir Putin by waiving new sanctions targeting Moscow’s energy revenues, warning that easing economic pressure could encourage further attacks on Ukraine. Zelenskyy’s criticism came as Russia intensified strikes on Ukraine’s energy infrastructure ahead of winter. He said Moscow had attacked 15 critical power-generation facilities in one of the largest assaults on the country’s energy grid. “A US sanctions blackout, easing pressure on the aggressor, financial rewards for Putin -- all of this only encourages him to keep attacking,” Zelenskyy said in a social media post on Saturday, urging Ukraine’s allies to maintain pressure on Russia. Trump signed an executive order on Friday pausing a sweeping package of sanctions targeting the Russian economy, including restrictions aimed at Moscow’s energy revenues. The decision has raised concerns in Kyiv and Europe over Washington’s approach to the war as Russian attacks continue. Zelenskyy called on supporters in the United States to speak out in favour of the proposed restrictions, arguing that the Lindsey Graham Act could have helped pressure Moscow to end the conflict. “A decision was unfortunatel","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 17:43:19 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/zelenskyy-says-trumps-russia-sanctions-waiver-rewards-putin-as-moscow-targets-ukraines-energy-grid-14053558.html,2026-10-18 01:50:54
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,‘Fake govt of powerless rulers’: Pakistan opposition leader warns Shehbaz Sharif govt over military’s growing power,‘Fake govt of powerless rulers’: Pakistan opposition leader warns Shehbaz Sharif govt over military’s growing power,"‘Fake govt of powerless rulers’: Pakistan opposition leader warns Shehbaz Sharif govt over military’s growing power Lahore: Pakistan’s opposition leader and Jamiat Ulema-e-Islam-F chief Maulana Fazlur Rehman has warned against the growing concentration of political and institutional power in the military establishment, saying an imbalance among state institutions could threaten the country’s stability. Lahore: Pakistan’s opposition leader and Jamiat Ulema-e-Islam-F chief Maulana Fazlur Rehman has warned against the growing concentration of political and institutional power in the military establishment, saying an imbalance among state institutions could threaten the country’s stability. Pakistan opposition leader and Jamiat Ulema-e-Islam-F (JUI-F) chief Maulana Fazlur Rehman has branded Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif’s government a “fake government” of “powerless rulers”, accusing powerful forces of undermining parliamentary politics and democracy. Addressing a public gathering in Punjab’s Multan district on Friday, Rehman warned that the growing concentration of authority in the military establishment could threaten Pakistan’s stability, arguing that state institutions must operate within their constitutional limits. Rehman also accused Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif’s government of lacking an electoral mandate, calling it a “fake government” of “powerless rulers”. “We have chosen the path of Parliament, and today the people of Pakistan and the world can see which force is sabotaging parliamentary politics, democracy and politics rooted in the people. This is no longer hidden,” he said. “The powerful forces that are taking more and more authority onto their shoulders will soon reach a point where they themselves will be crushed under the weight of that power,” he said, in an apparent reference to the military establishment. Rehman said Pakistan could function effectively onl","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 17:40:03 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/fake-govt-of-powerless-rulers-pakistan-opposition-leader-warns-shehbaz-sharif-govt-over-militarys-growing-power-14053559.html,2026-10-18 01:50:54
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,'Historic moment': Russia welcomes Trump's sanction waiver on its energy exports,'Historic moment': Russia welcomes Trump's sanction waiver on its energy exports,"'Historic moment': Russia welcomes Trump's sanction waiver on its energy exports Russia's presidential envoy Kirill Dmitriev said on Saturday that US President Donald Trump's decision to waive some sanctions and tariffs on Moscow's energy exports is a ""historic moment"". Russia's presidential envoy Kirill Dmitriev said on Saturday that US President Donald Trump's decision to waive some sanctions and tariffs on Moscow's energy exports is a ""historic moment"". Russia's presidential envoy Kirill Dmitriev said on Saturday that US President Donald Trump's decision to waive some sanctions and tariffs on Moscow's energy exports is a ""historic moment"". The remarks from the Russian envoy came just hours after Trump announced an exception to the Lindsey O. Graham Act that aimed to impose punitive measures against Russia and Iran. The move came amid a clash with Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy that has fuelled speculation that the US leader is increasingly siding with Moscow nearly five years into Russia's offensive against Ukraine, which has killed hundreds of thousands of people and displaced millions. ""The Trump administration's suspension of the Graham Act is an important and truly historic moment,"" Kirill Dmitriev, envoy for Russian President Vladimir Putin, said on social media. The Lindsey Graham Act -- named after the deceased US senator who sponsored the bill -- targeted Russia's energy and defence sectors as well as Putin and other senior officials. In the executive order released late Friday, Trump said that the waiver is being imposed keeping the ""national interest"" in mind. ""I am hereby waiving the application of such sanctions, restrictions, and duties provided for in the Graham Act. I have submitted to Congress a certification that","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:57:04 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/historic-moment-russia-welcomes-trumps-sanction-waiver-on-its-energy-exports-14053542.html,2026-10-18 01:50:54
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,‘Verify before you vilify’: India rebukes UN Rapporteur over claims on youth rights and protests,‘Verify before you vilify’: India rebukes UN Rapporteur over claims on youth rights and protests,"‘Verify before you vilify’: India rebukes UN Rapporteur over claims on youth rights and protests India has rejected a report by UN Special Rapporteur Gina Romero, questioning its claims that young people in the country face retaliation or stigma for speaking about mental health and gender justice India has rejected a report by UN Special Rapporteur Gina Romero, questioning its claims that young people in the country face retaliation or stigma for speaking about mental health and gender justice India has rejected a report by UN Special Rapporteur Gina Romero, questioning its claims that young people in the country face retaliation or stigma for speaking about mental health and gender justice. New Delhi said the allegations relied on anonymous and unverified submissions, calling the report an example of arbitrary reporting that has weakened the credibility of the United Nations and its institutions. Speaking at the 81st session of the United Nations General Assembly (UNGA) Third Committee, India's First Secretary Anuj Swarup strongly criticised the report during an interactive dialogue with Romero, who oversees the mandate on the rights to freedom of peaceful assembly and association. The rebuttal comes amid youth-led protests in India against Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar. Swarup argued that the report had made sweeping claims about India's young population without providing verifiable evidence to support them. “A sweeping charge against the world's largest youth population resting on anonymous and unverified claims is not evidence. It is hearsay, dressed up as a UN report, and my delegation rejects it outright,” he said. He also questioned the report's assertion that young Indians faced retaliation or stigmatisation for discussing mental health and gender ","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:49:29 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/verify-before-you-vilify-india-rebukes-un-rapporteur-over-claims-on-youth-rights-and-protests-14053539.html,2026-10-18 01:50:54
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Magnitude 5.8 earthquake strikes Pakistan,Magnitude 5.8 earthquake strikes Pakistan,"Magnitude 5.8 earthquake strikes Pakistan The earthquake struck at a shallow depth of 15 kilometres, with the National Centre for Seismology reporting the tremor at 10.37 am IST on Saturday The earthquake struck at a shallow depth of 15 kilometres, with the National Centre for Seismology reporting the tremor at 10.37 am IST on Saturday An earthquake of magnitude 5.8 struck Balochistan, Pakistan, on Saturday, according to the National Centre for Seismology (NCS). The quake occurred at 10:37 am IST at a shallow depth of 15 kilometres, the NCS said. “EQ of M: 5.8, On: 17/10/2026 10:37:00 IST, Lat: 27.944 N, Long: 66.532 E, Depth: 15 Km, Location: Pakistan,” NCS posted on X. Pakistan is considered one of the most earthquake-prone countries in the world due to its location along the boundary where the Indian and Eurasian tectonic plates collide. Regions such as Balochistan, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa and Gilgit-Baltistan lie near major fault lines, making them particularly vulnerable to seismic activity, while Punjab and Sindh, located along the northwestern edge of the Indian Plate, also remain vulnerable.","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 14:35:53 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/magnitude-5-8-earthquake-strikes-pakistans-14053537.html,2026-10-18 01:50:54
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"‘When in doubt, say Mohammad’: Trump jibes at Iran, says ‘nobody wants to be its president’","‘When in doubt, say Mohammad’: Trump jibes at Iran, says ‘nobody wants to be its president’","‘When in doubt, say Mohammad’: Trump jibes at Iran, says ‘nobody wants to be its president’ The US president targeted Iran’s leadership while reiterating his vow to prevent Tehran from acquiring nuclear weapons The US president targeted Iran’s leadership while reiterating his vow to prevent Tehran from acquiring nuclear weapons ""You know, if you're in doubt about somebody's name from the Middle East, just say 'Mohammad,'"" Trump told a crowd in Dalton, Georgia during a rally. Reuters quoted Trump saying, ""You have about a 65 per cent chance. 'Hello, Mohammad.' I've done it for years now. I've never been wrong."" Mohammad is among the most common male names in the Muslim world, with many Muslim families naming their sons after the Prophet Mohammad, the founder of Islam. Trump made the remarks while discussing Iran at a rally held in a hangar at Dalton Municipal Airport. Mocking the Iranian presidency, he claimed that the position was one nobody wanted. Muslim advocacy groups have repeatedly criticised Trump’s rhetoric, accusing him of mocking Muslims, immigrants and ethnic minorities.","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 12:55:05 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/united-states/when-in-doubt-say-mohammad-trump-jibes-at-iran-says-nobody-wants-to-be-its-president-14053526.html,2026-10-18 01:50:54
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,US Rep Pramila Jayapal's office receives suspicious package; staff safe,US Rep Pramila Jayapal's office receives suspicious package; staff safe,"US Rep Pramila Jayapal's office receives suspicious package; staff safe The Washington Democrat said her office was following protocol after receiving a package similar to those sent to lawmakers and election officials nationwide. The Washington Democrat said her office was following protocol after receiving a package similar to those sent to lawmakers and election officials nationwide. With just over a week left for the US midterm elections, US Representative Pramila Jayapal on Friday said her office had received a suspicious package similar to those received by Members of Congress and elections officials across the country. In a statement, Jayapal said her staff and she were safe and that protocol was being followed. “My office today received a suspicious package, similar to that received by Members of Congress and elections officials across the country. We are following protocol, and at the time being, my staff and I are safe. I will continue to provide updates as available', Jayapal said. Calling the incidents a threat to democratic institutions, she added, “This seemingly deliberate and widespread attack on our democracy and elections is unacceptable.” Elected in 2016, Congresswoman Pramila Jayapal, 61, is now serving her fifth term in Congress representing Washington’s 7th District. She is the first South Asian American woman elected to the US House of Representatives and one of only two dozen naturalized citizens currently serving in the United States Congress. Jayapal is seeking a sixth term in the US House of Representatives from Washington state’s 7th Congressional District in the 3 November midterm elections. This time, Jayapal is facing Republican challenger Nirav Sheth, an Indian-American ","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:48:29 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/indian-diaspora/us-rep-pramila-jayapals-office-receives-suspicious-package-staff-safe-14053523.html,2026-10-18 01:50:54
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,‘US must leave region for Strait of Hormuz to reopen’: Iranian Army spokesperson,‘US must leave region for Strait of Hormuz to reopen’: Iranian Army spokesperson,"‘US must leave region for Strait of Hormuz to reopen’: Iranian Army spokesperson Tehran warns it will expel US forces if Washington refuses to withdraw, while Pezeshkian denies seeking nuclear weapons. Tehran warns it will expel US forces if Washington refuses to withdraw, while Pezeshkian denies seeking nuclear weapons. Iran has said the United States must leave the region for the Strait of Hormuz to reopen, as the conflict enters its eighth month, with President Masoud Pezeshkian denying that Tehran is seeking nuclear weapons and expressing readiness for dialogue. Iranian Army spokesperson Mohammad Akraminia said Friday that regional countries could establish collective security and help improve the global economy if Washington withdrew its forces. ""If America leaves the region, the Strait of Hormuz will open, collective security will be established with the participation of regional states, and the global economy will improve. And if it does not leave, we will expel it,"" Akraminia said on X. Pezeshkian denied that Tehran was seeking nuclear weapons, expressed readiness for dialogue and condemned US sanctions during talks with Georgian Prime Minister Irakli Kobakhidze. The two leaders met on the sidelines of a United Nations biodiversity summit in Yerevan, the capital of Armenia. ""Iran is not seeking nuclear weapons, and we consider the malicious propaganda against our country to be lies,"" Pezeshkian said, according to a statement from his office. ""We have always been ready for dialogue. They think they can force us to surrender, but we will not bow to coercion,"" he added, calling US sanctions against Tehran ""unjustifiable and inhumane."" Pezeshkian also called for closer ","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 09:31:19 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/us-must-leave-region-for-strait-of-hormuz-to-reopen-iranian-army-spokesperson-14053494.html,2026-10-18 01:50:54
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,No tariff on India for buying Russian oil as Trump waives sanctions under Lindsey Graham law,No tariff on India for buying Russian oil as Trump waives sanctions under Lindsey Graham law,"No tariff on India for buying Russian oil as Trump waives sanctions under Lindsey Graham law In a major relief to India, US President Donald Trump has waived many sanctions and tariffs related to Russian energy exports under the Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026 In a major relief to India, US President Donald Trump has waived many sanctions and tariffs related to Russian energy exports under the Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026 In a major relief to India and China, US President Donald Trump has waived many sanctions and tariffs related to Russian energy exports under the Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026, which he signed in September. In the executive order released late Friday, Trump said that the waiver is being imposed keeping the ""national interest"" in mind. ""I am hereby waiving the application of such sanctions, restrictions, and duties provided for in the Graham Act. I have submitted to Congress a certification that the issuance of such a waiver at this time is in the national interest of the United States,"" Trump said in anexecutive orderreleased late Friday. It is pertinent to note that the directive only suspends implementation of the Russia-related tariffs, meaning Trump could apply them later if he chooses. It is pertinent to note that the law was championed by Senator Lindsey O. Graham as a way to bring punitive measures against Russia for its invasion of Ukraine. The legislation proposed tariffs of up to 100 per cent on goods from the top five purchasers of Russian oil and gas – which include China and India – within 30 days after its enactment. However, the law did not automatically impose tariffs, leaving the decision of imposing them, their rate and timing to the Presid","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 09:01:12 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/trump-waives-sanctions-tariffs-india-russia-oil-gas-purchase-china-iran-sanction-act-lindsey-graham-14053500.html,2026-10-18 01:50:54
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,UK police to investigate national security adviser's wife over post supporting Palestine Action,UK police to investigate national security adviser's wife over post supporting Palestine Action,"UK police to investigate national security adviser's wife over post supporting Palestine Action The British police on Friday said that they are assessing the social media posts after the wife of National Security Adviser Jonathan Powell appeared to express support for Palestine Action, a pro-Palestinian rights group banned as a terrorist organisation in the country The British police on Friday said that they are assessing the social media posts after the wife of National Security Adviser Jonathan Powell appeared to express support for Palestine Action, a pro-Palestinian rights group banned as a terrorist organisation in the country The British police on Friday said that they are assessing the social media posts after the wife of National Security Adviser Jonathan Powell appeared to express support for Palestine Action, a pro-Palestinian rights group banned as a terrorist organisation in the country. Journalist and author Sarah Helm, who is married to Powell, caught media attention after she responded on X/Twitter to a post about the Irish novelist Sally Rooney. This week, Rooney announced that her books would no longer be sold in the UK since she plans to use her royalties to support the Palestine Action group. Helm responded to the news and wrote in her post, ""God help us authors who support Palestine and Palestine Action."" In response to the post, a Metropolitan Police spokesperson said the post is ""being assessed to determine whether any further police investigation may be required"". Helm has been facing scrutiny since Palestine Action was proscribed under terrorism laws last year. Hence, expressing support for the group constitutes a criminal offence in the country. When asked about the comment on the matter, a government spokeswoman said: ""These views are held by an individual who does not represent the government in any capacity.""","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 08:07:34 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/uk-police-to-investigate-national-security-advisers-wife-over-post-supporting-palestine-action-14053496.html,2026-10-18 01:50:54
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Trump to campaign in Michigan next week before election,Trump to campaign in Michigan next week before election,"Trump to campaign in Michigan next week before election Trump’s October 25 visit comes amid a closely watched Senate race, with Obama and Vance also campaigning in Michigan ahead of the November 3 midterms. Trump’s October 25 visit comes amid a closely watched Senate race, with Obama and Vance also campaigning in Michigan ahead of the November 3 midterms. US President Donald Trump will campaign in Michigan on October 25, adding the state to a packed midterm election tour as Republicans seek to strengthen their position in closely watched Senate, gubernatorial and House races ahead of the November 3 election. The Republican National Committee (RNC) announced on Friday that Trump would headline a rally in St. Clair County, Michigan, following an appearance in Macon, Georgia, on October 24. The event will mark his seventh campaign stop in seven days. The rally is scheduled for Sunday, October 25, with doors opening at 11.30 am and Trump expected to take the stage at 3 pm. The organisers have yet to announce the exact venue. The Trump 47 Committee and the RNC announced the event on October 16. Trump's planned visit comes amid growing national attention on Michigan's Senate race between Democratic candidate Abdul El-Sayed and Republican nominee Mike Rogers, a former congressman. Vice President JD Vance campaigned in Michigan on Thursday alongside Rogers, urging voters to back Republican candidates ahead of the midterm elections. Vance described the Senate contest as “the single most important race of the 2026 midterms”. During his visit, Vance also campaigned for Republicans contesting other key races, including Michigan's 7th Congressional District. The Senate contest has drawn prom","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 06:47:51 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/trump-to-campaign-in-michigan-next-week-before-election-14053489.html,2026-10-18 01:50:55
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Trump claims neither he nor Musk caused Starlink disruption in Ukraine,Trump claims neither he nor Musk caused Starlink disruption in Ukraine,"Trump claims neither he nor Musk caused Starlink disruption in Ukraine US President Donald Trump claimed on Friday that neither he nor SpaceX CEO Elon Musk caused Ukraine's Starlink outage earlier this week. US President Donald Trump claimed on Friday that neither he nor SpaceX CEO Elon Musk caused Ukraine's Starlink outage earlier this week. US President Donald Trump claimed on Friday that neither he nor SpaceX CEO Elon Musk caused Ukraine's Starlink outage earlier this week. The remarks from the American leader came at a time when he is sparring with Ukraine's President Volodymyr Zelenskyy over resumed strikes on Russian oil refineries despite the energy ceasefire. “I didn’t do that. No, we didn’t do that at all. In fact, I don’t even think it was slowed down from what I understood. Because I just checked this morning,” Trump told reporters Friday while on his way to a campaign rally in Georgia. “I heard about it from the news, the media. And we didn’t slow it down at all. And I think it’s probably functioning, from what I understand,” he continued. When asked by a reporter if trillionaire Musk has any involvement in the matter, Trump said: ""I don’t know if there’s anybody because it wasn’t me, and it wasn’t Elon, as I understand it.” A White House official made similar remarks to NewNation, maintaining that ""no such request” was ever made by Trump. Ukraine first reported the outage on Wednesday, saying its military lost access to the military version of Starlink, which it uses for vital communications amid the ongoing war against Russia. Starlink services were cut off for objects travelling more than 100 mph, affecting Ukraine’s drones, aeroplanes and helicopters, a source fam","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 06:18:12 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/trump-claims-neither-he-nor-musk-caused-starlink-disruption-in-ukraine-14053487.html,2026-10-18 01:50:55
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"24 Indian seafarers safe after tanker incidents in Strait of Hormuz, Romania","24 Indian seafarers safe after tanker incidents in Strait of Hormuz, Romania","24 Indian seafarers safe after tanker incidents in Strait of Hormuz, Romania Union Minister Sonowal says all Indians on MT ST 3 and YARA J safe after projectile, drone strikes Union Minister Sonowal says all Indians on MT ST 3 and YARA J safe after projectile, drone strikes All 24 Indian seafarers aboard two vessels involved in separate incidents in the Strait of Hormuz and Romania are safe, Union Minister for Ports, Shipping and Waterways Sarbananda Sonowal said on Friday. In a post on X, Sonowal said MT ST 3, carrying 15 Indians, was struck by a projectile in the Strait of Hormuz on October 16, while YARA J, carrying nine Indians, was hit by a drone off Romania on October 14. ""On Oct 16, crude oil tanker MT ST 3, carrying 15 Indian seafarers, was struck by a projectile in the Strait of Hormuz; additionally, an incident on Oct 14 involved chemical tanker YARA J, carrying 9 Indian seafarers, hit by a drone at Sulina Anchorage, Romania,"" he wrote on X. The minister added that authorities were continuously monitoring both situation and remained in close contact with the ship management companies to ensure the safety and well-being of the Indian crew members. ""Thankfully, all 24 Indian nationals across both vessels are completely safe with no casualties or injuries. We are continuously monitoring both situations and remain in close contact with the ship management companies to ensure the ongoing security and wellbeing of our crew members,"" he added. The latest incidents follow a series of attacks on commercial vessels carrying Indian seafarers in the Gulf region. On October 11, the Nicaragua-flagged tanker MT Petro Dolphin, carrying 11 Indian seafarer","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 06:03:29 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/indian-diaspora/24-indian-seafarers-safe-after-tanker-incidents-in-strait-of-hormuz-romania-14053483.html,2026-10-18 01:50:55
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Iran rejects Saudi Arabia's accusation, denies involvement in Houthi attacks","Iran rejects Saudi Arabia's accusation, denies involvement in Houthi attacks","Iran rejects Saudi Arabia's accusation, denies involvement in Houthi attacks Iran on Friday denied having anything to do with attacks by Yemen's Houthis, a day after Saudi Arabia accused it of having encouraged the group's recent escalation against the kingdom. Iran on Friday denied having anything to do with attacks by Yemen's Houthis, a day after Saudi Arabia accused it of having encouraged the group's recent escalation against the kingdom. Iran on Friday denied having anything to do with attacks by Yemen's Houthis, a day after Saudi Arabia accused it of having encouraged the group's recent escalation against the kingdom. Since Yemen's civil war flared back to life in July, the pro-Iran Houthis have repeatedly struck Saudi targets including Red Sea tankers, oil facilities and Riyadh's international airport, where 13 people were killed in a single attack last week. A Saudi-led military coalition has supported Yemen's internationally recognised government for more than a decade, and reported on Friday having intercepted more missiles and drones as the kingdom comes under sustained attack from the Houthis. A day earlier, a Saudi foreign ministry official said Iran had ""encouraged"" the escalation, which Tehran denied. ""The Islamic Republic of Iran rejects any claim that Iran has played a role in Yemeni (Houthi) operations,"" Iranian foreign ministry spokesman Esmaeil Baqaei said in a statement. ""Yemen's armed forces act according to their own interests and assessments, and such claims are not credible to those familiar with relations between Iran and its friends. Nevertheless, Iran does not consider an escalation of tensions to be in the interests of any party,"" he added. The Saudi official, deputy minister for public diplomacy Dr. Ray","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 05:27:51 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/iran-rejects-saudi-arabias-accusation-denies-involvement-in-houthi-attacks-14053485.html,2026-10-18 01:50:55
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"US India Caucus urges FBI, DOJ to probe Illinois Hindu temple attack plot as federal hate crime","US India Caucus urges FBI, DOJ to probe Illinois Hindu temple attack plot as federal hate crime","US India Caucus urges FBI, DOJ to probe Illinois Hindu temple attack plot as federal hate crime The lawmakers asked the FBI and the Department of Justice to investigate the failed plot as a potential federal hate crime and bring all charges supported by the evidence. The lawmakers asked the FBI and the Department of Justice to investigate the failed plot as a potential federal hate crime and bring all charges supported by the evidence. US Congressional Caucus on India on Friday demanded that the FBI and the Department of Justice thoroughly investigate the alleged plot to carry out a mass shooting at a Hindu temple in Illinois as a federal hate crime. In a letter to Attorney General Todd Blanche and FBI Director Kash Patel, the House India Caucus demanded that the probe should determine whether anyone encouraged, assisted, or coordinated with the 19-year-old attacker, online or offline. “This plot did not emerge in isolation but reflects broader, troubling trends,” Congressmen Rich McCormick and Ro Khanna, co-chairs of the House India Caucus, said in the letter. The lawmakers asked the FBI and the Department of Justice to investigate the failed plot as a potential federal hate crime and bring all charges supported by the evidence. They also asked the FBI and the Justice Department to “share timely threat information with state and local law enforcement and with Hindu temples, Sikh gurdwaras, and other South Asian houses of worship, and offer them protective resources. The Congressmen also asked Blanche and Patel to brief the Caucus on threats facing Hindu Americans and Indian Americans and the steps the Department and FBI are taking to address them. The letter said that the FBI data showed an increase in reported hate crimes targeting Sikh","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 01:50:40 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/us-india-caucus-urges-fbi-doj-to-probe-illinois-hindu-temple-attack-plot-as-federal-hate-crime-14053480.html,2026-10-18 01:50:55
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Trump fires Katie Zacharia as White House press secretary after turbulent week,Trump fires Katie Zacharia as White House press secretary after turbulent week,"Trump fires Katie Zacharia as White House press secretary after turbulent week Zacharia’s brief tenure ended after a shaky debut in the briefing room and scrutiny over her background, with Trump telling her, “Maybe it’s not for her” Zacharia’s brief tenure ended after a shaky debut in the briefing room and scrutiny over her background, with Trump telling her, “Maybe it’s not for her” US President Donald Trump has fired Katie Zacharia as White House press secretary after just five days in the role, following a rocky debut in the briefing room and mounting controversy over her claims to be a lawyer, according to aCNNreport, citing advisers to the president. Zacharia took over from Karoline Leavitt, who had helped Trump reshape White House media coverage by giving greater access to friendly outlets while frequently attacking critical media organisations. Her first briefing on Tuesday lasted less than 15 minutes. She began by slowly reading out what she described as Trump’s many accomplishments before taking just three questions from journalists. The brief appearance was followed by questions about her professional credentials after it emerged that she had described herself as a lawyer despite not passing the bar examination required to practise law in the United States. Trump had indicated earlier on Friday that Zacharia should reconsider her position, saying he had discussed the matter with her that morning and would leave the final decision to her. “Honestly, if I were you, I’d reconsider,”CNNquoted Trump as telling Zacharia on Friday morning. The president said they would speak again after he returned to the White House, but his remarks suggested he was not keen on retaining her as the administration’s c","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:32:52 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/united-states/trump-fires-katie-zacharia-as-white-house-press-secretary-after-turbulent-week-14053472.html,2026-10-18 01:50:55
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,US lawmaker introduces bipartisan bill to block Trump’s Russia diesel deal,US lawmaker introduces bipartisan bill to block Trump’s Russia diesel deal,"US lawmaker introduces bipartisan bill to block Trump’s Russia diesel deal Gregory W. Meeks introduced bipartisan legislation seeking to restrict Russian energy financing and impose sanctions, arguing the deal would fund Putin’s war in Ukraine without lowering costs for Americans Gregory W. Meeks introduced bipartisan legislation seeking to restrict Russian energy financing and impose sanctions, arguing the deal would fund Putin’s war in Ukraine without lowering costs for Americans US Representative Gregory W. Meeks on Friday introduced bipartisan legislation seeking to block the Trump administration from purchasing and importing Russian diesel, arguing that the arrangement would provide financial support to Russian President Vladimir Putin amid the war in Ukraine. The proposed legislation, titled the Prohibit Corrupt Russia Oil Deals Act, seeks to restrict US government financing for Russian energy projects and invalidate certain licences issued by the Treasury Department’s Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC). It would also prohibit future licences and require the Trump administration to impose sanctions on Russian energy, according to a statement released by Meeks. Criticising the proposed arrangement, Meeks argued that the deal would benefit Moscow without delivering meaningful savings for American consumers. ""This deal won’t lower costs for the American people, who are already paying for Trump’s failed war of choice in Iran. It will hand Putin millions of dollars to continue slaughtering Ukrainian civilians with impunity,"" Meeks said. Meeks also accused US President Donald Trump of easing pressure on the Kremlin by pursuing an agreement with Putin instead of backing Ukraine. ""At a time when the United States should be increasing pressure on th","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 23:47:07 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/us-lawmaker-introduces-bipartisan-bill-to-block-trumps-russia-diesel-deal-14053466.html,2026-10-18 01:50:55
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Indian national arrested in US for false citizenship claim to vote in 2024 election,Indian national arrested in US for false citizenship claim to vote in 2024 election,"Indian national arrested in US for false citizenship claim to vote in 2024 election Chaudhari is charged with one count of false information in voting and one count of making a false claim of citizenship in order to register to vote or vote. If convicted, he faces a maximum penalty of five years in prison on each count. Chaudhari is charged with one count of false information in voting and one count of making a false claim of citizenship in order to register to vote or vote. If convicted, he faces a maximum penalty of five years in prison on each count. A non-US citizen has been arrested for allegedly falsely claiming American citizenship to vote in the 2024 US general election, the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) said on Friday. Mukeshkumar Somabhai Chaudhari was arrested by US Immigration and Customs Enforcement’s (ICE) Homeland Security Investigations (HSI), according to a DHS statement issued on October 16. A federal grand jury in Minnesota indicted Chaudhari on two counts on September 10 over the alleged false claim of citizenship. According to the indictment, on or about November 5, 2024, Chaudhari signed the voter signature certificate required by Minnesota law to vote in the general election. The certificate required him to swear or affirm that he was a citizen of the United States. The indictment alleges Chaudhari was not a US citizen and knowingly made the false citizenship statement to vote. “Our message to aliens who vote in American elections is clear: we will find you, arrest you, and you will face the consequences, including criminal charges and deportation,” said DHS Secretary Markwayne Mullin. “Under President Trump’s leadership, this administration will never allow the votes of American citizens to be cancelled out by non-citizens illegally voting in our elections. On","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:24:14 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/indian-origin-man-arrested-in-us-for-false-citizenship-claim-to-vote-in-2024-election-14053468.html,2026-10-18 01:50:56
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Indian-origin man jailed for life for murdering woman at London Airbnb over £500 dispute,Indian-origin man jailed for life for murdering woman at London Airbnb over £500 dispute,"Indian-origin man jailed for life for murdering woman at London Airbnb over £500 dispute Deepak Sandhu was sentenced to a minimum of 28 years in prison after being convicted of murdering Xiaoqing Ke and attempting to pervert the course of justice. Deepak Sandhu was sentenced to a minimum of 28 years in prison after being convicted of murdering Xiaoqing Ke and attempting to pervert the course of justice. An Indian national has been sentenced to life imprisonment in the United Kingdom for murdering a woman at her London accommodation after she confronted him over £500 he had stolen from her and ended their relationship. The man, identified as Deepak Sandhu (24), was ordered to serve at least 28 years in prison for killing 39-year-old Xiaoqing Ke in an attack that prosecutors described as a calculated act of revenge. He was also convicted of attempting to pervert the course of justice by trying to flee the country after the murder,Sky Newsreported on Friday. Sandhu was sentenced at the Old Bailey on Friday, a day after a jury found him guilty of the murder. Ke was discovered dead on the bedroom floor of her bedsit on Old Brompton Road in Earl’s Court, west London on December 10, 2025. She had suffered multiple injuries, including a fatal stab wound to the neck. The court heard that Sandhu and Ke had met in Southampton in early 2025 and developed a relationship while communicating through a translation application. Ke, who was from China, later moved to London, but the two remained in contact, according to the report. According to reports, their relationship deteriorated after Ke discovered that Sandhu had taken £500 from her handbag. Prosecutor Caroline Carberry KC told the court that Sandhu had repeatedly misled Ke about","Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:24:04 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/indian-origin-man-jailed-for-life-for-murdering-woman-at-london-airbnb-over-500-dispute-14053464.html,2026-10-18 01:50:56
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Zelenskyy slams Trump’s Russia diesel deal, says it will put more money in Putin’s pocket","Zelenskyy slams Trump’s Russia diesel deal, says it will put more money in Putin’s pocket","Zelenskyy slams Trump’s Russia diesel deal, says it will put more money in Putin’s pocket The Ukrainian president accused Putin of turning peace talks into a “parody” as he urged European allies to provide at least €70 billion in additional support for Kyiv next year The Ukrainian president accused Putin of turning peace talks into a “parody” as he urged European allies to provide at least €70 billion in additional support for Kyiv next year Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy on Friday criticised US President Donald Trump’s decision to ease restrictions on Russian diesel imports, warning that the move would channel more money into Moscow’s war effort, as he accused Russian President Vladimir Putin of showing no genuine interest in ending the conflict. Speaking on the sidelines of a European Union summit in Brussels, Zelenskyy also welcomed a greater role for European allies in negotiations, while taking aim at Moscow’s approach to peace talks. His remarks came a day after Trump called Ukraine’s renewed attacks on Russian oil refineries a “disgrace” and blamed them for driving up global energy prices. Zelenskyy appeared in no mood to soften his criticism of Washington. Asked at a press conference whether he felt Trump was using him as a scapegoat for high energy prices and the course of the Iran war, the Ukrainian leader paused for a long time, smiled and replied, ""this is my answer"", drawing laughter from the room. EU leaders rallied behind Zelenskyy at a critical juncture for Ukraine, as Russian missile and drone attacks continued to hit cities and concerns mounted over the future of US intelligence sharing and military assistance to Kyiv. On arriving at the summit, Zelenskyy pointedly referred to his European counterparts as ""real ","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 22:07:37 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/zelenskyy-slams-trumps-russia-diesel-deal-says-it-will-put-more-money-in-putins-pocket-14053460.html,2026-10-18 01:50:56
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Trump says US may target Iran’s Pickaxe Mountain nuclear site, vows ‘we’ll knock it out fast’","Trump says US may target Iran’s Pickaxe Mountain nuclear site, vows ‘we’ll knock it out fast’","Trump says US may target Iran’s Pickaxe Mountain nuclear site, vows ‘we’ll knock it out fast’ President Trump said the US may have to target Pickaxe Mountain, a site linked to Iran's nuclear program, as he aims to ensure Tehran does not get a nuclear weapon. President Trump said the US may have to target Pickaxe Mountain, a site linked to Iran's nuclear program, as he aims to ensure Tehran does not get a nuclear weapon. US President Donald Trump has said Washington may launch a strike on Iran’s underground Pickaxe Mountain facility as he seeks to prevent Tehran from acquiring nuclear weapons, while ruling out the use of a nuclear bomb in the ongoing conflict. In an interview with Newsmax on Thursday night, Trump also said he would not use a nuclear weapon in the war on Iran, which began in February with US-Israeli strikes. Trump said the US wanted to make sure Iran would never have a nuclear weapon ""because if they had it, they would use it."" ""Their nuclear is buried under millions of tons of mountain. Now we're looking at Pickaxe Mountain. We may have to go there. We'll take a shot at that one. We'll knock it out fast,"" Trump told the news outlet. Pickaxe Mountain is a heavily fortified underground facility near one of Iran’s main nuclear sites. Its location deep beneath the ground presents a challenge for any military operation aimed at damaging the infrastructure. The US president said there was no need to use a nuclear bomb in Iran. ""I wouldn't use nuclear. We don't have to."" Trump reiterated he was not considering using US ground troops in Iran The US president reiterated that he was not considering deploying American ground troops to Iran, signalling that a ground invasion was not part of the approach he described in the in","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 21:30:38 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/trump-says-us-may-target-irans-pickaxe-mountain-nuclear-site-vows-well-knock-it-out-fast-14053462.html,2026-10-18 01:50:56
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"PM Modi’s Canada visit dates under discussion, says MEA amid reports of December trip","PM Modi’s Canada visit dates under discussion, says MEA amid reports of December trip","PM Modi’s Canada visit dates under discussion, says MEA amid reports of December trip New Delhi says the dates remain undecided amid reports of a December visit, days after Modi and Mark Carney discussed strengthening bilateral ties and expediting a trade agreement. New Delhi says the dates remain undecided amid reports of a December visit, days after Modi and Mark Carney discussed strengthening bilateral ties and expediting a trade agreement. The dates of Prime Minister Narendra Modi’s visit to Canada are currently ”under discussion” between the two sides, the Ministry of External Affairs (MEA) said on Friday. MEA Spokesperson Randhir Jaiswal, during his bi-weekly media briefing, was asked to confirm reports suggesting the dates of the forthcoming visit in December. The dates are ”currently under discussion” between the two sides, he said. Prime Minister Modi had a ”warm and productive” conversation with his Canadian counterpart Mark Carney on Tuesday, with both leaders agreeing to build on the ”positive momentum” in bilateral ties. In a post on X on October 13, PM Modi had said that together, India and Canada are contributing towards a ”more stable and prosperous world.” ”Had a warm and productive conversation with Prime Minister Mark Carney. We agreed to build on the positive momentum in India-Canada ties and work towards concrete outcomes across key areas of our Strategic Partnership, including trade, energy, technology, security and people-to-people ties,” he said. A statement from Carney’s office earlier said that the two leaders reaffirmed their commitment to conclude negotiations on the Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (CEPA) at the earliest opportunity, supporting efforts to double two-way trade by 2030. Canadian Pri","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:22:10 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/india/pm-modis-canada-visit-dates-under-discussion-says-mea-amid-reports-of-december-trip-14053458.html,2026-10-18 01:50:56
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"MEA calls on US to ensure safety, security of Indian nationals after Illinois incident","MEA calls on US to ensure safety, security of Indian nationals after Illinois incident","MEA calls on US to ensure safety, security of Indian nationals after Illinois incident India on Friday flagged rising hate crimes and racial discrimination against the Indian community after a 19-year-old was arrested for allegedly planning a mass shooting at a Hindu temple in Illinois India on Friday flagged rising hate crimes and racial discrimination against the Indian community after a 19-year-old was arrested for allegedly planning a mass shooting at a Hindu temple in Illinois India on Friday expressed concern over the attempted mass shooting in Illinois, saying the incident highlighted the increasing cases of hate crimes and racial discrimination against the Indian community in the US. Ministry of External Affairs (MEA) spokesperson Randhir Jaiswal said India had taken note of the action taken by US law enforcement authorities following the incident and called on Washington to ensure the safety and well-being of international communities in the country. “This is obviously a matter of deep concern for us, and at the same time, it is something that shows or highlights the increasing cases of hate crimes and racial discrimination against the Indian community in the United States,” Jaiswal said. “We have taken note of the action that US law enforcement authorities have undertaken after this incident happened. We call on the United States side to ensure the safety, security, and well-being of internationals in the United States in the country,” he added. A 19-year-old was arrested in Palatine near Chicago for allegedly planning a mass shooting at the Sri Venkateswara Temple in Aurora, Illinois on Sunday. The plan failed as the gun carried by the teenager jammed and could not fire. He appeared in court on October 14 and was charged with one count of making ","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:59:59 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/mea-calls-on-us-to-ensure-safety-security-of-indian-nationals-after-illinois-incident-14053453.html,2026-10-18 01:50:56
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Indian-origin UK police officer charged with attempted rape, sexual assault and strangulation","Indian-origin UK police officer charged with attempted rape, sexual assault and strangulation","Indian-origin UK police officer charged with attempted rape, sexual assault and strangulation Metropolitan Police Constable Harman Singh, who was suspended after his arrest in January, is due to appear before Westminster Magistrates’ Court on Monday. Metropolitan Police Constable Harman Singh, who was suspended after his arrest in January, is due to appear before Westminster Magistrates’ Court on Monday. An Indian-origin Metropolitan Police officer in London has been charged with attempted rape, sexual assault and intentional strangulation, Scotland Yard said on Friday. Police Constable Harman Singh, who is attached to the force’s North Area command unit, faces one count of attempted rape, three counts of sexual assault and one count of intentional strangulation. He is scheduled to appear before Westminster Magistrates’ Court on Monday. The charges relate to alleged offences against a woman in her 20s in January 2026, the Metropolitan Police said. “The allegations were reported that month, and Singh was arrested on Saturday, January 10, on suspicion of one count of attempted rape and two counts of sexual assault against a woman. Singh was bailed pending further enquiries,” the Met Police said in a statement. Singh was suspended from duty following his arrest. “Following a Met investigation, the case was referred to the Crown Prosecution Service, which authorised the charges on Friday, September 4,” the force stated. The female witness in the case is being supported by officers. With inputs from agencies","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:04:14 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/indian-origin-uk-police-officer-charged-with-attempted-rape-sexual-assault-and-strangulation-14053448.html,2026-10-18 01:50:56
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Jaishankar, Rubio to hold talks in Mumbai on October 20: MEA","Jaishankar, Rubio to hold talks in Mumbai on October 20: MEA","Jaishankar, Rubio to hold talks in Mumbai on October 20: MEA EAM S Jaishankar and US Secretary of State Marco Rubio will hold talks on October 20 to discuss ways to enhance the bilateral partnership during his visit to India, the MEA said on Friday. EAM S Jaishankar and US Secretary of State Marco Rubio will hold talks on October 20 to discuss ways to enhance the bilateral partnership during his visit to India, the MEA said on Friday. External Affairs Minister S Jaishankar will meet US Secretary of State Marco Rubio in Mumbai on October 20 to discuss ways to strengthen bilateral ties, the Ministry of External Affairs (MEA) said on Friday. MEA spokesperson Randhir Jaiswal confirmed the meeting during his bi-weekly media briefing while responding to a question about Jaishankar’s engagements during his planned visit to Mumbai next week. EAM Jaishankar will host UAE Deputy Prime Minister and Foreign Minister Sheikh Abdullah bin Zayed Al Nahyan in Mumbai on October 19 and 20 for an official visit. On October 20, he will hold talks with Rubio and attend celebrations marking the 250th anniversary of US independence as a Guest of Honour, Jaiswal said. The meeting between External Affairs Minister Jaishankar and Secretary of State Rubio will allow both sides to discuss ways to bolster the Comprehensive Global Strategic Partnership, Jaiswal said. During an interactive session at the Bloomberg New Economy Forum here on Thursday, US envoy Sergio Gor said that Rubio will meet Indian government officials in Mumbai and ”we expect that minister Dr S Jaishankar will be there”. The US secretary of state will travel to India next week to further strengthen energy cooperation, the Department of State said on Tuesday. Rubio will first visit ","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:56:18 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/india/jaishankar-rubio-to-hold-talks-in-mumbai-on-october-20-mea-14053449.html,2026-10-18 01:50:56
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"India approves rollover of Rs 3,000 crore currency swap facility with Maldives","India approves rollover of Rs 3,000 crore currency swap facility with Maldives","India approves rollover of Rs 3,000 crore currency swap facility with Maldives At the request of the Maldivian government, India will roll over the Rs 3,000 crore currency swap facility from October 23, 2026 At the request of the Maldivian government, India will roll over the Rs 3,000 crore currency swap facility from October 23, 2026 India on Friday confirmed that it has decided to carry out the first rollover of its Rs 3,000 crore currency swap arrangement with Maldives from October 23 at the request of the Maldivian government. MEA spokesperson Randhir Jaiswal said the move would contribute to the financial stability of the Maldives and strengthen the two countries’ development cooperation partnership. “We have a close development cooperation partnership with the Maldives. At the request of the Government of Maldives, we have decided that we will carry out the first rollover of the INR 3000 crore currency swap with effect from 23 October 2026,” Jaiswal said. He added that the currency swap arrangement was established under an agreement signed in October 2024. ""The present rollover will add to the financial stability of the Maldives,"" he said. The facility is being extended under the Framework on Currency Swap Arrangement for SAARC countries, signed between the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) and the Maldives Monetary Authority during Maldivian President Mohamed Muizzu’s state visit to India in October 2024. The first drawdown of Rs 30 billion was extended to the Maldivian government on April 23, 2026. Under the 2024–27 framework, the bilateral currency swap arrangement provides concessions, including favourable interest rates and other conditions. Since the framework was introduce","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:46:00 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/india-approves-rollover-of-rs-3000-crore-currency-swap-facility-with-maldives-14053428.html,2026-10-18 01:50:57
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"France, Germany confirm ‘made in Europe’ deal as China eyes to flood EU with EVs","France, Germany confirm ‘made in Europe’ deal as China eyes to flood EU with EVs","France, Germany confirm ‘made in Europe’ deal as China eyes to flood EU with EVs The agreement seeks to boost European manufacturing and local supply chains as Chinese automakers expand their push into Europe including its premium segment. The agreement seeks to boost European manufacturing and local supply chains as Chinese automakers expand their push into Europe including its premium segment. France and Germany have agreed on a joint plan to strengthen Europe's automotive industry through local manufacturing requirements and more flexible carbon-emission targets as Chinese carmakers step up efforts to capture a larger share of the European market. The agreement, announced by the two countries on Friday, aims to ensure that the transition to electric vehicles supports European production, investment and jobs. However, the proposal still needs broader European Union backing to take effect. The deal comes as Chinese automakers, having expanded their presence in Europe's mass-market segment with competitively priced vehicles and advanced technology, are increasingly targeting the region's premium car market. At the Paris Motor Show this week, Chinese brands showcased electric vehicles with advanced driver-assistance systems, rapid charging, and comfort technologies, aiming to challenge established European luxury manufacturers. Under the Franco-German proposal, the EU's planned “Made in Europe” framework would link public support and procurement benefits to local manufacturing and supply-chain requirements. The plan proposes that the automotive sector's definition of “Made in Europe” cover all 27 EU member states, while allowing other European partners, including Britain, to qualify under specified conditions. T","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:41:08 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/business/france-germany-confirm-made-in-europe-deal-as-china-eyes-to-flood-eu-with-evs-14053406.html,2026-10-18 01:50:57
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Watch: Ukraine’s ‘StarGate’ drone evacuates soldiers from battlefield,Watch: Ukraine’s ‘StarGate’ drone evacuates soldiers from battlefield,"Watch: Ukraine’s ‘StarGate’ drone evacuates soldiers from battlefield Drones could soon be evacuating wounded soldiers from the front lines of the Russia-Ukraine war. The Ukrainian military is testing drones designed to extract troops trapped in intense combat, potentially cutting evacuation times by up to two hours. Drones could soon be evacuating wounded soldiers from the front lines of the Russia-Ukraine war. The Ukrainian military is testing drones designed to extract troops trapped in intense combat, potentially cutting evacuation times by up to two hours. Drones could soon be evacuating wounded soldiers from the front lines of the Russia-Ukraine war. The development of the drone began last year after a front line commander approached drone manufacturer SkyFall about creating a safer way to evacuate wounded soldiers from hard-to-reach positions on the battlefield, according to The Associated Press. The company named the platform StarGate. The Ukrainian military is currently testing the system, which is expected to cut evacuation times by up to two hours. While drone warfare and missile barrages behind the frontlines make headlines, fighting continues across multiple sectors of the front as Russian leader Vladimir Putin presses ahead with his maximalist goals — emboldened by US President Donald Trump, who has helped him financially and politically by buying Russian oil, directing his regime-change rhetoric against Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy, and restricting Starlink access in Ukraine. Even as Russia has suffered more casualties, Ukraine has also paid a steep price in the war. As Ukraine has a much smaller population, battlefield losses have a greater impact on the country, making every soldier saved through faster evacuation and treatment especially valuable. In the 'kill","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:30:06 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/ukraine-russia-war-drone-evacuations-skyfall-stargate-14053414.html,2026-10-18 01:50:57
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Trump says Zelenskyy harder to deal with than Putin, calls Ukraine’s end to energy truce ‘a disgrace’","Trump says Zelenskyy harder to deal with than Putin, calls Ukraine’s end to energy truce ‘a disgrace’","Trump says Zelenskyy harder to deal with than Putin, calls Ukraine’s end to energy truce ‘a disgrace’ Trump accused Ukraine of driving up global oil prices by resuming attacks on Russian energy facilities, as tensions between Kyiv and Washington deepen over the US-brokered energy truce Trump accused Ukraine of driving up global oil prices by resuming attacks on Russian energy facilities, as tensions between Kyiv and Washington deepen over the US-brokered energy truce US President Donald Trump on Thursday said Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy was “more difficult to deal with than Putin” as he criticised Kyiv’s decision to resume strikes on Russian energy facilities, calling the move “a disgrace” and warning it would push up global oil prices. Speaking toNewsmaxon Thursday evening, Trump said he was “not a big fan of Zelenskyy” and accused the Ukrainian leader of acting against the world’s interests by targeting Russian fuel infrastructure. “All he’s doing is making the world oil price higher. Well, I think that’s a disgrace,” Trump said. “There are a lot ‌of targets you can ‌hit, but when you shoot at diesel refineries where you make, you know, ‌diesel oil, and we need that, and the world needs that, then he’s not doing a good service for the world,” he added. Trump’s remarks came after Ukraine announced an end to its pause on strikes against Russian energy infrastructure, following Moscow’s continued attacks on Ukrainian energy facilities despite the US president’s announcement of an “energy ceasefire” between Kyiv and Moscow last Sunday. On Thursday, Ryazan regional governor Pavel Malkov said a major Ukrainian drone attack had struck the Ryazan oil refinery. The reported strike was the first on Russian energy infrastructure since Trump announced the","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 17:41:00 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/trump-says-zelenskyy-harder-to-deal-with-than-putin-calls-ukraines-end-to-energy-truce-a-disgrace-14053395.html,2026-10-18 01:50:57
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,US holds secret talks with Myanmar junta as Trump eyes rare earths: Report,US holds secret talks with Myanmar junta as Trump eyes rare earths: Report,"US holds secret talks with Myanmar junta as Trump eyes rare earths: Report American officials have reportedly held talks with Myanmar's junta in Tokyo amid President Donald Trump's interest in the country's rare earths' reserves. Myanmar is believed to have significant reserves of rare earths like dysprosium and terbium. American officials have reportedly held talks with Myanmar's junta in Tokyo amid President Donald Trump's interest in the country's rare earths' reserves. Myanmar is believed to have significant reserves of rare earths like dysprosium and terbium. American officials last week held talks with Myanmar's junta in Tokyo, according to Reuters. Sources told Reuters that US Assistant Secretary of ​State (Intelligence and Research) Michael Vance again led the Tokyo talks. It was not immediately clear who led Myanmar's delegation and what the two sides discussed. This is the second round of talks between the United States and Myanmar's junta. Last month, Vance visited the country's capital, Naypyitaw, to hold talks with Min Aung Hlaing, the junta leader. The quick follow-up shows that both the sides are keen to keep discussions going on. The meeting was focussed on securing the release of an American businessman and shutting down scam centers that have swindled billions of dollars from Americans. But President Donald Trump has also been interested in Myanmar's rare earths' reserves. Since China weaponised its control of the world's rare earths' supplies, countries have sought alternate suppliers and have looked at Myanmar as a potential source — the country has significant but poorly quantified reserves of rare earths like dysprosium and terbium. American officials have discussed access to Myanmar's large deposits of heavy rare earths, according to Reuters. But US officials have ackno","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 16:19:09 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/us-myanmar-junta-talks-scam-centres-rare-earth-reserves-14053397.html,2026-10-18 01:50:57
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,‘I want to hold a Kalashnikov and shoot Jews’: Inside the chilling letter written by Yahya Sinwar’s 10-year-old son,‘I want to hold a Kalashnikov and shoot Jews’: Inside the chilling letter written by Yahya Sinwar’s 10-year-old son,"‘I want to hold a Kalashnikov and shoot Jews’: Inside the chilling letter written by Yahya Sinwar’s 10-year-old son The Israeli Defense Forces has released a letter allegedly written by Yahya Sinwar’s 10-year-old son, Ibrahim, on the second anniversary of the former Hamas chief’s killing. Recovered by Israeli troops in Gaza in 2024, the writings reveal the boy’s longing for his father alongside his hatred for Jews The Israeli Defense Forces has released a letter allegedly written by Yahya Sinwar’s 10-year-old son, Ibrahim, on the second anniversary of the former Hamas chief’s killing. Recovered by Israeli troops in Gaza in 2024, the writings reveal the boy’s longing for his father alongside his hatred for Jews The Israel Defense Forces has released a letter allegedly written by Ibrahim Sinwar, the 10-year-old son of former Hamas chief Yahya Sinwar, expressing a desire to shoot Jews with a Kalashnikov rifle. Recovered by Israeli troops in Gaza in 2024, the letter and accompanying drawings have resurfaced two years after his father was killed. The letter, addressed to his father, combines a child’s longing for parental affection with references to weapons and violence. Ibrahim writes about missing his father, wanting to see him victorious and making toy weapons from paper and cardboard, while also expressing a wish to grow up and shoot Jews. The Israeli military published the document on Thursday, 15 October, according toThe Times of Israel. The release coincides with thesecond anniversary of Yahya Sinwar’s death on 16 October 2024. The Jerusalem Postsaid the handwritten letter was on lined paper and featured a Spider-Man sticker in its lower-left corner. The IDF did not specifically comment on the sticker. According to the English translation released by the IDF, Ibrahim begins by addressing his father affectionately before expressing his desire to embrace him and see him victorious. Th","Fri, 16 Oct 2026 15:42:46 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/explainers/i-want-to-hold-a-kalashnikov-and-shoot-jews-inside-the-chilling-letter-written-by-yahya-sinwars-10-year-old-son-14053377.html,2026-10-18 01:50:57
